// Built-in exercise bank: several exercises per Reading/Listening question type (original practice texts; names and figures invented)
window.IELTS_EXBANK = [{"id":"lb-lflow-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lflow","title":"How the repair café works","context":"A volunteer explains to new visitors how the Saltmarsh Repair Café deals with items brought in for repair.","lines":[{"speaker":"Volunteer","text":"Hello, and welcome to the Saltmarsh Repair Café. We meet here in the church hall on the first Sunday of every month, and the idea is simple: rather than throwing broken things away, you bring them to us and we try to fix them with you. Let me explain what happens from the moment you walk in."},{"speaker":"Volunteer","text":"First of all, you go to the welcome desk by the door. There, you fill in a card with your name, what the item is and what seems to be wrong with it. We used to do this on a tablet, but a lot of our older visitors found that awkward, so we've gone back to paper. The card also has a number on it, which tells you when it's your turn."},{"speaker":"Volunteer","text":"Once your number is called, your item goes to our coordinator, Hamid, who checks it for safety problems. This is especially important with anything electrical, like kettles or lamps. If there's a risk, for example a damaged cable, we won't go any further, and we'll explain why."},{"speaker":"Volunteer","text":"After that, you sit down with one of our volunteers, and the two of you repair the item together. We don't just take it away and hand it back. We want you to understand how it works, so that you might be able to do it yourself next time."},{"speaker":"Volunteer","text":"When the repair is finished, the item is tested, and the details go into our logbook. We keep it so that we can tell the council how much waste we've saved. Last year it was about nine hundred kilos."},{"speaker":"Volunteer","text":"Finally, there's no charge for any of this. However, we do ask everyone to make a donation, whatever you can afford, because that pays for the hall and for spare parts. Some people have suggested a fixed fee, but we prefer to keep it voluntary. Right, does anyone have any questions?"}],"groups":[{"type":"lflow","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Visitor completes a [1] at the welcome desk"],["Coordinator checks the item for [2] problems"],["Visitor and volunteer repair the item together"],["Item is tested and details are entered in the [3]"],["Visitor is asked to give a [4]"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"card","explanation":"You fill in a card at the welcome desk; the tablet is no longer used."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"safety","explanation":"Hamid checks it for safety problems."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"logbook","explanation":"The item is tested and the details go into our logbook."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"donation","explanation":"There is no charge or fixed fee, but everyone is asked to make a donation."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lflow-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lflow","title":"Making salt in coastal ponds","context":"Part of a history lecture on traditional coastal industries, describing how salt was produced in shallow ponds.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"In today's session I want to continue our look at coastal industries by describing how salt was produced in shallow ponds, or salt pans, along the Merrow coast, the invented region we've been using as a case study. The basic principle is evaporation, but the process was far more carefully managed than you might expect."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The first stage relied on the sea itself. Workers opened a small wooden gate and let seawater flow into the first and largest pond at high tide. You might assume they simply pumped the water in, but pumps were rarely used, as they were expensive to maintain. Timing the tide was cheaper and far more reliable."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The water then sat in this pond for several days, and here the weather did most of the work. Many students assume the sun was the main factor, and of course heat matters. But the workers' own records suggest they valued the wind even more, because a dry breeze removes moisture from the surface much faster. As the water became saltier, it was moved through a series of smaller ponds."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"In the final pond, crystals began to form on the surface. These were gathered using wooden rakes. Metal tools were avoided, because they reacted with the salt and discoloured it, which reduced its value at market."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Next, the salt was piled into heaps beside the pond and left to dry for several weeks. Only then was it ready to leave the site. Now, in earlier centuries, salt had been packed in wooden barrels, but by the period we're looking at, these had largely been replaced by sacks, which were lighter and easier to load onto carts."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"For next week, I'd like you to think about how these methods compare with modern industrial production."}],"groups":[{"type":"lflow","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Seawater is let into the largest pond at high [1]"],["Water evaporates, mainly because of the [2], and moves to smaller ponds"],["Crystals are collected with wooden [3]"],["Salt is heaped beside the pond to dry for several weeks"],["Salt is packed in [4] and taken away by cart"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"tide","explanation":"Seawater flowed into the first pond at high tide; pumps were rarely used."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"wind","explanation":"Students assume the sun, but the workers 'valued the wind even more'."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"rakes","explanation":"Crystals were gathered using wooden rakes; metal tools were avoided."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"sacks","explanation":"Barrels belong to earlier centuries; by this period they had been replaced by sacks."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lflow-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lflow","title":"Making a starch bioplastic","context":"Two chemistry students, Lena and Josh, go over the method for making a starch-based plastic before their lab session.","lines":[{"speaker":"Josh","text":"Lena, can we run through the method before tomorrow's lab? I don't want to get anything wrong in front of Dr Okafor."},{"speaker":"Lena","text":"Sure. So the first thing is to put the potato starch into the beaker with the water. And then we add vinegar."},{"speaker":"Josh","text":"Vinegar? I thought it was lemon juice."},{"speaker":"Lena","text":"That was in the original recipe we found online, but the lab sheet says vinegar, because it gives more consistent results. The acid helps break down the starch."},{"speaker":"Josh","text":"Okay. And then we heat it?"},{"speaker":"Lena","text":"Yes, on a low heat, stirring all the time. At first it looks white and milky, but you keep going until it turns clear. That's the sign it's ready. If you stop too early, the plastic cracks when it dries."},{"speaker":"Josh","text":"Got it. And the glycerol goes in after that, right? How much was it?"},{"speaker":"Lena","text":"Five millilitres. Without it, the plastic is really brittle. With it, it bends without snapping."},{"speaker":"Josh","text":"Then we pour it out. Do we use those plastic trays by the sink?"},{"speaker":"Lena","text":"No, it sticks to those. We need to line a tray with foil first. Dr Okafor said baking paper works too, but the department's run out of it."},{"speaker":"Josh","text":"Foil, then. And after that we just wait?"},{"speaker":"Lena","text":"We spread it evenly, about three millimetres thick, and leave it to dry for two days. It needs to be somewhere warm. Last year someone left theirs by an open window and it never set properly."},{"speaker":"Josh","text":"So not by the window. What about the shelf above the radiator?"},{"speaker":"Lena","text":"Perfect. Then on Friday we test how far each sample stretches before it breaks."}],"groups":[{"type":"lflow","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Mix potato starch with water and [1]"],["Heat gently, stirring, until the mixture becomes [2]"],["Add glycerol so the plastic can bend"],["Pour into a tray lined with [3]"],["Leave to dry for two days in a [4] place"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"vinegar","explanation":"Lemon juice was in the online recipe, but the lab sheet says vinegar."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"clear","explanation":"It looks white and milky at first; keep going until it turns clear."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"foil","explanation":"The plastic trays are rejected and baking paper has run out, so they line a tray with foil."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"warm","explanation":"It needs to be somewhere warm, not by an open window."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lform-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lform","title":"Joining an evening pottery course","context":"Section 1: a woman phones a pottery studio to book a place on an evening course.","lines":[{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Good afternoon, Kiln Street Pottery Studio, Mara speaking. How can I help?"},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"Hello. I saw your advert for the evening pottery course, and I’d like to sign up, if there are still places."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"There are, yes. I’ll just take a few details. Could I have your surname?"},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"It’s Fenwick. That’s F, E, N, W, I, C, K."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Thank you, Mrs Fenwick. And your address?"},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"Forty-two Mill Lane. Oh, sorry, what am I saying? That’s my old flat. I moved last month. It’s forty-seven Mill Lane now, in Carrow Vale."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Forty-seven Mill Lane, Carrow Vale. Got it. Now, the course runs on two evenings. Monday is the more popular one, I’m afraid, and it’s full until the summer. But we still have a few spaces on Wednesdays."},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"Wednesday is better for me anyway. I often work late on Mondays."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Perfect. Have you done any pottery before?"},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"Only a little at school, and that was years ago. I’d call myself a complete beginner."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"That’s fine, most people are. Is there anything in particular you’d like to make? The tutor likes to know, so she can plan the first few sessions."},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"Well, I thought about plates, but my sister says they’re really hard to get flat on the wheel. What I’d love is a set of bowls for my kitchen."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Bowls are a lovely first project. I’ll put that down. And just for our records, how did you hear about us? Was it through our website?"},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"No, I haven’t even looked at the website. A neighbour did mention you once, but actually it was a poster that caught my eye, in the window of the bakery on the high street."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"A poster, thank you. That’s useful to know. So the course costs ninety pounds for ten weeks, and that includes all your clay and the firing. Just bring an apron, and keep your nails short if you can!"},{"speaker":"Helen","text":"I’ll remember that. Thanks very much."}],"groups":[{"type":"lform","instructions":"Complete the form below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"form","rows":[["Surname:","[1]"],["Address:","[2] Mill Lane, Carrow Vale"],["Class evening:","[3]"],["Would like to make:","a set of [4]"],["Heard about studio from:","a [5] in a bakery window"],["Fee:","£90 for 10 weeks"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"Fenwick","explanation":"She spells her surname F-E-N-W-I-C-K."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"47","alt":["forty-seven","forty seven"],"explanation":"Forty-two was her old address; she corrects it to forty-seven Mill Lane."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"Wednesday","alt":["Wednesdays"],"explanation":"Monday is full until the summer; there are spaces on Wednesdays and she says Wednesday is better."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"bowls","explanation":"She thought about plates but rejects them: what she’d love is a set of bowls."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"poster","explanation":"Not the website, and the neighbour is not the main source: ’it was a poster that caught my eye, in the window of the bakery’."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lform-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lform","title":"Reporting a lost bag","context":"Section 1: a student phones the lost property office of a city tram company.","lines":[{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Northgate Trams lost property, Gareth speaking."},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"Hi. I left my bag on one of your trams, and I’m hoping someone has handed it in."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Let’s see if we can find it. I’ll fill in a report as we go. What’s your name, please?"},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"Ana Quaile. The surname is Q, U, A, I, L, E."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Q, U, A, I, L, E. Thanks. And when did you lose it?"},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"It was Tuesday the ninth of October. Oh, hang on, no. I had a dentist’s appointment on the ninth, and I drove there. So it must have been the next day, Wednesday the tenth."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"The tenth of October. And do you remember where you got on and off?"},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"I got on at the Station Road stop, near the college, and I got off at Market Square. I think I left the bag on the seat when I stood up."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Market Square. Okay. Can you describe the bag?"},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"It’s a backpack, a fairly small one."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Black? Most of the ones we get are black."},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"No, mine’s red. Bright red, with a white zip."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"That should make it easier. What was inside?"},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"The thing I really need is a notebook. It’s got all my lecture notes for the term, so I’m a bit desperate. There’s also a scarf. My purse wasn’t in it, luckily. I’d put that in my coat pocket."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Right. Is there anything with your name on it?"},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"Yes, my name is written inside the front cover of the notebook."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"That helps. Bags are usually sent here from the depot within two days, so it may not have arrived yet. Can I take a phone number?"},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"Sure, it’s oh seven nine double four, six one three, five two eight."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Thanks. If it comes in, we’ll keep it for thirty days, and you’ll need to bring some photo identification to collect it."},{"speaker":"Ana","text":"Great. Thank you so much."}],"groups":[{"type":"lform","instructions":"Complete the form below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"form","rows":[["Name:","Ana [1]"],["Date of loss:","[2] October"],["Got off at:","[3] Square"],["Description:","small [4] backpack, white zip"],["Most important item inside:","[5] (name inside front cover)"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"Quaile","explanation":"She spells her surname Q-U-A-I-L-E."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"10","alt":["10th","tenth","the tenth"],"explanation":"She first says the ninth, then corrects it: she was at the dentist on the ninth, so it was the tenth."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"Market","explanation":"She got on at Station Road and got off at Market Square."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"red","explanation":"The officer suggests black, but she says ’No, mine’s red’."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"notebook","explanation":"’The thing I really need is a notebook’; the scarf is less important and the purse was not in the bag."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lform-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lform","title":"Booking a holiday science camp","context":"Section 1: a father phones a science museum to book his son onto a summer camp.","lines":[{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Hello, Lowfield Science Museum, bookings desk. How can I help?"},{"speaker":"Father","text":"Hi, I’d like to book my son onto the summer science camp, please."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Certainly. What’s your son’s name?"},{"speaker":"Father","text":"Leo Marchetti. That’s M, A, R, C, H, E, T, T, I."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Lovely. And how old is Leo? The camp is for children aged seven to twelve."},{"speaker":"Father","text":"He’s eight. Oh, wait, no, sorry, he had his birthday in May. He’s nine now. I keep forgetting!"},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Nine, no problem. We run four activity groups: robots, rockets, fossils and kitchen chemistry. Which would he like?"},{"speaker":"Father","text":"He’s mad about robots, but he did the robots group last year, so this time he’s asked for rockets."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Rockets it is. Now, does Leo have any allergies we should know about? We provide a snack each morning."},{"speaker":"Father","text":"People always ask about nuts, but he’s fine with those. It’s eggs he can’t eat. He gets quite ill."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"I’ll write eggs on the form in capital letters, and the kitchen will be told. And who’ll be collecting him at the end of each day? We only release children to adults named on the form."},{"speaker":"Father","text":"I’ll drop him off in the mornings on my way to work, but his grandfather will pick him up. My wife might come on the Friday, but that’s not certain yet, so just put his grandfather for now."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Fine. The camp runs from half past nine until three, Monday to Friday, and the cost is one hundred and twenty pounds for the week. He’ll need a packed lunch and clothes that can get messy."},{"speaker":"Father","text":"Sounds perfect. Can I pay by card?"},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Of course. I’ll take the details now."}],"groups":[{"type":"lform","instructions":"Complete the form below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"form","rows":[["Child’s name:","Leo [1]"],["Age:","[2]"],["Activity group:","[3]"],["Allergy:","[4]"],["Collected by:","his [5]"],["Fee:","£120 for the week"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"Marchetti","explanation":"The father spells it M-A-R-C-H-E-T-T-I."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"9","alt":["nine"],"explanation":"He says eight, then corrects himself: Leo had his birthday in May and is nine now."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"rockets","alt":["rocket"],"explanation":"He loves robots but did that group last year, so he has asked for rockets."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"eggs","alt":["egg"],"explanation":"He is fine with nuts; ’It’s eggs he can’t eat’."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"grandfather","explanation":"The father drops him off; his grandfather picks him up. The mother on Friday is not certain."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmap-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmap","title":"A tour of the new library","context":"Section 2: a librarian shows visitors round the ground floor of a newly refurbished public library.","lines":[{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"Good morning, and welcome to the newly refurbished Fernhill Library. We’re standing just inside the main entrance, which is at the bottom of the plan you’ve been given. I’ll take you round the ground floor so you know where everything is."},{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"Straight ahead of you, right in the middle of the floor, is the help desk. That’s where you can join, renew books or ask any questions."},{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"Before you go any further, you might want to leave your coats and bags. The lockers are immediately on your left as you come in through the doors. They take a one-pound coin, which you get back afterwards."},{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"If you walk past the lockers and keep going straight, you’ll come to the children’s area. It’s against the left-hand wall, directly to the left of the help desk. We put it there because it’s close to the entrance, so parents with buggies don’t have to go far."},{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"Behind the help desk, against the back wall in the middle, you’ll find the computer zone. There are twenty machines, and you can book them for up to two hours at a time."},{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"A lot of our regular visitors are asking about the café. It used to be on your right as you came in, next to the entrance, but it was rather noisy there, so we’ve moved it to the top right-hand corner of the building, the far corner on that side. The old café space beside the entrance is now the newspaper and magazine area."},{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"And finally, the study rooms, which you can book for group work. They’re in the top left-hand corner, beyond the children’s area, so they’re the furthest point from the doors on the left. The walls are soundproofed, so you won’t hear the children at all."},{"speaker":"Librarian","text":"The area to the right of the help desk is still being fitted out. It’ll be an exhibition space by the spring. Right, are there any questions before we go upstairs?"}],"groups":[{"type":"lmap","instructions":"Label the plan. Choose the correct letter, A–G. You are at the entrance at the bottom of the plan.","map":{"cols":3,"rows":3,"cells":[{"r":0,"c":0,"t":"A"},{"r":0,"c":1,"t":"B"},{"r":0,"c":2,"t":"C"},{"r":1,"c":0,"t":"D"},{"r":1,"c":1,"t":"Help desk"},{"r":1,"c":2,"t":"E"},{"r":2,"c":0,"t":"F"},{"r":2,"c":1,"t":"Entrance"},{"r":2,"c":2,"t":"G"}]},"options":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],"questions":[{"q":"Lockers","answer":"F","explanation":"Immediately on your left as you come in through the doors."},{"q":"Children’s area","answer":"D","explanation":"Against the left-hand wall, directly to the left of the help desk."},{"q":"Computer zone","answer":"B","explanation":"Behind the help desk, against the back wall in the middle."},{"q":"Café","answer":"C","explanation":"It used to be on the right of the entrance (G, now newspapers) but moved to the top right-hand corner."},{"q":"Study rooms","answer":"A","explanation":"Top left-hand corner, beyond the children’s area. E is the future exhibition space."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmap-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmap","title":"Volunteering at Brookside City Farm","context":"Section 2: a volunteer coordinator briefs new helpers at the gate of a city farm.","lines":[{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"Hello, everyone, and thanks for coming along. We’re standing at the farm gate, which you’ll see at the bottom of the map I’ve handed out. Let me quickly explain the layout, because in your first few days you’ll be sent all over the place."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"The large building straight ahead of you, at the far end of the main path, is the barn. That’s where we store the animal feed, and it’s where you’ll sign in every morning."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"The first thing visitors see is the farm shop. It’s just to your right as you come through the gate. We sell eggs, honey and vegetables from the farm, and some of you will be working on the till."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"Now, the animals. The goats are halfway up the path, on the left. No, sorry, I always get this wrong. Let me check. Yes, the goats are on the right, halfway between the gate and the barn. The pigs are opposite them, on the left-hand side. Please don’t feed the goats, however much they beg."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"In the very centre of the farm, between the gate and the barn, with the pigs on one side and the goats on the other, are the vegetable beds. That’s where most of the school groups spend their time."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"The beehives are kept well away from the busiest paths. You’ll find them in the top left-hand corner, to the left of the barn. Only trained staff go in there, so if a child wanders over, just bring them back."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"In the top right-hand corner, on the other side of the barn, there’s a small orchard of apple trees."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"And lastly, the picnic area. People often expect it to be next to the shop, but it’s actually in the opposite corner, the bottom left, so it’s on your left as you come in. There are tables and a shelter in case it rains. Right, let’s walk up to the barn and get you signed in."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmap","instructions":"Label the map. Choose the correct letter, A–G. You are at the farm gate at the bottom of the map.","map":{"cols":3,"rows":3,"cells":[{"r":0,"c":0,"t":"A"},{"r":0,"c":1,"t":"Barn"},{"r":0,"c":2,"t":"B"},{"r":1,"c":0,"t":"C"},{"r":1,"c":1,"t":"D"},{"r":1,"c":2,"t":"E"},{"r":2,"c":0,"t":"F"},{"r":2,"c":1,"t":"Farm gate"},{"r":2,"c":2,"t":"G"}]},"options":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],"questions":[{"q":"Farm shop","answer":"G","explanation":"Just to your right as you come through the gate."},{"q":"Goats","answer":"E","explanation":"She first says left, then corrects herself: on the right, halfway between the gate and the barn. The pigs (C) are opposite."},{"q":"Vegetable beds","answer":"D","explanation":"In the very centre, between the gate and the barn, with pigs and goats on either side."},{"q":"Beehives","answer":"A","explanation":"Top left-hand corner, to the left of the barn. B is the orchard."},{"q":"Picnic area","answer":"F","explanation":"Not next to the shop; it is in the opposite corner, bottom left, on your left as you come in."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmap-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmap","title":"Inside Stanmore Market Hall","context":"Section 2: a market manager speaks to new stallholders at the main doors of an indoor market.","lines":[{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Good morning, and welcome to Stanmore Market Hall. Some of you start trading next week, so I want you to know your way around. We’re standing at the main doors, which are in the middle of the bottom wall on your plan."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"First, the information point. That’s the round desk right in the centre of the hall, straight ahead of you. If a customer is lost, or a child gets separated from their parents, send them there."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Now, the flower stall. On older plans you’ll see it to the left of the doors as you come in, but that space is the cheese counter now. The flowers moved to the right-hand side of the doors last spring, so they’re the first thing customers see on that side."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"The fish stalls need running water and good drainage, so they’re in the top right-hand corner, against the back wall, as far from the doors as you can get on that side."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Directly opposite the fish, in the top left-hand corner, is the bakery. The ovens are just behind the wall there, and the smell of fresh bread in the morning brings a lot of people in."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Between the bakery and the fish, in the middle of the back wall, are the toilets and the baby-changing room."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Our seating area is on the left-hand side of the hall, halfway along, between the bakery and the cheese counter, and right next to the information point. It’s a good place to eat, and it gets very busy at lunchtime."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"And on the other side of the information point, on the right, are the two butchers. Right, let’s walk round, and I’ll introduce you to some of the traders."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmap","instructions":"Label the plan. Choose the correct letter, A–H. You are at the main doors at the bottom of the plan.","map":{"cols":3,"rows":3,"cells":[{"r":0,"c":0,"t":"A"},{"r":0,"c":1,"t":"B"},{"r":0,"c":2,"t":"C"},{"r":1,"c":0,"t":"D"},{"r":1,"c":1,"t":"E"},{"r":1,"c":2,"t":"F"},{"r":2,"c":0,"t":"G"},{"r":2,"c":1,"t":"Main doors"},{"r":2,"c":2,"t":"H"}]},"options":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H"],"questions":[{"q":"Information point","answer":"E","explanation":"The round desk right in the centre of the hall, straight ahead."},{"q":"Flower stall","answer":"H","explanation":"Old plans show it on the left (G, now cheese); it moved to the right-hand side of the doors."},{"q":"Fish stalls","answer":"C","explanation":"Top right-hand corner, against the back wall."},{"q":"Bakery","answer":"A","explanation":"Directly opposite the fish, in the top left-hand corner. B is the toilets."},{"q":"Seating area","answer":"D","explanation":"Left-hand side, halfway along, between the bakery and the cheese counter, next to the information point. F is the butchers."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmatch-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmatch","title":"Planning a family weekend","context":"Section 1: a brother and sister, Rob and Kate, plan activities for their grandparents’ weekend visit.","lines":[{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Rob, Gran and Grandad arrive on Friday afternoon, so we need to sort out the weekend. Have you got a minute?"},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"Sure. What have we got so far?"},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Well, Gran really wants to see the botanical gardens. I thought Saturday, because they’re open later that day."},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"The forecast for Saturday is heavy rain, though. Sunday’s meant to be sunny."},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Oh, then Sunday for the gardens, definitely."},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"What about the theatre? There’s a comedy on at the Playhouse."},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"There are only tickets left for the Friday evening show and the Saturday afternoon one. On Friday night they’ll be tired after the train."},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"Saturday afternoon, then. Gran loves an afternoon show anyway."},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Okay, I’ll book that. Then there’s the boat trip on the river. Grandad’s been talking about it for months."},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"If it’s raining on Saturday, a boat isn’t ideal. Let’s do it on Sunday morning, before the gardens."},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Hmm, wait. I’ve just checked, and the boats stop running at the end of September. The last sailing is this Friday at five, and their train gets in at three."},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"Then it’ll have to be Friday. We can go straight there from the station."},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Good. And what about dinner at Luca’s? I’d like the whole family together for that."},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"Mum can’t get here until Saturday, so let’s make it Saturday evening, after the theatre."},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Perfect. Oh, and Grandad wants to visit the model railway museum."},{"speaker":"Rob","text":"That’s closed on Sundays. Could we squeeze it in on Saturday morning?"},{"speaker":"Kate","text":"Yes, it’s indoors, so the rain won’t matter. That’s everything, then."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmatch","instructions":"On which day will the family do each activity? Choose the correct letter, A, B or C. You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Friday","B Saturday","C Sunday"],"questions":[{"q":"botanical gardens","answer":"C","explanation":"Kate suggests Saturday, but rain is forecast, so ’Sunday for the gardens’."},{"q":"theatre trip","answer":"B","explanation":"Friday night they’ll be tired, so the Saturday afternoon show."},{"q":"boat trip","answer":"A","explanation":"Rob suggests Sunday morning, but the last sailing is this Friday at five, so ’it’ll have to be Friday’."},{"q":"family dinner","answer":"B","explanation":"Mum arrives on Saturday: ’Saturday evening, after the theatre’."},{"q":"model railway museum","answer":"B","explanation":"Closed on Sundays; they fit it in on Saturday morning."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmatch-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmatch","title":"Inside the new Ashgrove Sports Centre","context":"Section 2: a sports centre manager describes the facilities of a new building to local residents at an open evening.","lines":[{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Good evening, and thank you for coming to our open evening. Let me give you a quick guide to where everything is in the new building, which has three floors: the ground floor, the first floor and the second floor."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Let’s start with the climbing wall, which a lot of you have asked about. In the original plans it was going to be on the second floor, but the engineers found that the ceiling there wasn’t high enough. So in the end it was built on the ground floor, at the back of the main hall, where it can rise the full height of the building."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"The gym is on the first floor. It’s twice the size of the old one, with a separate area for free weights. The changing rooms, by the way, are on the ground floor, so you’ll need to take the stairs or the lift."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"The dance studio is right at the top, on the second floor. It has a sprung wooden floor and mirrors along one wall, and the views over the park are wonderful."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Many people expected the café to be beside reception on the ground floor, as it was in the old centre. We did consider that, but we decided to put it on the first floor instead, with a glass wall overlooking the climbing wall, so parents can watch their children while they have a coffee."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Finally, the physiotherapy clinic. It opens next month, and you’ll find it on the second floor, just along the corridor from the dance studio, where it’s nice and quiet."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Now, if you’d like to follow me, we’ll begin the tour on the ground floor."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmatch","instructions":"On which floor is each facility? Choose the correct letter, A, B or C. You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A ground floor","B first floor","C second floor"],"questions":[{"q":"climbing wall","answer":"A","explanation":"Planned for the second floor, but the ceiling was too low, so it was built on the ground floor."},{"q":"gym","answer":"B","explanation":"’The gym is on the first floor’; the changing rooms (ground floor) are a distractor."},{"q":"dance studio","answer":"C","explanation":"’right at the top, on the second floor’."},{"q":"café","answer":"B","explanation":"Expected beside reception on the ground floor, but put on the first floor instead."},{"q":"physiotherapy clinic","answer":"C","explanation":"On the second floor, along the corridor from the dance studio."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmatch-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmatch","title":"Evaluating sources for a history project","context":"Section 3: two history students, Leah and Sam, decide which sources to use for an assignment on a local canal.","lines":[{"speaker":"Sam","text":"Leah, shall we go through the sources for the Garrow Canal project? We need to decide which ones to use for the section on the workers."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Good idea. Let’s start with the canal museum’s website."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"It’s well written, and it gives a clear timeline of when each section of the canal was built. But it says almost nothing about the workers, which is our focus."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"So it’s fine for background, but not much more."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"Exactly. What about the diary of the chief engineer, Thomas Hale? I found a typed-up copy in the library."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"I thought that would be really valuable, but apparently the handwriting in the original is so bad that the person who typed it up had to guess at half the words. I’m not sure we can trust the details."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"Then I think we have to treat it as unreliable."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Agreed. Next, the canal company’s account books."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"They show exactly what the workers were paid, year by year. They’re huge, but they’re exactly what we need."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Definitely. And then there’s the article by Professor Ingram."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"I started reading it, and honestly, it’s full of economic theory. I found it far too difficult to follow."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Let me have a look before we give up on it. Actually, the second half is all about the workers’ wages, and it’s quite clear. It explains the figures in the account books."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"Oh, in that case it’s really useful after all. I’ll read the second half properly."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"Finally, the old tourist guidebook from the local history society."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"It’s charming, but it repeats stories that have been shown to be false, like the one about the tunnel collapse."},{"speaker":"Leah","text":"So we can’t rely on it for anything."},{"speaker":"Sam","text":"No. I’ll leave it out."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmatch","instructions":"What do the students decide about each source? Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D. You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A very useful","B useful for background only","C unreliable","D too difficult to use"],"questions":[{"q":"the canal museum’s website","answer":"B","explanation":"A clear timeline but almost nothing on the workers: ’fine for background, but not much more’."},{"q":"the chief engineer’s diary","answer":"C","explanation":"The transcriber guessed at half the words: ’we have to treat it as unreliable’."},{"q":"the company’s account books","answer":"A","explanation":"They show exactly what workers were paid: ’exactly what we need’."},{"q":"Professor Ingram’s article","answer":"A","explanation":"Sam first finds it too difficult, but changes his mind: the second half is clear, so ’it’s really useful after all’."},{"q":"the tourist guidebook","answer":"C","explanation":"It repeats stories shown to be false, so ’we can’t rely on it’."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmcq-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmcq","title":"Hiring a bicycle","context":"Section 1: a tourist talks to an assistant in a bicycle hire shop in a small town.","lines":[{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Morning! Are you looking to hire a bike?"},{"speaker":"Jade","text":"Yes, please. I’m staying in town for a few days, and I’d like to cycle out to the lakes."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Good choice. We’ve got road bikes, mountain bikes and electric bikes. If you’re sticking to the roads, a road bike is the cheapest."},{"speaker":"Jade","text":"I was going to go for a road bike, but I’ve heard the route to the lakes is quite hilly."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"It is. There’s one climb that’s about three kilometres long. A mountain bike will cope with the gravel tracks, but it won’t make the hills any easier."},{"speaker":"Jade","text":"In that case, I think I’ll take an electric one. I’m on holiday, after all. I don’t want to arrive exhausted."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Sensible. Now, how long would you like it for? We do half days, full days, or two days at a discount."},{"speaker":"Jade","text":"I did think about two days, but the forecast for tomorrow is terrible, so let’s just say one full day."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"One day, so that’s thirty-five pounds. The helmet comes free with every hire, and I’ll give you a map of the cycle routes as well, at no charge. The only extra is a lock, which is three pounds, but honestly, you’ll want one at the lakes."},{"speaker":"Jade","text":"Fine, I’ll take the lock too. Is there anywhere good to stop for lunch?"},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Most people head for the castle tearoom, but it gets packed with coach parties at lunchtime. There’s a pub by the river at Elmbridge, though the food is a bit pricey. My favourite is the farm café at Holt End, just before the last lake. Their soup is fantastic, and it’s never too busy."},{"speaker":"Jade","text":"The farm café it is, then. Do you need anything from me?"},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Just some identification. A passport or a driving licence is fine. I’ll also need a card to take a deposit."},{"speaker":"Jade","text":"I left my passport at the hotel, but I’ve got my driving licence here."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"That’s perfect. I’ll just adjust the seat for you."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"q":"Which type of bike does Jade hire?","options":["A an electric bike","B a road bike","C a mountain bike"],"answer":"A","explanation":"She planned a road bike, but because of the hills she says ’I’ll take an electric one’."},{"q":"How long does she hire the bike for?","options":["A half a day","B one day","C two days"],"answer":"B","explanation":"She thought about two days, but the forecast is bad, so ’one full day’."},{"q":"What does she have to pay extra for?","options":["A a lock","B a helmet","C a map"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The helmet and map are free; ’The only extra is a lock’."},{"q":"Where does the assistant recommend having lunch?","options":["A the castle tearoom","B a pub by the river","C a farm café"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The tearoom is crowded and the pub pricey; ’My favourite is the farm café at Holt End’."},{"q":"Which document does Jade show?","options":["A a passport","B a driving licence","C a bank card"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Her passport is at the hotel; she has her driving licence. The card is for the deposit, not identification."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmcq-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmcq","title":"Discussing essay feedback","context":"Section 3: two geography students, Ella and Marcus, discuss their tutor’s feedback on a joint report about river flooding.","lines":[{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"So, Ella, did you read Doctor Okafor’s comments on our flooding report?"},{"speaker":"Ella","text":"I did. To be honest, I was expecting her to criticise the maps, because we rushed them. But she didn’t mention them at all."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"No, and she said the writing style was fine, if a bit informal in places. What she really liked was the case study of Hollinsby. She said our interviews with residents gave it real depth."},{"speaker":"Ella","text":"That’s good to hear, because those interviews took ages. So what do we need to change?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Her main point was about the introduction. She thinks it’s too long, and that we spend half a page on the history of the river before we get to the question."},{"speaker":"Ella","text":"I’d actually rather fix the conclusion first. It doesn’t really answer the question either."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"True, but she said the conclusion was acceptable. The introduction is the part she wants rewritten, so let’s start there."},{"speaker":"Ella","text":"Fine. Did she say anything about our sources?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Yes. She’s happy with the government report and the journal articles, but she questioned the newspaper piece about the big flood. She says it exaggerates the damage."},{"speaker":"Ella","text":"Then we should drop it. We could use the council’s own records instead. They cover exactly the same flood."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Agreed. Now, she wants to see us before we resubmit. She offered Monday morning or Thursday afternoon."},{"speaker":"Ella","text":"I’ve got a lab on Monday morning. What about Thursday?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"I’ve got football training on Thursday afternoon, but I suppose I could miss it once. Oh, wait, she also said Friday at ten was possible, if neither of the others worked."},{"speaker":"Ella","text":"Friday at ten would suit both of us, then. Shall I email her to confirm?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Yes, please. And let’s aim to finish the new introduction by Wednesday."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"q":"What did the tutor praise most in the report?","options":["A the maps","B the writing style","C the case study"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The maps were not mentioned and the style was only ’fine’; ’What she really liked was the case study’."},{"q":"Which part of the report will the students rewrite first?","options":["A the introduction","B the conclusion","C the section on the river’s history"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Ella wants the conclusion first, but Marcus says the introduction is the part the tutor wants rewritten: ’let’s start there’."},{"q":"What will they do about the newspaper article?","options":["A keep it and comment on its accuracy","B replace it with council records","C replace it with a journal article"],"answer":"B","explanation":"’We could use the council’s own records instead’; Marcus agrees."},{"q":"When will they meet the tutor?","options":["A on Monday morning","B on Thursday afternoon","C on Friday morning"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Monday clashes with a lab and Thursday with football; they choose Friday at ten."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmcq-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmcq","title":"Whistled languages","context":"Section 4: part of a linguistics lecture on whistled forms of speech.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Today I want to introduce one of the more unusual forms of human communication: whistled speech. In a number of mountain communities, people can hold whole conversations by whistling. It’s important to understand that these are not separate languages. A whistled language is a way of transmitting an ordinary spoken language, with the whistle copying the melody and rhythm of speech."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Why would people whistle rather than shout? Some writers have suggested that it was used to keep messages secret from outsiders, but there’s little evidence for that. Others link it to herding animals. The explanation most researchers accept, though, is geography. People in these areas live and work on opposite sides of deep valleys. A whistle carries much further than a shout and is less affected by echo, so it was simply the most practical way to talk over long distances."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"I’d like to describe some research by Doctor Hana Lindqvist in the mountain village of Asterra. She recorded whistled conversations and played them to two groups: local whistlers, and people who spoke the same language but had never learned to whistle it. Unsurprisingly, the whistlers understood almost everything. More interestingly, the non-whistlers could still recognise many individual words, even though they couldn’t follow whole sentences. Lindqvist argues that this shows how much information is carried by the melody of speech alone."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Sadly, whistled speech is declining in most places. New roads now reach most villages, so people rarely need to call across valleys. But the main cause, according to the people Lindqvist interviewed, is the mobile phone. Why whistle when you can simply ring?"},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Some communities are fighting back. In Asterra, festivals and shows for tourists were tried, but interest didn’t last. Instead, whistling has now been made part of the primary school timetable, so children learn it alongside reading and writing. I think that’s the approach most likely to keep the tradition alive."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecturer, a whistled language is","options":["A a separate language with its own grammar.","B a way of transmitting a spoken language.","C a code used mainly by herders."],"answer":"B","explanation":"’these are not separate languages ... a way of transmitting an ordinary spoken language’."},{"q":"Most researchers believe whistled speech developed because people","options":["A wanted to keep information private.","B needed to control their animals.","C had to communicate over long distances."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Secrecy has little evidence and herding is another theory; most accept geography: ’the most practical way to talk over long distances’."},{"q":"In Lindqvist’s study, listeners who could not whistle","options":["A understood almost everything.","B recognised some individual words.","C could follow whole sentences."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Only the whistlers understood almost everything; non-whistlers ’could still recognise many individual words’."},{"q":"According to Lindqvist’s interviewees, the main reason for the decline is","options":["A the building of new roads.","B the use of mobile phones.","C young people moving to cities."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Roads are mentioned, ’But the main cause ... is the mobile phone’."},{"q":"In Asterra, the tradition is now being kept alive through","options":["A teaching in primary schools.","B annual festivals.","C performances for tourists."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Festivals and tourist shows were tried but did not last; it is now part of the primary school timetable."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmcq2-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmcq2","title":"Volunteering at a community radio station","context":"Section 2: the manager of a local community radio station talks to people interested in volunteering.","lines":[{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Good evening, everyone, and thank you for coming along to Riverside Community Radio. I’m Priya, the station manager, and I’m going to tell you a little about what our volunteers do and what you get out of it."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"First, I should say what you won’t be doing, at least to begin with. A lot of people come here hoping to present their own show straight away. That does happen eventually, but new volunteers don’t go on air for at least six months. We also don’t ask volunteers to sell advertising. We have a paid member of staff for that."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"So what will you do? Most of our new volunteers start in the newsroom, where you’ll help to research local stories: phoning the council, checking facts and so on. The other main job is editing audio. Our interviewers record hours of material every week, and it all has to be cut down into short clips for the programmes. Don’t worry if you’ve never done it before. We’ll train you on the software."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Some people ask about answering the phones during our phone-in shows, but that’s handled by an automated system now, so we don’t need help there any more."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"Now, the benefits. Obviously, you won’t be paid, and I’m afraid we can’t cover travel costs either, although we’d love to one day. But you will receive a certificate at the end of your first year, which is recognised by several media colleges, so it’s really useful if you’re thinking of applying to one."},{"speaker":"Manager","text":"And the thing past volunteers mention most is the chance to meet people from all over the town, people of every age and background that you’d never normally come across. We also put on a free meal at Christmas, though that’s a small thing compared with the others. Right, let me hand out the application forms."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq2","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO tasks will new volunteers do?","options":["A presenting programmes","B researching news stories","C selling advertising","D editing recordings","E answering phone calls"],"answer":["B","D"],"explanation":"New volunteers research local stories in the newsroom and edit audio. They do not go on air for six months, advertising is sold by paid staff, and phones are automated."},{"q":"Which TWO benefits of volunteering does the manager emphasise?","options":["A help with travel costs","B a certificate valued by colleges","C meeting a wide range of people","D free meals every week","E the offer of a paid job"],"answer":["B","C"],"explanation":"The certificate is recognised by media colleges, and volunteers most mention meeting people from all over the town. Travel is not covered and the meal is only at Christmas."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmcq2-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmcq2","title":"Reviewing a wetland field trip","context":"Section 3: two environmental science students, Nadia and Owen, discuss their field trip and plan their report.","lines":[{"speaker":"Owen","text":"So, Nadia, how do you think the trip to Fallow Marsh went?"},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"Mostly well, I think. Though the minibus breaking down on the way there wasn’t a great start. We lost almost an hour waiting for the replacement."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"That’s true. But the weather was much better than forecast, so we still had plenty of time on site. The real problem for me was the equipment. Two of the water-testing kits had out-of-date chemicals, so half our readings were useless."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"Yes, that was frustrating. I also thought we’d be rushed at the bird hide."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"We actually stayed there longer than planned, because the guide let us stay on. She was brilliant, wasn’t she? She knew every plant on the marsh."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"She was. So, for the report, what should we include? I’d definitely like to put in the plant survey. We collected really good data on the reed beds."},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"Definitely. I’d like to include the water quality results too, but with half of them missing, I’m not sure they’re worth reporting."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"I think we should leave them out of the main sections and just mention the problem as a limitation. What about photos?"},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"Our tutor said not to fill the report with photos, maybe one or two at most. But the interview with the warden about how the marsh is managed would be useful. She gave us lots of detail about the flooding schedule."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"Agreed, let’s include that. And the bird counts?"},{"speaker":"Owen","text":"They’re too thin, really. Let’s just put them in the appendix."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"Okay. So the plant survey and the warden interview will be the main sections. I’ll start drafting the plant part tonight."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq2","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO problems did the students have on the field trip?","options":["A Their transport broke down.","B The weather was poor.","C Some equipment was faulty.","D The guide was unhelpful.","E They had too little time at the bird hide."],"answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"The minibus broke down and two testing kits had out-of-date chemicals. The weather was better than forecast, the guide was brilliant, and they stayed longer at the hide."},{"q":"Which TWO things will be main sections of their report?","options":["A the plant survey","B the water quality results","C a large number of photographs","D the interview with the warden","E the bird counts"],"answer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Water results become a limitation, photos are limited to one or two, and bird counts go in the appendix."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lmcq2-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lmcq2","title":"The rise of repair cafés","context":"Section 4: part of a lecture on a sustainability and business course about community repair cafés.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"In the last part of today’s lecture, I want to look at repair cafés. These are free community events, usually held in a church hall or a library once a month, where volunteers with practical skills help people fix broken items: toasters, lamps, bicycles, torn clothes, you name it."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Why have they become so popular? A researcher called Tobias Brandt surveyed visitors to repair cafés in the Dunmore region and asked them why they came. You might assume the main reason is saving money, and certainly some people mentioned it, but it came fairly low on the list. Far more common was a dislike of waste, a feeling that throwing away something that could be fixed is simply wrong. The other answer that came up again and again was learning: people valued being shown how to do the repair themselves. Volunteers are encouraged to explain what they’re doing rather than just doing it. Interestingly, very few people said they came to meet friends, although many did end up chatting for hours."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"So what are the challenges for organisers? You might think finding volunteers would be the hardest part, but in Brandt’s study most cafés had more helpers than they needed. Retired engineers and electricians are often keen to share their skills. Nor was money for venues a major issue, as halls are often lent free of charge."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The two problems organisers raised most often were these. The first was insurance. If a repaired kettle later causes a fire, who is responsible? Finding affordable insurance was a constant worry. The second was spare parts. Many modern products are designed so that they can’t be opened, and manufacturers often refuse to sell replacement parts to the public, which means some items simply can’t be saved."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"This brings us to the so-called right to repair, which we’ll discuss next week."}],"groups":[{"type":"lmcq2","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"According to Brandt’s survey, which TWO were the most common reasons for attending a repair café?","options":["A to save money","B to avoid waste","C to learn repair skills","D to meet friends","E to buy second-hand goods"],"answer":["B","C"],"explanation":"Saving money came fairly low and few came to meet friends; the common answers were a dislike of waste and learning to do repairs."},{"q":"Which TWO difficulties did organisers report most often?","options":["A a shortage of volunteers","B the cost of renting halls","C obtaining insurance","D getting spare parts","E a lack of publicity"],"answer":["C","D"],"explanation":"Most cafés had more helpers than needed and halls are often free; the two problems raised most were insurance and spare parts."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lnote-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lnote","title":"How habits form","context":"Section 4: part of a psychology lecture on how habits are formed and broken.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Good morning. Today I’m going to talk about habits: how they form, and why they’re so hard to break. In psychological terms, a habit is a behaviour that has become automatic, something we do without really deciding to do it."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Habits develop through repetition, but repetition alone isn’t enough. What matters is repeating the behaviour in a stable context: the same place, the same time of day, or straight after the same event. Over time, the context itself starts to trigger the behaviour."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Researchers often describe this as a loop with three parts. First there’s a cue, such as walking into your kitchen. Then there’s the routine, for instance making a coffee. And finally there’s the reward, the pleasant feeling that makes the brain want to repeat the whole sequence."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Let me tell you about a study by Doctor Ilse Varga and her team at the Halden Institute. They asked volunteers to choose one new behaviour and do it every day after breakfast. Some chose to go for a run, others to eat a piece of fruit, and others simply to drink a glass of water. Perhaps not surprisingly, the simplest behaviours became automatic fastest. Drinking water took the least time, and exercise took the longest."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"What surprised the team was that missing a single day had very little effect. People who forgot once usually caught up quickly, so the popular idea that one slip ruins all your progress isn’t supported."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"So how do we break an unwanted habit? Willpower, it seems, is less useful than we think. A more effective strategy is to change the environment, so that the cue no longer appears. People who move house, for example, often find it easier to give up habits they’ve had for years. Another approach is substitution: keeping the cue but replacing the routine with something healthier, such as chewing gum instead of reaching for a snack."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Next week we’ll look at how technology companies use these ideas when they design apps."}],"groups":[{"type":"lnote","instructions":"Complete the notes below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.","outline":{"style":"notes","rows":[["Habit formation"],["• behaviour repeated in a stable [1], e.g. same place or time"],["• three-part loop: cue → routine → [2]"],["Varga study (Halden Institute)"],["• one new behaviour done daily after [3]"],["• quickest to become automatic: drinking [4]; slowest: exercise"],["• missing one day had little effect"],["Breaking habits"],["• more effective than willpower: change the [5]"],["• substitution, e.g. chewing gum instead of a snack"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"context","explanation":"’repeating the behaviour in a stable context’."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"reward","explanation":"Cue, then routine, ’and finally there’s the reward’."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"breakfast","explanation":"’do it every day after breakfast’."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"water","explanation":"Fruit and running were other choices; 'Drinking water took the least time'. 'A glass of water' is too long for the word limit and does not fit after 'drinking'."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"environment","explanation":"’Willpower ... is less useful ... A more effective strategy is to change the environment’."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lnote-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lnote","title":"Living light in the ocean","context":"Section 4: part of a marine biology lecture on bioluminescence.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"In today’s lecture I’d like to look at bioluminescence, the ability of living things to produce their own light. On land it’s fairly rare, fireflies being the best-known example, but in the deep ocean it’s extremely common. In the darkest layers of the sea, a large proportion of animals can glow in some way."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Let’s start with the chemistry. The light is produced when a molecule called luciferin reacts with oxygen, and the reaction is controlled by an enzyme. Unlike a light bulb, almost none of the energy is lost as heat, which is why it’s sometimes called cold light."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"You might expect sea creatures to glow in all sorts of colours, but most of the light we see is blue. That isn’t an accident. Red light is absorbed very quickly by water, whereas blue travels the furthest, so it’s the most useful colour for signalling."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"So what is the light actually for? There are three main uses. Some predators use it to attract prey, dangling a glowing lure in front of their mouths. Other animals use it in defence, releasing a cloud of glowing fluid to confuse an attacker. And some species use patterns of flashes to find a mate, a little like a code."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"One of the cleverest uses was described by Doctor Samira Anwar and her colleagues, who studied a small squid in the waters off the island of Kerrin. Seen from below, an animal swimming near the surface would normally appear as a dark outline against the moonlight. The squid gets round this by producing light on its underside, which hides its shadow. This is known as counter-illumination."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Finally, practical applications. Scientists have learned to use the genes behind bioluminescence in the laboratory, and one promising use is detecting pollution in water. Certain glowing bacteria grow dimmer when toxic chemicals are present, so a fall in brightness acts as an early warning."}],"groups":[{"type":"lnote","instructions":"Complete the notes below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.","outline":{"style":"notes","rows":[["Bioluminescence"],["• rare on land; very common in the deep [1]"],["Chemistry"],["• luciferin reacts with [2]; an enzyme controls the reaction"],["• almost no energy lost as heat (“cold light”)"],["• most light is [3] because it travels furthest in water"],["Uses"],["• attracting prey, confusing attackers, finding a mate"],["• Anwar’s squid: light on its underside hides its [4]"],["Applications"],["• glowing bacteria used to detect [5] in water"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"ocean","alt":["sea"],"explanation":"’in the deep ocean it’s extremely common’."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"oxygen","explanation":"’luciferin reacts with oxygen’; the enzyme controls it."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"blue","explanation":"Red is absorbed quickly; ’blue travels the furthest’."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"shadow","explanation":"’producing light on its underside, which hides its shadow’."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"pollution","alt":["toxic chemicals"],"explanation":"’one promising use is detecting pollution in water’."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"la-lnote-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lnote","title":"Listening to forests","context":"Section 4: part of an environmental science lecture on monitoring wildlife with sound.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Good afternoon. Today’s topic is acoustic monitoring, which means using sound to study wildlife. Traditionally, if ecologists wanted to know which species lived in a forest, they had to walk through it and count what they saw. That’s slow and expensive, and many animals simply hide when people approach."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The alternative is to leave small recording devices, usually called acoustic sensors, fixed to trees for weeks or even months at a time. They’re cheap, they don’t disturb the animals, and crucially, they keep working at night, when many frogs, owls and insects are most active."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Of course, a single sensor can produce thousands of hours of audio, far more than any researcher could listen to. So the recordings are analysed by software that identifies species by their calls. These programs are trained on libraries of recordings that have already been labelled by experts."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Let me give you an example. A team led by Professor Joel Mendez placed sensors in two parts of the Kesra Forest, one untouched and one that had recently been logged. In the untouched area, the dawn chorus, when birds sing together at sunrise, lasted well over an hour. In the logged area it was not only quieter, it was also much shorter, sometimes ending after just twenty minutes. The team argued that the length of the chorus could serve as a simple measure of a forest’s health."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"There are limitations, however. Wind can be a nuisance, but the biggest problem is rain. Heavy rain creates so much noise that it masks the calls of smaller animals, so recordings from wet days often have to be thrown away. And of course, the method only works for species that make sounds. A silent animal is invisible to a microphone."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"In the seminar, we’ll listen to some real recordings and try identifying the species ourselves."}],"groups":[{"type":"lnote","instructions":"Complete the notes below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.","outline":{"style":"notes","rows":[["Acoustic monitoring"],["• recording devices (acoustic [1]) fixed to trees"],["• advantages: cheap, cause no disturbance, keep working at [2]"],["• software identifies species by their [3]"],["Mendez study (Kesra Forest)"],["• logged area: dawn chorus quieter and much [4]"],["• length of chorus = possible measure of forest [5]"],["Limitations"],["• heavy rain masks the calls of small animals"],["• silent species cannot be detected"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"sensors","explanation":"’usually called acoustic sensors’."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"night","explanation":"’they keep working at night’."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"calls","explanation":"’software that identifies species by their calls’."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"shorter","explanation":"’not only quieter, it was also much shorter’; quieter is already in the notes."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"health","explanation":"’a simple measure of a forest’s health’."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lsent-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lsent","title":"Starting at a climbing centre","context":"A man phones the Redfern Climbing Centre to ask about starting climbing as a beginner.","lines":[{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Redfern Climbing Centre, Jade speaking. How can I help?"},{"speaker":"Caller","text":"Hi. I'm interested in taking up climbing, but I've never done it before. What do I need to do?"},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Great. All beginners have to do an introductory session first, so our instructors can show you the basics and check you can use the safety equipment. We normally run them on Tuesday and Thursday evenings, but the Thursday ones are fully booked until next month, I'm afraid."},{"speaker":"Caller","text":"So it would be a Tuesday, then. That's fine. How long does it last?"},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"About two hours, starting at seven. Can I take your name?"},{"speaker":"Caller","text":"Yes, it's Daniel Fairclough. That's F, A, I, R, C, L, O, U, G, H."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Thanks, Daniel. Is there anything else you'd like to know?"},{"speaker":"Caller","text":"Yes, actually. Do I need any special clothes?"},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Not at all. Some people think you need proper sportswear, but just wear loose clothing that lets you stretch. Avoid jeans, though. They're too stiff."},{"speaker":"Caller","text":"And what about shoes? I've only got running shoes."},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"You'll need climbing shoes, but you don't have to buy them straight away. You can hire them from the reception desk for three pounds. People often go to the café to ask for them, but it's definitely reception."},{"speaker":"Caller","text":"Okay. And if I decide to join after the session, what are the advantages of membership?"},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Well, members can climb any time we're open, and they also get ten percent off in our equipment shop, which is handy when you're ready to buy your own shoes and harness."},{"speaker":"Caller","text":"That sounds good. Could you book me in for next Tuesday, please?"},{"speaker":"Receptionist","text":"Of course. You're booked in for seven o'clock next Tuesday."}],"groups":[{"type":"lsent","instructions":"Complete the sentences below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Daniel's introductory session will take place on a ____ evening.","answer":"Tuesday","explanation":"Thursday sessions are fully booked, so it will be a Tuesday."},{"q":"For the session, Daniel should wear ____.","answer":"loose clothing","explanation":"No special sportswear is needed: 'just wear loose clothing'. Jeans should be avoided."},{"q":"Climbing shoes can be hired at the ____.","answer":"reception desk","alt":["reception"],"explanation":"You can hire them from the reception desk; people wrongly go to the café."},{"q":"Members receive a discount in the centre's ____.","answer":"equipment shop","explanation":"Members get ten percent off in our equipment shop."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lsent-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lsent","title":"How migrating birds find their way","context":"Part of a biology lecture on navigation in migrating birds, based on a study of an invented songbird.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Good afternoon. Today we're looking at one of the most remarkable feats in the animal world: the ability of migrating birds to travel thousands of kilometres and arrive at almost exactly the same place each year. I'll be referring to a study of a small songbird, the Calder warbler, an invented species for our purposes, which breeds in northern forests and spends the winter in coastal wetlands far to the south."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Let's start with young birds. On their first migration, they travel alone, without adults to follow. So how do they know where to go? The evidence suggests they're born with an inherited sense of direction. When young warblers raised in captivity were released, they set off on the correct course, even though they had never made the journey."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Experienced birds, however, use a range of cues. At night, when most warblers fly, they use the stars. But what happens when the sky is covered in cloud? Some researchers believed the birds simply waited for clear weather. In fact, the warblers carried on flying, and the best explanation is that they were using the Earth's magnetic field, which they can detect even when they can't see a single star."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"As they approach their destination, there's another shift. The birds begin to recognise landmarks, such as coastlines, mountain ranges and large rivers, which help them find their exact wintering site."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"So how do we know all this? In the past, scientists could only put rings on birds' legs and hope someone found them. In the Calder study, the team originally planned to use GPS devices, but these turned out to be too heavy for such a small bird. Instead, they attached tiny radio tags, each weighing less than a paperclip, to the birds' backs."}],"groups":[{"type":"lsent","instructions":"Complete the sentences below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Young warblers appear to be born with a sense of ____.","answer":"direction","explanation":"They're born with an inherited sense of direction."},{"q":"When clouds hide the stars, the birds are probably guided by the Earth's ____.","answer":"magnetic field","explanation":"They did not wait for clear weather; they used the Earth's magnetic field."},{"q":"Near the end of the journey, the birds identify ____ such as rivers and coastlines.","answer":"landmarks","explanation":"The birds begin to recognise landmarks, such as coastlines and large rivers."},{"q":"Instead of GPS devices, the researchers fixed small ____ to the birds.","answer":"radio tags","explanation":"GPS devices were too heavy, so they attached tiny radio tags."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lsent-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lsent","title":"Feedback on a business report","context":"A tutor, Dr Wells, gives a business student, Amira, feedback on her draft report about a family-run bakery.","lines":[{"speaker":"Dr Wells","text":"Come in, Amira. I've read your draft report on Hollin's Bakery, so let's go through my comments."},{"speaker":"Amira","text":"Thanks. I was quite pleased with the introduction, actually. I spent ages on it."},{"speaker":"Dr Wells","text":"It's clear, but it isn't the strongest part. What really stood out was your customer survey. Getting over sixty responses from people in the shop was impressive, and you analysed them carefully."},{"speaker":"Amira","text":"Oh, that's good to hear. I wasn't sure my questions were right."},{"speaker":"Dr Wells","text":"They were fine. Now, the main problem is length. You're about eight hundred words over the limit, so something has to go. I'd cut most of the section on the bakery's history. It's interesting that the family started the business in the nineteen fifties, but it doesn't help answer your research question."},{"speaker":"Amira","text":"Okay. Could I keep one or two sentences, maybe?"},{"speaker":"Dr Wells","text":"That's fine. What's missing, though, is any comparison. You say their bread is expensive, but expensive compared to what?"},{"speaker":"Amira","text":"I did think about comparing them with other independent bakeries, but there aren't really any in that part of town."},{"speaker":"Dr Wells","text":"Then compare their prices with the supermarkets nearby. That's who they're really competing with."},{"speaker":"Amira","text":"That makes sense. There are two within walking distance."},{"speaker":"Dr Wells","text":"Perfect. And one last thing: please don't email the final version to me. The department wants everything submitted through the online portal now, so it can be checked automatically."},{"speaker":"Amira","text":"Right. And is the deadline still the end of the month?"},{"speaker":"Dr Wells","text":"Yes, nothing's changed there. Good luck with the rewrite."}],"groups":[{"type":"lsent","instructions":"Complete the sentences below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Dr Wells thinks the best part of the draft is the ____.","answer":"customer survey","explanation":"Amira thinks it's the introduction, but Dr Wells says 'what really stood out was your customer survey'.","alt":["survey"]},{"q":"Amira should remove most of the material on the bakery's ____.","answer":"history","explanation":"'I'd cut most of the section on the bakery's history.'"},{"q":"She should compare the bakery's prices with those of local ____.","answer":"supermarkets","explanation":"There are no other independent bakeries, so compare prices with the supermarkets nearby."},{"q":"The final report must be submitted via the ____.","answer":"online portal","explanation":"Not by email: 'submitted through the online portal'.","alt":["portal"]}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lshort-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lshort","title":"A bag left on a bus","context":"A woman phones the lost property office of a city bus company about a bag she left on a bus.","lines":[{"speaker":"Officer","text":"City Link Buses, lost property. How can I help?"},{"speaker":"Woman","text":"Hello. I left a bag on one of your buses yesterday afternoon, and I'm hoping someone handed it in."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Let's see what we've got. Which route were you on?"},{"speaker":"Woman","text":"It was the seventy-four. No, sorry, the seventy-four is the one I take to work. Yesterday I was on the forty-seven, going towards the hospital."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"The forty-seven, okay. And can you describe the bag?"},{"speaker":"Woman","text":"It's a dark green backpack, quite small, with a brown leather label on the front."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Is there a name on the label?"},{"speaker":"Woman","text":"Yes, my surname. It's Varga. That's V, A, R, G, A."},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Just a moment. Yes, we have a green backpack with that name. The driver handed it in last night. Good news."},{"speaker":"Woman","text":"Oh, that's such a relief. My work laptop is in there. Can I come and get it today?"},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"I'm afraid not. Items from yesterday are still at the depot. They come to our office here on Wednesday, but we're closed on Wednesdays for staff training. So the earliest you could collect it would be Thursday morning."},{"speaker":"Woman","text":"Thursday morning is fine. Is there anything I need to bring?"},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"Some identification with your name on it, like a driving licence. And there's a small handling fee. It used to be two pounds, but it went up in January, so it's three pounds fifty now."},{"speaker":"Woman","text":"That's no problem. Where exactly is your office?"},{"speaker":"Officer","text":"We're on the ground floor of the main bus station, next to the ticket machines."}],"groups":[{"type":"lshort","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Write NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Which bus route was the woman on when she lost her bag?","answer":"47","alt":["forty-seven","the forty-seven","route 47"],"explanation":"She first says seventy-four, then corrects herself: 'Yesterday I was on the forty-seven'."},{"q":"What surname is written on the bag's label?","answer":"Varga","explanation":"'It's Varga. That's V, A, R, G, A.'"},{"q":"When can she collect the bag?","answer":"Thursday morning","alt":["on Thursday morning","Thursday","on Thursday"],"explanation":"Items arrive on Wednesday but the office is closed then, so the earliest is Thursday morning."},{"q":"How much is the handling fee?","answer":"£3.50","alt":["three pounds fifty","3.50"],"explanation":"It used to be two pounds; it is now three pounds fifty."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lshort-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lshort","title":"Volunteering at a half marathon","context":"An organiser briefs volunteers who will help at the Brackenford Half Marathon.","lines":[{"speaker":"Organiser","text":"Good evening, everyone, and thank you so much for volunteering at this year's Brackenford Half Marathon. We've got around three thousand runners taking part, so we really couldn't do it without you. I'll go through the key information now, and you'll also get it all by email."},{"speaker":"Organiser","text":"First, timings. The race starts at half past eight, but we need you in place well before that. Last year we asked people to arrive at six thirty, and, to be honest, many of you were standing around in the cold for ages. So this year, please arrive at the start area at a quarter to seven. That still gives us plenty of time."},{"speaker":"Organiser","text":"When you arrive, you'll need your volunteer T-shirt, so that runners and spectators can recognise you. Please don't go to the information desk for these, as the staff there will be very busy answering questions from the public. Instead, collect your shirt from the blue tent, right beside the main stage."},{"speaker":"Organiser","text":"Most of you will be working at the water stations along the route, handing out paper cups of water. We've decided not to give runners plastic bottles this year. They create a lot of waste, and people throw half-full bottles onto the road, which is a hazard for the other runners."},{"speaker":"Organiser","text":"Now, safety. If you see a runner who is injured or unwell, don't try to treat them yourself, and there's no need to phone me, either. Use the radio in your pack and call the medical team directly. They have staff every two kilometres and can reach anyone very quickly."},{"speaker":"Organiser","text":"Finally, there'll be a free lunch for all volunteers after the race, in the sports hall."}],"groups":[{"type":"lshort","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Write NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"What time should volunteers arrive at the start area this year?","answer":"6.45","alt":["6:45","a quarter to seven","quarter to seven","6.45 am","6.45am","6:45am","6:45 am"],"explanation":"Six thirty was last year's time; this year it's 'a quarter to seven'."},{"q":"Where should volunteers collect their T-shirts?","answer":"the blue tent","alt":["blue tent"],"explanation":"Not the information desk, but 'the blue tent, right beside the main stage'."},{"q":"What will water-station volunteers not give to runners this year?","answer":"plastic bottles","explanation":"'We've decided not to give runners plastic bottles this year.' They hand out paper cups instead."},{"q":"Who should volunteers call if a runner is injured?","answer":"the medical team","alt":["medical team"],"explanation":"Don't phone the organiser; 'call the medical team directly'."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lshort-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lshort","title":"Ancient underground water channels","context":"Part of an archaeology lecture on an underground water system in an invented desert region.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Today I'm going to talk about one of the most impressive engineering achievements of the ancient world: the underground water channels of the Tarsa Plateau, a dry upland region in our invented case study. These tunnels carried water from underground reserves beneath the mountains to farming villages many kilometres away, and they did it without any pumps, using only a gentle slope."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Let's begin with how they were built. Workers dug a line of vertical shafts and then connected them at the bottom to form a tunnel. Early researchers assumed the tunnel walls were lined with stone, as that was the main building material in the region. But excavations in the last decade have shown that the walls were actually lined with fired clay, made into curved sections a bit like pipes cut in half. This stopped the soft soil from collapsing and reduced water loss."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The scale is remarkable. When the longest tunnel was first surveyed, it was estimated at around nine kilometres. However, a later survey traced it further back into the hills, and we now know that it ran for twelve kilometres."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"So who looked after all this? For a long time, the assumption was that a powerful king must have organised the work. Yet inscriptions found near the villages suggest otherwise. Each tunnel seems to have been managed by a council of farmers, who decided how the water was shared and who would carry out repairs."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Finally, why was the system abandoned? Many textbooks say a long drought was responsible. But the underground reserves would still have held water during a drought. The evidence, including cracked tunnel sections and collapsed shafts, points instead to an earthquake, after which the damage was simply too great to repair."}],"groups":[{"type":"lshort","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Write NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"What material were the tunnel walls lined with?","answer":"fired clay","explanation":"Early researchers assumed stone, but excavations show the walls were lined with fired clay."},{"q":"How long was the longest tunnel?","answer":"12 kilometres","alt":["twelve kilometres","12 km","12 kilometers","twelve kilometers","12"],"explanation":"First estimated at nine kilometres; a later survey showed it ran for twelve kilometres."},{"q":"Who managed each tunnel?","answer":"council of farmers","alt":["the council of farmers","farmers"],"explanation":"Not a king: 'each tunnel seems to have been managed by a council of farmers'."},{"q":"What caused the system to be abandoned?","answer":"an earthquake","alt":["earthquake"],"explanation":"A drought is often blamed, but the evidence points to an earthquake."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lsumm-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"lsumm","title":"The rise and fall of a canal","context":"Part of a history lecture about the Harlow Canal in an invented industrial region.","lines":[{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"This morning we're turning to transport history, and specifically to the Harlow Canal, which I've chosen because its story is typical of many smaller canals. It was dug in the late seventeen hundreds and ran for about thirty kilometres, from the quarries in the hills down to the port at Selby Bay."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Now, you'll often read that canals like this one were built for coal. It's true that some coal was carried on the Harlow, but the records show that its main cargo was limestone, which was burnt in kilns near the port to make lime for building and farming."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"For the first fifty years or so, the canal was extremely profitable. Each boat was pulled by a single horse walking along the towpath, and one horse could move a load many times heavier than it could pull on a road."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"The turning point came in the eighteen forties. Some historians blame competition from improved roads, but roads at this time were still slow and badly maintained. The real threat was the railway, which reached Harlow in eighteen forty-six and could carry goods in a fraction of the time. Within twenty years, most of the canal's traffic had gone."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"In the twentieth century, some sections were filled in and built over. The remaining stretches had no commercial traffic at all, and they were used mainly for irrigation, with local farmers pumping water from the canal onto their fields in dry summers."},{"speaker":"Lecturer","text":"Since the nineteen eighties, a volunteer trust has been restoring the canal. Interestingly, the people who use it most today are not boaters, as you might expect, because only a few boats can get through the old locks. Instead, it attracts thousands of walkers, who use the towpath as a long-distance trail."}],"groups":[{"type":"lsumm","instructions":"Complete the summary below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["Although canals are often linked with coal, the Harlow Canal mainly transported [1]. Its boats were pulled by horses along the towpath. The canal began to lose business when the [2] arrived in the 1840s. In the twentieth century, the surviving sections were used by farmers for [3]. Today, after restoration by volunteers, the canal is most popular with [4]."]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"limestone","explanation":"Some coal was carried, but 'its main cargo was limestone'."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"railway","explanation":"Improved roads are rejected; 'the real threat was the railway'."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"irrigation","explanation":"The remaining stretches were used mainly for irrigation by local farmers."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"walkers","explanation":"Not boaters, as you might expect; it attracts thousands of walkers."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lsumm-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"lsumm","title":"Bees on the roof","context":"The coordinator of a city beekeeping project speaks to a local community group.","lines":[{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"Thanks for inviting me along this evening. I'm Rosa Delaney, and I coordinate the Kettering Street Bee Project. I'd like to tell you how it started and how you can get involved."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"The project began four years ago. Our first idea was to put hives on the roof of the town hall, but the council turned us down because of concerns about public access. Then the central library offered us the use of its flat roof, which turned out to be perfect. It's sunny, it's sheltered from the wind, and the staff can keep an eye on things."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"We now have eight hives up there. If you'd like to volunteer, the first step is a short course, which runs on three Saturday mornings. We provide the protective suits and veils, but we do ask volunteers to buy their own gloves, partly for hygiene reasons and partly because getting the right fit really matters. They cost about twelve pounds."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"Now, people always ask about the honey. In a good year we collect around a hundred and fifty jars. We did talk to some local shops about selling it, but the paperwork was too complicated for a small group like ours. So instead, we sell it at the weekly market on Fridays, and it usually sells out by lunchtime."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"All the money we make goes back into the project, and most of it is spent on planting wildflowers in parks and along roadsides across the city. That's important, because bees need a wide variety of food, and a neatly mown lawn offers them almost nothing."},{"speaker":"Coordinator","text":"So, if you're interested, please leave your email address on the sheet by the door, and I'll send you the dates of the next course."}],"groups":[{"type":"lsumm","instructions":"Complete the summary below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["After an earlier request was refused, the project was allowed to keep its hives on the roof of the [1]. New volunteers attend a short course; suits are supplied, but volunteers must buy their own [2]. The honey is not sold in shops but at a [3] once a week, and the money raised pays for planting [4] around the city."]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"library","explanation":"The town hall request was turned down; the central library offered its flat roof."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"gloves","explanation":"Suits and veils are provided, but volunteers buy their own gloves."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"market","explanation":"Shops were too complicated; the honey is sold at the weekly market."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"wildflowers","alt":["wild flowers"],"explanation":"Most of the money is spent on planting wildflowers in parks and along roadsides."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-lsumm-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"lsumm","title":"Handwritten notes and memory","context":"Two psychology students, Nadia and Ben, discuss a study they have read for their seminar on note-taking.","lines":[{"speaker":"Ben","text":"Nadia, did you finish reading that study for Thursday's seminar? The one about note-taking?"},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"The one by Morrow and Patel? Yes, I thought it was really interesting. Basically, they compared two groups of students. One group wrote their notes by hand, and the other typed them on laptops."},{"speaker":"Ben","text":"I thought there was a third group using tablets."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"They mention tablets in the introduction, but they decided not to include them in the end. So it's just two groups. Everyone watched the same recorded lecture, about forty minutes long."},{"speaker":"Ben","text":"Right, and then they were tested a week later. What surprised me was that on the simple factual questions, like dates and names, there was hardly any difference."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"Exactly. But the handwriting group did much better on questions about concepts, where you had to understand how the ideas were connected."},{"speaker":"Ben","text":"And the explanation they give is that you can't write as fast as you type, so..."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"So you're forced to summarise what you hear, instead of copying it down word for word. You have to process it."},{"speaker":"Ben","text":"That makes sense. I tend to type everything the lecturer says and then never look at it again."},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"Me too, honestly. But I do think the study has a weakness. Some people might criticise the sample size, but eighty participants is quite reasonable for this kind of study."},{"speaker":"Ben","text":"I agree. For me, the bigger issue is who they were. They were all engineers, weren't they?"},{"speaker":"Nadia","text":"Yes, all from the same engineering faculty. And engineers might take notes very differently from, say, history students, who have to follow long arguments."},{"speaker":"Ben","text":"Good point. Let's raise that in the seminar."}],"groups":[{"type":"lsumm","instructions":"Complete the summary below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["The study compared students who wrote notes by hand with students who used [1]. A week after watching a lecture, both groups scored similarly on factual questions, but the handwriting group did better on questions about [2]. The researchers suggest that writing by hand makes students [3] the information rather than copy it. Nadia and Ben feel the main limitation is that all the participants were [4]."]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"laptops","explanation":"The other group typed their notes on laptops; tablets were not included in the end."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"concepts","explanation":"The handwriting group did much better on questions about concepts."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"summarise","alt":["summarize","process"],"explanation":"You're forced to summarise what you hear instead of copying it word for word: 'You have to process it'. Both summarise and process fit the gap."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"engineers","explanation":"Sample size is rejected as a problem; the bigger issue is that they were all engineers."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-ltable-1","kind":"listening","qtype":"ltable","title":"Hiring bikes for a weekend","context":"A customer phones a bicycle hire shop in the town of Elmbridge to ask about bikes for a weekend trip.","lines":[{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Good morning, Elmbridge Cycle Hire. How can I help?"},{"speaker":"Customer","text":"Hi. My partner and I are planning a weekend trip, and I'd like to know what kinds of bikes you have and how much they cost."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Sure. We've got three main types. The most popular is the city bike, and that's fourteen pounds a day. Oh, sorry, I'm looking at last year's price list. It's sixteen pounds a day now."},{"speaker":"Customer","text":"Sixteen, okay. And what comes with it?"},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Every city bike has a basket on the front, and we also fit a lock, so you don't need to bring your own. Lights aren't included, though. You'd pay a little extra for those."},{"speaker":"Customer","text":"That's fine, we won't be riding at night. What about something for rougher paths? We were thinking of going up into the hills on the Saturday."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Then you'd want a mountain bike. They're twenty-two pounds a day, and that includes a helmet. The only thing is that we don't keep them here at the station branch. They're only available from our harbour branch, which is about ten minutes' walk away."},{"speaker":"Customer","text":"The harbour. Right. And do you have electric bikes? My partner isn't very keen on hills."},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"We do. They're thirty-five pounds a day. Some people think that's a lot, but it's actually cheaper than most places around here."},{"speaker":"Customer","text":"And how far can you go on one charge?"},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"The battery lasts up to six hours. The manufacturer says eight, but in my experience, with all the hills round here, six is more realistic."},{"speaker":"Customer","text":"Six hours should be plenty. I think we'll go for one electric bike and one mountain bike, then. Can I reserve them now?"},{"speaker":"Assistant","text":"Of course. I'll just need a name and a phone number, and you can pay when you collect them."}],"groups":[{"type":"ltable","instructions":"Complete the table below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"table","rows":[["Type of bike","Price per day","Notes"],["City bike","£[1]","comes with a basket and a [2]"],["Mountain bike","£22","helmet included; collect from the [3] branch"],["Electric bike","£[4]","battery lasts up to [5] hours"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"16","alt":["sixteen","£16"],"explanation":"Fourteen pounds is last year's price; the assistant corrects it to sixteen pounds a day."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"lock","explanation":"City bikes have a basket and 'we also fit a lock'. Lights are not included."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"harbour","alt":["harbor"],"explanation":"Mountain bikes are not kept at the station branch; they are 'only available from our harbour branch'."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"35","alt":["thirty-five","thirty five","£35"],"explanation":"Electric bikes are thirty-five pounds a day."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"6","alt":["six"],"explanation":"The manufacturer says eight, but the assistant says the battery lasts up to six hours."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-ltable-2","kind":"listening","qtype":"ltable","title":"Holiday workshops at a science centre","context":"A guide at the Corran Valley Science Centre describes the workshops on offer during the school holidays.","lines":[{"speaker":"Guide","text":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the Corran Valley Science Centre. I'm going to tell you about the three workshops we're running over the school holidays, so you can decide which ones suit your children."},{"speaker":"Guide","text":"Let's start with Rocket Lab, which is our most popular session. Last year it was open to children from seven upwards, but we found the youngest ones struggled with the tools, so this year it's for ages eight to twelve. Participants build a water rocket from a plastic bottle and then, weather permitting, launch it on the field behind the centre. We did experiment with paper rockets at one point, but they didn't fly very far, so we've dropped those."},{"speaker":"Guide","text":"Next is Night Sky, which takes place in our planetarium. This one is suitable for all ages, so it's ideal for families who want to do something together. After a short show about the constellations, everyone makes a star map to take home. Some parents ask whether children get to use a telescope. We do have one, but it's only used at our evening events, not in this workshop."},{"speaker":"Guide","text":"Finally, there's Bug Hunt, which is designed for our younger visitors, from four to seven years old. The children go out into the wildlife garden with nets and collecting pots. Then they come back inside and look at what they've found under a microscope. And don't worry, all the insects are returned to the garden afterwards."},{"speaker":"Guide","text":"You can book all three workshops at the front desk or on our website. Rocket Lab sessions fill up very quickly, so if you're interested in that one, I'd recommend booking today."}],"groups":[{"type":"ltable","instructions":"Complete the table below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"table","rows":[["Workshop","Age group","Main activity"],["Rocket Lab","[1] to 12","build and launch a [2] rocket"],["Night Sky","all ages","watch a show, then make a star [3]"],["Bug Hunt","4 to [4]","study insects under a [5]"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"8","alt":["eight"],"explanation":"It used to start at seven, but 'this year it's for ages eight to twelve'."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"water","explanation":"Participants build a water rocket; paper rockets were dropped."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"map","explanation":"Everyone makes a star map to take home. The telescope is only for evening events."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"7","alt":["seven"],"explanation":"Bug Hunt is for children 'from four to seven years old'."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"microscope","explanation":"They look at what they've found under a microscope."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"lb-ltable-3","kind":"listening","qtype":"ltable","title":"Choosing a material for a lunch container","context":"Two engineering students, Priya and Marcus, compare materials for a reusable food container in their design project.","lines":[{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Okay, Priya, we need to choose a material for our lunch container before we meet Dr Hale on Thursday. Shall we go through the three on our shortlist?"},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Yes. Let's start with bamboo fibre. I think its biggest plus is that it's so lightweight. I weighed our sample and it was about half the weight of the plastic one."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"I'd have said its main strength was that it's biodegradable. But the article I read said that's only true if it doesn't contain resin, and most of it does."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Fair point. So we'll go with lightweight. The downside is that it tends to absorb stains. I left some curry in the sample overnight, and it went yellow."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"That would definitely put people off. What about glass?"},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Glass is great because it's so easy to clean. You can put it in a dishwasher hundreds of times and it still looks new."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"And I know people worry that it'll break, but the tempered kind is surprisingly tough. For me, the real problem is that it's heavy. Our brief says the container is for schoolchildren, and they'd struggle to carry it around all day."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Agreed. Then there's recycled steel. The supplier quoted us four pounds a unit at first."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"But that was for small orders, wasn't it? If we order more than five hundred, it drops to three pounds twenty."},{"speaker":"Priya","text":"Yes, and that's the figure we should use, because the brief assumes mass production. The only real disadvantage is that you can't put it in a microwave."},{"speaker":"Marcus","text":"Which might be a deal-breaker for some parents. Let's put all this in a table for Dr Hale and see what she thinks."}],"groups":[{"type":"ltable","instructions":"Complete the table below. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","outline":{"style":"table","rows":[["Material","Advantage","Disadvantage"],["Bamboo fibre","very [1]","tends to absorb [2]"],["Glass","easy to [3]","too [4] for children to carry"],["Recycled steel","about £[5] per unit in large orders","cannot be used in a microwave"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"lightweight","explanation":"Priya: its biggest plus is that it's so lightweight. Marcus's 'biodegradable' idea is rejected."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"stains","explanation":"The downside is that it tends to absorb stains."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"clean","explanation":"Glass is great because it's so easy to clean."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"heavy","explanation":"Breaking is not the real worry; 'the real problem is that it's heavy'."},{"q":"Gap 5","answer":"3.20","alt":["three pounds twenty","£3.20"],"explanation":"Four pounds is for small orders; large orders drop to three pounds twenty."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-diag-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"diag","title":"Paper from rice straw","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In the village of Hanmori, paper has been made from rice straw for generations. After the harvest, farmers once burned the leftover straw in their fields, but a small workshop now buys it and turns it into writing paper, gift wrap and lampshades. The workshop employs six people, most of them women from local farming families, and it uses no chemicals at any stage."},{"label":"B","text":"The process begins by cutting the straw into short pieces, which are soaked in water for two days to soften them. The pieces are then boiled for several hours with wood ash, which breaks down the tough outer layer of the stalks and separates the fibres."},{"label":"C","text":"Next, the fibres are rinsed in clean water and beaten with wooden hammers until they form a smooth pulp. This stage is the most tiring part of the work, and the workshop has recently bought an electric beater to help."},{"label":"D","text":"The pulp is stirred into a large tank of water. A worker dips a frame covered with a fine mesh into the tank and lifts it out slowly, so that an even layer of fibres is left on the mesh. Each new sheet is pressed to remove water and then brushed onto a heated metal wall, where it dries in about twenty minutes."},{"label":"E","text":"The finished paper is slightly rough and pale brown in colour. Some sheets are dyed with natural colours from leaves and flowers. Visitors to the workshop can try making a sheet themselves, and the owners say the paper has become a popular souvenir."}],"groups":[{"type":"diag","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Straw is cut into short pieces and [1] in water for two days"],["Pieces are boiled with wood [2] so that the fibres separate"],["Fibres are rinsed and beaten into a smooth [3]"],["A frame with a fine [4] is lifted out of the tank"],["Each sheet is pressed and then dried on a heated metal wall"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"soaked","explanation":"B: short pieces, which are soaked in water for two days."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"ash","explanation":"B: boiled for several hours with wood ash, which separates the fibres."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"pulp","explanation":"C: beaten with wooden hammers until they form a smooth pulp."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"mesh","explanation":"D: a frame covered with a fine mesh is dipped into the tank and lifted out."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-diag-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"diag","title":"Towers that catch the wind","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Long before electric air conditioning, builders in the hot, dry region around the city of Qarvan cooled their houses with tall towers known as wind-catchers. Rising several metres above the roof, these structures can lower indoor temperatures noticeably without consuming any energy at all."},{"label":"B","text":"At the top of each tower are openings that face the direction from which the wind usually blows. The tower captures the moving air and directs it downwards through a vertical shaft, which is divided inside by thin walls so that air can travel down one channel while warm air escapes up another."},{"label":"C","text":"In many traditional houses, the shaft leads not directly into the living rooms but into a basement. There, the air passes over a pool of water or through a passage beneath the ground, where the surrounding soil stays cool even in summer. As the water evaporates, it absorbs heat, so the air that eventually enters the rooms is both cooler and slightly more humid."},{"label":"D","text":"The system also works on still days. Because warm air is lighter than cool air, it rises and leaves through the tower's openings, drawing fresher air in through windows at ground level. In this way the tower acts rather like a chimney, keeping air moving through the house even when there is no breeze."},{"label":"E","text":"Interest in wind-catchers has grown recently among architects who want to reduce the energy use of new buildings. Modern versions are often made of metal or plastic rather than mud brick, and some include filters to keep out dust and insects. Engineers in Qarvan are also testing towers fitted with small sensors that open and close the openings automatically according to the direction of the wind."}],"groups":[{"type":"diag","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Openings at the top are positioned to catch the prevailing [1]"],["Air descends through a [2] that is split into separate channels"],["In the basement, air moves across a [3] of water or through an underground passage"],["Evaporation takes [4] from the air"],["Cooler, slightly more humid air flows into the living rooms"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"wind","explanation":"B: openings face the direction from which the wind usually blows."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"shaft","explanation":"B: air is directed downwards through a vertical shaft divided inside by thin walls. A channel is only one part of the shaft."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"pool","explanation":"C: the air passes over a pool of water or through a passage beneath the ground."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"heat","explanation":"C: as the water evaporates, it absorbs heat."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-diag-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"diag","title":"From snowflake to glacier","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"A glacier is often described as a river of ice, but it begins as something much lighter: snow. In regions where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, each year's snowfall is buried by the next, and over time the accumulated layers undergo a remarkable transformation."},{"label":"B","text":"Freshly fallen snow consists largely of air trapped between delicate six-sided crystals. As further snow accumulates above it, the weight of the overlying layers compresses these crystals, and their fine points gradually break off or melt and refreeze. The result is a granular material resembling coarse sugar which, once it has survived at least one summer without melting, is known as firn."},{"label":"C","text":"Compression continues as burial deepens. The grains of firn are squeezed ever closer together, and the air between them is forced out or sealed into tiny bubbles. When the remaining air spaces are no longer connected, the material has become glacial ice, a dense substance that may take anything from decades to several centuries to form, depending chiefly on how quickly snow accumulates."},{"label":"D","text":"Ice alone does not make a glacier; it must also move. Once the mass of ice becomes sufficiently thick, its own weight causes the lower layers to deform and flow slowly, rather like extremely stiff toothpaste. In addition, where the base of the glacier is lubricated by meltwater, the entire body of ice may slide over the rock beneath it."},{"label":"E","text":"The bubbles sealed within glacial ice have proved unexpectedly valuable. Because each contains a sample of the atmosphere at the time it was trapped, scientists who drill cores from deep within a glacier can analyse the air of the distant past. Such work depends, however, on the ice remaining frozen, and researchers at the Arnholt Polar Institute warn that warming may destroy some records before they have been studied."}],"groups":[{"type":"diag","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["New snow: fragile crystals with a large amount of [1] between them"],["Pressure from layers above turns the crystals into a grainy substance called [2]"],["Air escapes or becomes trapped in very small [3]"],["Dense glacial ice forms once the air spaces are no longer linked"],["Thick ice flows under its own weight and may slide where [4] lubricates its base"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"air","explanation":"B: freshly fallen snow consists largely of air trapped between delicate crystals."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"firn","explanation":"B: the granular material resembling coarse sugar is known as firn once it has survived a summer."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"bubbles","explanation":"C: the air between the grains is forced out or sealed into tiny bubbles."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"meltwater","explanation":"D: where the base of the glacier is lubricated by meltwater, the ice may slide over the rock."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-diag-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"diag","title":"Every drop counts: water recovery at Kessler Station","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"At Kessler Station, a research base on the Antarctic ice sheet, fresh water is among the most expensive resources. Every litre must either be produced by melting snow, which consumes large quantities of fuel, or delivered by aircraft during the brief summer season. Several years ago, the station installed a system that recovers most of the water used for washing so that it can be used again."},{"label":"B","text":"The system's final stage is perhaps the most surprising. Before treated water is returned to the station's storage tanks, it passes beneath ultraviolet lamps, which destroy any microorganisms that have survived the earlier steps. Engineers chose this method in preference to adding chlorine, which would have had to be flown in and which left an unpleasant taste."},{"label":"C","text":"That last step, however, would achieve little if the water still contained solid matter, which shields microorganisms from the light. For this reason, the process begins with screening: water from sinks and showers drains into a collection tank, where a coarse screen removes hair, fibres and other debris. The water is then pumped into a bioreactor, a sealed chamber in which bacteria feed on dissolved soap and organic waste. Oxygen is supplied continuously, since these bacteria cannot survive without it."},{"label":"D","text":"After roughly a day in the bioreactor, the water is forced at high pressure through a membrane whose pores are small enough to block not only remaining particles but also most dissolved salts. The membrane is the most delicate component of the system: if the bioreactor fails, fats reach it and clog the pores within hours, and replacement parts can take months to arrive."},{"label":"E","text":"The station's managers remain cautious. Recovered water is used for washing and laundry but not yet for drinking, partly because some residents feel uneasy about the idea. The operators record water quality daily and hope that a year of reliable results will persuade the station's medical officer to approve wider use."}],"groups":[{"type":"diag","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"flowchart","rows":[["Waste water from sinks and showers collects in a tank, where a coarse [1] takes out solid debris"],["In a sealed chamber, bacteria break down soap and organic waste; they need a constant supply of [2]"],["High pressure pushes the water through a [3] that holds back particles and most salts"],["[4] lamps destroy any remaining microorganisms"],["Water returns to storage tanks for washing and laundry"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"screen","explanation":"C: the process begins with screening; a coarse screen removes hair, fibres and other debris."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"oxygen","explanation":"C: oxygen is supplied continuously, since these bacteria cannot survive without it. Soap is what the bacteria feed on, not what is supplied to them."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"membrane","explanation":"D: the water is forced at high pressure through a membrane that blocks particles and most dissolved salts."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"ultraviolet","explanation":"B: the final stage, described first in the passage, uses ultraviolet lamps. Chlorine was rejected."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq","title":"Catching water from fog","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Along parts of the dry Corran coast, rain may fall only once every few years, yet on many mornings the hills are covered in thick fog blown in from the ocean. For generations, the people of the village of Aldera watched this moisture pass them by while they carried drinking water up from a well more than an hour's walk away. Today, rows of large mesh nets stand on the ridge above the village, quietly turning fog into water."},{"label":"B","text":"The idea is simple. When the wind pushes fog through a fine plastic mesh, tiny droplets stick to the threads. As more droplets collect, they join together, become heavy and run down into a gutter at the bottom of the net. From there, pipes carry the water downhill to a storage tank in the village. No pumps or electricity are needed."},{"label":"C","text":"The nets do not work everywhere. They need fog that is frequent and thick, a steady wind to move it, and high ground close to where people live. The engineer Marta Iglesio, who designed the Aldera system, spent almost a year measuring fog on different ridges before choosing the site. 'The wrong hill can produce almost nothing,' she says."},{"label":"D","text":"The water has changed daily life. Children who once spent their mornings fetching water now arrive at school on time, and several families have started small vegetable gardens. The biggest challenge is maintenance: strong winds can tear the mesh, so the village has trained a group of young residents to inspect and repair the nets every week."},{"label":"E","text":"Similar projects have now begun in other dry coastal regions. Iglesio warns, however, that fog nets should be seen as an addition to other water sources, not a replacement for them."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"q":"Before the nets were installed, the people of Aldera got their drinking water from","options":["A rain collected on their roofs.","B a river near the village.","C water delivered by truck.","D a well some distance away."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph A: they 'carried drinking water up from a well more than an hour's walk away'."},{"q":"What makes the water run into the gutter at the bottom of the net?","options":["A the wind blowing it downwards","B a small electric pump","C droplets joining and becoming heavy","D the slope of the ridge"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph B: droplets 'join together, become heavy and run down into a gutter'. No pumps or electricity are needed."},{"q":"Why did Iglesio spend almost a year studying different ridges?","options":["A to find a place where the nets would collect enough water","B to persuade the villagers to accept the project","C to test different kinds of mesh","D to measure how much rain fell in the area"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph C: the nets only work with frequent fog, steady wind and nearby high ground; 'The wrong hill can produce almost nothing.'"},{"q":"According to paragraph D, the main difficulty with the system is","options":["A the cost of buying new nets.","B keeping the nets in good condition.","C the quality of the water.","D finding young people to train."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph D: 'The biggest challenge is maintenance': wind can tear the mesh. Training young residents is the solution, not the problem."},{"q":"What is Iglesio's view of fog nets?","options":["A They can work in any dry region.","B They should replace wells and other supplies.","C They are too expensive for most villages.","D They should be used together with other water sources."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph E: fog nets are 'an addition to other water sources, not a replacement for them'."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq","title":"A second chance for Teshari","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"When the linguist Dafydd Arnell first visited the Orlan valley in the 1970s, he estimated that fewer than two hundred people still spoke Teshari as their first language, almost all of them over sixty. Younger residents understood a few phrases but used the national language at school, at work and increasingly at home. Arnell recorded hours of conversations, songs and stories, expecting that his tapes would soon be the only trace of the language left."},{"label":"B","text":"His prediction proved wrong. In the 1990s a group of parents, worried that their children were losing touch with their grandparents' culture, opened a small nursery in which staff spoke only Teshari. It had no official support and was run from a church hall, but demand grew quickly. Within a decade the regional government had agreed to fund Teshari-medium primary classes, and a secondary stream followed."},{"label":"C","text":"The revival has relied heavily on Arnell's recordings. Because few adults remained who could teach the language, teachers used the tapes to learn pronunciation and vocabulary themselves, often staying only a step ahead of their pupils. This has had an unexpected consequence: the Teshari taught in schools today is closer to the speech of the 1970s elders than to any variety used in the years since, and some older speakers say it sounds slightly old-fashioned."},{"label":"D","text":"Success in the classroom has not automatically led to use outside it. Researchers at the regional university found that many students who speak Teshari fluently in lessons switch to the national language with their friends, partly because popular music, games and social media are rarely available in Teshari. Community groups have responded by producing podcasts and translating video games, with mixed results."},{"label":"E","text":"Even so, the number of Teshari speakers has grown for the first time in a century. Arnell, now retired, describes the outcome as 'not a rescue, but a second chance'."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"q":"What did Arnell expect when he made his recordings?","options":["A that they would be useful to teachers","B that Teshari would soon stop being spoken","C that young people would become interested in them","D that the government would pay for Teshari classes"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph A: he expected his tapes 'would soon be the only trace of the language left'."},{"q":"The first Teshari nursery was","options":["A set up by the regional government.","B started by Arnell.","C part of a primary school.","D opened by parents without official help."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph B: a group of parents opened it; it 'had no official support' and was run from a church hall. Government funding came later."},{"q":"According to paragraph C, the Teshari taught in schools today","options":["A has been strongly influenced by the national language.","B is based on the speech of young people.","C is similar to the way people spoke in the 1970s.","D is preferred by older speakers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph C: it 'is closer to the speech of the 1970s elders'. Older speakers find it old-fashioned, so option D is wrong."},{"q":"Why do many students speak the national language with their friends?","options":["A Little popular entertainment exists in Teshari.","B Their Teshari is not yet fluent.","C Their teachers do not encourage them.","D Their friends cannot understand Teshari."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph D: partly because 'popular music, games and social media are rarely available in Teshari'. The students are described as fluent, so option B is wrong."},{"q":"What does Arnell's comment in paragraph E suggest?","options":["A The language is now completely safe.","B The revival has largely failed.","C Teshari has an opportunity to survive, but its future is not certain.","D His recordings were the only reason for the revival."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph E: 'not a rescue, but a second chance' means the language has a new opportunity, not a guaranteed future."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq","title":"The long road home of the Selkirk eel","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Each autumn, adult Selkirk eels leave the rivers in which they have spent as long as twenty years and swim thousands of kilometres to a single spawning area in the open ocean. Their young then drift back towards the coast on ocean currents, a journey that may take more than a year. How fish that left the spawning area as tiny larvae manage to find their way back to it decades later has puzzled biologists for over a century."},{"label":"B","text":"Early explanations focused on the sun and stars, but eels migrate at depths of several hundred metres, where little light penetrates, and much of their swimming takes place at night. Attention therefore shifted to the Earth's magnetic field. In experiments at the Ostervik marine station, young eels were placed in tanks surrounded by electric coils that reproduced the magnetic conditions of different points along their migration route. The eels consistently turned in the direction that, in the open sea, would have carried them into the currents leading to the coast."},{"label":"C","text":"The finding suggests that eels inherit a kind of 'magnetic map' rather than learning the route. Yet the researchers, led by Hanne Solberg, caution against assuming this is the whole explanation. Magnetic information can indicate roughly where an animal is, but it is too imprecise, Solberg argues, to guide an eel to a small spawning ground on its own. She proposes that eels switch to other cues, possibly the smell or temperature of particular bodies of water, once they are close."},{"label":"D","text":"There are also practical reasons to settle the question. Numbers of Selkirk eels have fallen sharply, and although overfishing and river dams are thought to be the main causes, some scientists have asked whether human-made magnetic disturbances, such as undersea power cables, might be interfering with navigation. Solberg considers this possible but unproven; the cables studied so far produce fields that weaken rapidly with distance."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"q":"What has puzzled biologists about Selkirk eels?","options":["A how they survive in rivers for so long","B how adults return to the place where they hatched","C why the young take so long to reach the coast","D why they all spawn in a single area"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph A: the puzzle is how fish that left the spawning area as larvae 'find their way back to it decades later'."},{"q":"Why did researchers stop concentrating on the sun and stars?","options":["A Eels travel where and when there is very little light.","B Eels swim too fast to follow them.","C Eels have poor eyesight.","D The position of the stars changes during the year."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph B: eels migrate at depths 'where little light penetrates' and largely 'at night'."},{"q":"In the Ostervik experiments, the young eels","options":["A swam towards the electric coils.","B became confused by the changing conditions.","C oriented themselves in a way that would lead them towards the coast.","D learned the correct direction after several attempts."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph B: they 'consistently turned in the direction' that would carry them 'into the currents leading to the coast'."},{"q":"What does Solberg believe about the eels' use of magnetic information?","options":["A Eels do not actually rely on it.","B It is precise enough to lead eels to their spawning ground.","C Eels learn to use it while living in rivers.","D It is probably replaced by other signals near the end of the journey."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph C: it is 'too imprecise' on its own, and eels may 'switch to other cues' once they are close."},{"q":"What is Solberg's view of the possible effect of undersea cables?","options":["A It is the main cause of the fall in eel numbers.","B It has been shown not to exist.","C It cannot be ruled out, but there is no evidence for it yet.","D It affects eels only near the coast."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph D: she 'considers this possible but unproven'. Overfishing and dams are thought to be the main causes."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq","title":"The power of the default","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Standard economic theory assumes that people choose whatever best serves their interests, and that the way options are presented should therefore make little difference. A considerable body of research suggests otherwise. Among the most robust findings is the power of the default: the option that takes effect if a person does nothing. When employers in one national study stopped asking new staff to opt in to a workplace pension and instead enrolled them automatically unless they opted out, participation rose from a minority of workers to the overwhelming majority, even though the financial terms were identical."},{"label":"B","text":"Several mechanisms have been proposed. The simplest is inertia: completing a form requires effort, and effort is easily postponed. A second is that defaults are read as implicit recommendations, so that employees assume the option chosen by their employer was selected with their interests in mind. A third concerns the tendency for losses to loom larger than equivalent gains; once enrolled, workers may regard leaving the scheme as giving something up. The economist Priya Nandakumar maintains that these explanations are complementary rather than competing, and that their relative weight varies with the decision at hand."},{"label":"C","text":"The success of automatic enrolment has encouraged governments to apply defaults elsewhere, from organ donation registers to energy tariffs. Nandakumar, however, warns that defaults are a blunt instrument. Because they work precisely by sparing people the need to think, they leave those for whom the default is unsuitable no better informed. In the pension case, many enrolled workers remained for years at the initial contribution rate, which had been set deliberately low to discourage opting out, long after they could have afforded to save more. The policy thus increased the number of savers while, in some cases, reducing what individuals might otherwise have chosen to put aside."},{"label":"D","text":"Her remedy is not to abandon defaults but to design them dynamically: contributions that rise automatically with each pay increase, for instance, or periodic prompts asking people to confirm or revise their choices. Critics object that such measures are paternalistic, substituting the judgement of officials for that of citizens. Nandakumar replies that there is no neutral alternative. Every form must present some option as the starting point, and to pretend otherwise is merely to make that choice carelessly."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"q":"The pension study described in paragraph A showed that","options":["A workers choose schemes that offer better financial terms.","B the way a choice is presented can change behaviour even when the outcomes are the same.","C most workers had already joined a pension scheme before the change.","D standard economic theory predicts savings behaviour accurately."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph A: participation rose sharply 'even though the financial terms were identical', contrary to standard theory."},{"q":"According to Nandakumar, the proposed explanations for the default effect","options":["A cannot all be correct at the same time.","B apply equally to every kind of decision.","C may all operate, with their importance depending on the situation.","D have not yet been tested in real workplaces."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph B: they are 'complementary rather than competing' and 'their relative weight varies with the decision at hand'."},{"q":"The initial contribution rate in the pension scheme was","options":["A kept low so that fewer workers would leave the scheme.","B increased automatically every year.","C chosen by each worker individually.","D higher than many workers could afford."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph C: it 'had been set deliberately low to discourage opting out'. Automatic increases are Nandakumar's later proposal, not the original design."},{"q":"Which of the following best summarises Nandakumar's concern in paragraph C?","options":["A Defaults do not increase the number of people taking part.","B Defaults may lead some people to save less than they otherwise would.","C Defaults are too complicated for most people to understand.","D Defaults should not be used for organ donation."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph C: the policy increased savers 'while, in some cases, reducing what individuals might otherwise have chosen to put aside'."},{"q":"How does Nandakumar respond to the claim that her proposals are paternalistic?","options":["A She agrees that officials should not influence people's choices.","B She argues that most citizens prefer others to decide for them.","C She suggests that saving should be made compulsory instead.","D She argues that some starting option is unavoidable, so it should be chosen with care."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D: 'there is no neutral alternative. Every form must present some option as the starting point', and pretending otherwise means choosing 'carelessly'."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq2-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq2","title":"After dark at the harbour","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Every evening at six o'clock, the car park beside the old harbour in Port Lisan begins to change. Vans arrive, folding tables are set out, strings of lights are hung between lamp posts, and by seven o'clock more than two hundred stalls are open. The Port Lisan night market started twelve years ago with just fifteen traders, most of them selling cooked food. Today it also offers clothes, handmade jewellery, second-hand books and phone accessories."},{"label":"B","text":"Food is still the main attraction. Visitors can try dishes from more than twenty countries, reflecting the city's mix of communities, and prices are generally lower than in restaurants. Many traders started here because renting a stall costs far less than opening a shop. Lin Bao, who sells dumplings made to a family recipe, saved enough in four years to open her own small cafe nearby, although she still runs her stall at the market on Saturdays."},{"label":"C","text":"The market has also changed the area around the harbour. Before it opened, the streets were quiet and poorly lit after dark, and many residents avoided them. Now families stroll along the waterfront late into the evening, and several new businesses, including a bicycle hire shop, have opened in buildings that had stood empty for years."},{"label":"D","text":"Not everyone is happy, however. Some people living in nearby flats complain about noise and litter, especially on busy weekends. In response, the organisers have introduced a ten o'clock closing time on weeknights and now pay a cleaning team to clear the site every night. Most residents say the situation has improved, and the city council has recently agreed to let the market continue for another five years."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq2","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO statements about the market's traders are true?","options":["A Most of the first traders sold cooked food.","B Traders need experience of working in a restaurant.","C A stall is much cheaper to rent than a shop.","D Lin Bao no longer works at the market.","E The traders come from more than twenty countries."],"answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Paragraph A: the first fifteen traders were 'most of them selling cooked food'. Paragraph B: 'renting a stall costs far less than opening a shop'. Lin Bao still runs her stall on Saturdays (option D is wrong); the twenty countries refer to dishes, not traders (option E is wrong)."},{"q":"Which TWO changes to the harbour area have resulted from the market?","options":["A More people now walk beside the water in the evening.","B Rents for local shops have risen.","C New street lights have been put up.","D Empty buildings are now being used by businesses.","E The car park has been made larger."],"answer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Paragraph C: 'families stroll along the waterfront late into the evening' and new businesses 'have opened in buildings that had stood empty'. The streets were poorly lit before, but new lights are never mentioned (option C is wrong)."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq2-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq2","title":"Mangroves: the living sea wall","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For much of the twentieth century, mangrove forests were regarded as unproductive swamps, and along many tropical coasts they were cleared to make way for fish ponds, farmland and holiday resorts. Attitudes have since shifted considerably. Mangroves are now valued for a range of services that are difficult or costly to replace, and coastal protection is chief among them."},{"label":"B","text":"The trees' tangled roots, which rise above the mud like stilts, act as a barrier to incoming water. As waves pass through a band of mangroves they lose energy, so that their height at the landward edge may be a fraction of what it was at the seaward edge. The roots also trap sediment carried by tides and rivers, allowing some mangrove forests to build up the ground beneath them and, in favourable conditions, keep pace with gradually rising sea levels. A concrete sea wall, by contrast, cannot grow, and must be raised at great expense."},{"label":"C","text":"Mangroves also support fisheries. Their roots shelter young fish and shellfish, many of which later move to offshore waters where they are caught commercially. Communities in the Teluk Rasa region, where a large replanting scheme began in the 2000s, report bigger catches of crab since the forests returned, although researchers caution that new fishing rules introduced at the same time may also have contributed."},{"label":"D","text":"Replanting is not always successful. Early schemes often placed seedlings where mangroves had never grown naturally, such as mudflats exposed to strong waves, and most of the young trees died. The ecologist Dewi Hartono argues that restoration works best when the original pattern of tides is re-established, for example by removing old pond walls, so that seeds from nearby forests can settle and grow by themselves. This approach is slower, she admits, but considerably cheaper."},{"label":"E","text":"Mangroves cannot protect every coastline, however, and in the most exposed places they may need to be combined with engineered defences."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq2","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO ways in which mangroves protect coasts are described in the passage?","options":["A Their roots weaken the force of incoming waves.","B Their leaves absorb pollution from the water.","C They reduce the number of storms that reach land.","D Their roots collect material that raises the level of the ground.","E They prevent rivers from flooding."],"answer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Paragraph B: waves passing through mangroves 'lose energy', and the roots 'trap sediment', allowing forests 'to build up the ground beneath them'."},{"q":"Which TWO points does Hartono make about mangrove restoration?","options":["A Seedlings should be planted on open mudflats.","B Restoring natural tides allows mangroves to spread without planting.","C The scheme in Teluk Rasa has been unsuccessful.","D Natural regrowth takes longer than planting.","E Planting seedlings is the cheapest method."],"answer":["B","D"],"explanation":"Paragraph D: with the original tides re-established, seeds 'can settle and grow by themselves'; this approach 'is slower, she admits, but considerably cheaper' (so option E is wrong). Early schemes failed on mudflats (option A is wrong)."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq2-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq2","title":"Two languages from the start","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Parents raising children with two languages have long been warned that doing so may cause confusion or delay. Such advice was based largely on studies conducted in the early twentieth century, which compared bilingual children with monolingual peers and found the former performing worse on vocabulary tests. Later researchers pointed out serious flaws in this work: the bilingual children frequently came from poorer families, and were often tested only in the language they knew less well."},{"label":"B","text":"More recent studies paint a different picture. When both of a bilingual child's languages are taken into account, the total number of words the child knows is generally similar to that of a monolingual child of the same age, even if the vocabulary in each separate language is smaller. Children who hear two languages from birth also reach the main milestones of speech, such as first words and first two-word combinations, at broadly the same ages."},{"label":"C","text":"The habit of mixing languages within a sentence, which some parents find worrying, is now generally seen not as a sign of confusion but as a normal and often skilful strategy. The developmental psychologist Leonie Fairbank has shown that young bilinguals adjust their mixing to the listener, using less of a language with relatives who do not speak it. 'Children are not mixing because they cannot tell the languages apart,' she says, 'but because they know exactly who understands what.'"},{"label":"D","text":"Claims that bilingualism brings broader mental advantages, such as better control of attention, have proved more contentious. Several early experiments reporting such benefits have not been successfully repeated, and Fairbank believes the effect, if it exists, is smaller than was once suggested. She stresses, however, that the case for raising children bilingually does not depend on it: the ability to communicate with family and community is valuable in itself."},{"label":"E","text":"The most reliable predictor of whether a child becomes fluent in both languages, Fairbank's research suggests, is the amount and variety of input in each. A language heard only from one parent for a short time each day is likely to fall behind, particularly once the child starts school."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq2","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO weaknesses of the early studies of bilingual children are mentioned?","options":["A The two groups of children came from different economic backgrounds.","B The vocabulary tests were too difficult for young children.","C Too few children took part in the research.","D Children were tested in the language they spoke less well.","E The researchers ignored children's first words."],"answer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Paragraph A: bilingual children 'frequently came from poorer families' and were 'often tested only in the language they knew less well'. Test difficulty and sample size are not mentioned."},{"q":"Which TWO views are expressed by Fairbank?","options":["A Mixing languages shows that a child cannot keep them separate.","B Children consider who they are talking to when they mix languages.","C The benefits of bilingualism for attention are as large as early studies claimed.","D Children should hear each language from a different parent.","E Bilingual upbringing is worthwhile even if it brings no mental advantages."],"answer":["B","E"],"explanation":"Paragraph C: bilinguals 'adjust their mixing to the listener'. Paragraph D: the case for bilingualism 'does not depend on' mental benefits. She thinks the attention effect is 'smaller than was once suggested' (option C is wrong). Option D is never recommended: paragraph E only warns that a language heard briefly from one parent may fall behind."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-mcq2-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"mcq2","title":"Viruses against superbugs","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"The rise of bacteria resistant to antibiotics has revived interest in a treatment that predates the antibiotic era by several decades. Bacteriophages, or phages, are viruses that infect bacteria and, in many cases, destroy them. Discovered in the early twentieth century, they were used against infections in a number of countries before penicillin, which was cheaper to manufacture, easier to standardise and effective against a far wider range of organisms, pushed them to the margins of medicine."},{"label":"B","text":"It is precisely this narrowness that now makes phages attractive. Whereas a broad-spectrum antibiotic kills harmful and beneficial bacteria alike, a given phage typically attacks only one species, and often only certain strains within it, leaving the rest of the body's bacterial population undisturbed. Phages also multiply at the site of infection, so that, in principle, a small initial dose can increase in number for as long as susceptible bacteria remain."},{"label":"C","text":"The same specificity, however, poses considerable practical difficulties. Before a phage can be given, the bacterium responsible must be identified and matched with a phage able to kill it, a process that may take days, during which a seriously ill patient's condition can deteriorate. Bacteria can, moreover, evolve resistance to phages just as they do to antibiotics. The microbiologist Elena Varga, whose team at the Kessler Clinic has treated patients under emergency rules, uses 'cocktails' of several phages at once to reduce this risk, and has observed that bacteria which become resistant to certain phages sometimes become more vulnerable to particular antibiotics."},{"label":"D","text":"Regulation presents a further obstacle. Medicines are normally approved as fixed products tested in large clinical trials, whereas an effective phage treatment may need to be changed for each patient, or adjusted as the bacteria evolve. Varga argues that regulators should approve the process of selecting and preparing phages rather than each individual preparation. Others counter that this would weaken the safeguards on which patients rely, and point out that the few controlled trials completed so far have produced inconsistent results."},{"label":"E","text":"Few researchers now expect phages to replace antibiotics. The more realistic prospect, Varga suggests, is that they will be used alongside them, particularly for chronic infections that have resisted conventional treatment."}],"groups":[{"type":"mcq2","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"q":"Which TWO advantages of phages over broad-spectrum antibiotics are mentioned?","options":["A They do not harm the body's useful bacteria.","B They begin to work more quickly once a patient is diagnosed.","C Their numbers can increase inside the body while target bacteria are present.","D They are cheaper and easier to produce in a standard form.","E Bacteria are unable to develop resistance to them."],"answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Paragraph B: a phage leaves 'the rest of the body's bacterial population undisturbed' and 'a small initial dose can increase in number'. Matching a phage takes days (option B is wrong), penicillin was the cheaper one (option D is wrong), and bacteria can evolve resistance to phages (option E is wrong)."},{"q":"Which TWO statements reflect Varga's views or findings?","options":["A Combining several phages can make resistance less likely.","B Each phage preparation should go through large clinical trials.","C Phages will probably replace antibiotics for long-term infections.","D Regulators should approve the method of producing treatments rather than each product.","E Bacteria that resist phages always become easier to kill with antibiotics."],"answer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Paragraph C: she uses 'cocktails of several phages at once to reduce this risk'. Paragraph D: regulators 'should approve the process' rather than 'each individual preparation'. She expects phages to be used 'alongside' antibiotics (option C is wrong), and the effect on antibiotics happens only 'sometimes' (option E is wrong)."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mend-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"mend","title":"Lakes left by melting glaciers","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"As mountain glaciers shrink in a warming climate, they often leave behind something new: lakes. Water from the melting ice collects in hollows that the glacier once filled, held back by walls of loose rock and earth that the ice pushed into place long ago. In some mountain ranges, dozens of such lakes have appeared within a single lifetime."},{"label":"B","text":"These lakes can be beautiful, and some have become popular with tourists and walkers. However, they can also be dangerous. The natural walls that hold the water are often weak, and if one gives way, perhaps after a heavy storm or a fall of ice into the lake, a huge volume of water can rush down the valley in minutes, destroying roads, bridges and villages."},{"label":"C","text":"In the high valley of Tarsang, engineers have tried to reduce this risk. They dug a channel to lower the level of the largest lake by several metres, so that the pressure on its rocky wall is smaller. The work took three summers, because heavy machinery could only reach the site when the snow had melted. They also installed sensors that measure the water level and send a warning to the villages below if it rises suddenly."},{"label":"D","text":"Local people have played an important part. Villagers were trained to understand the warning signals and to follow marked routes to higher ground, and practice evacuations are held every year. Scientists admit that the sensors cannot prevent a flood, but they believe that, together with these preparations, they can give residents enough time to escape."}],"groups":[{"type":"mend","instructions":"Complete each sentence with the correct ending, A–F. There are more endings than sentences.","options":["A reduce the force on its natural wall.","B stops floods from happening.","C fills spaces once occupied by the ice.","D gives residents a chance to reach safety.","E tourists visit it in large numbers.","F the barrier holding it back collapses."],"questions":[{"q":"Glacial lakes form when water from melting ice","answer":"C","explanation":"A: water collects in hollows that the glacier once filled."},{"q":"A lake can flood a valley suddenly if","answer":"F","explanation":"B: if one of the natural walls gives way, water rushes down the valley. E is not given as a cause."},{"q":"Engineers lowered the level of the lake in order to","answer":"A","explanation":"C: so that the pressure on its rocky wall is smaller."},{"q":"According to scientists, the warning system","answer":"D","explanation":"D: it can give residents enough time to escape. B is wrong: the sensors cannot prevent a flood."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mend-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"mend","title":"Working apart, thinking together?","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Over the past decade, many companies have allowed staff to work from home for part or all of the week. Employees generally welcome the flexibility, and firms save money on office space. A harder question is whether working apart affects a company's ability to come up with new ideas."},{"label":"B","text":"Supporters of office work point to the value of unplanned encounters. A chance conversation in a corridor or a kitchen can bring together people from different departments who would never have arranged a meeting, and such conversations are often where unexpected ideas begin. When everyone works from home, communication tends to happen only between people who already work closely together."},{"label":"C","text":"Researchers at the Halberg School of Management examined thousands of messages sent within a large engineering company before and after it moved most of its staff to remote work. They found that employees continued to communicate frequently with their own teams but exchanged far fewer messages with colleagues in other groups. Over time, the company's internal networks became increasingly divided."},{"label":"D","text":"Yet remote work has advantages for some kinds of creative effort. Developing an idea in detail, as opposed to generating it in the first place, often requires long periods of uninterrupted concentration, which a noisy office rarely provides. Several employees in the Halberg study reported that they produced their best detailed work at home."},{"label":"E","text":"Many firms are therefore experimenting with a mixed approach. Staff work from home for focused tasks but come together on fixed days for workshops and informal contact. The difficulty is ensuring that those days actually produce the encounters they are designed for, rather than being spent on video calls that could just as easily have taken place from home."}],"groups":[{"type":"mend","instructions":"Complete each sentence with the correct ending, A–G. There are more endings than sentences.","options":["A had less contact with people outside their own team.","B save companies money on office space.","C requires long periods without interruption.","D stopped communicating with their own teams.","E bring together people who would not normally meet.","F involves producing ideas for the first time.","G lead to the kind of contact they are intended to create."],"questions":[{"q":"Unplanned conversations at work are valuable because they","answer":"E","explanation":"B: chance conversations bring together people from different departments who would never have arranged a meeting. B is true of home working, not of conversations."},{"q":"The Halberg researchers found that after the move to remote work, employees","answer":"A","explanation":"C: far fewer messages with colleagues in other groups. D is wrong: they still communicated frequently with their own teams."},{"q":"Working from home may particularly suit the stage of creative work which","answer":"C","explanation":"D: developing an idea in detail requires long periods of uninterrupted concentration. F describes generating ideas, which is contrasted."},{"q":"The challenge for firms using a mixed approach is to make sure that office days","answer":"G","explanation":"E: ensuring those days actually produce the encounters they are designed for."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mend-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"mend","title":"Light in the deep","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In the deepest parts of the ocean, no sunlight penetrates. Yet the deep sea is far from completely dark. A remarkable proportion of the animals living there can produce their own light, a phenomenon known as bioluminescence. The light is created by a chemical reaction inside the animal's body, in which one substance combines with oxygen and releases energy as a glow, usually blue or green, the colours that travel furthest through seawater."},{"label":"B","text":"Producing light costs energy, so it must bring important benefits. One of the most common is defence. Some shrimps, when attacked, release a cloud of glowing liquid into the water, which distracts the predator while the shrimp escapes into the darkness. Certain squid do something subtler: they produce a faint glow on their undersides which matches the light filtering down from above, so that a predator looking up from below cannot make out their shape."},{"label":"C","text":"Light can also be used to hunt. The anglerfish dangles a glowing lure in front of its mouth, attracting smaller fish that mistake it for food. Other predators use light more like a torch. One species of dragonfish produces red light, which most deep-sea animals are unable to see, allowing it to illuminate its prey without being noticed."},{"label":"D","text":"A third function is communication. Biologist Naomi Ferris, studying a small glowing fish in the Corran Trench, found that males and females flash in distinct patterns, which appear to help them find partners of their own species in the vast darkness. Because such patterns differ even between closely related species, Ferris believes they may have helped new species to form."},{"label":"E","text":"Studying these animals is extremely difficult. Many are fragile and die when brought to the surface, and the bright lights of research vehicles can disturb their behaviour. Ferris's team now uses cameras that record in very low light, which have revealed flashing behaviour that earlier expeditions, relying on powerful floodlights, had entirely missed."}],"groups":[{"type":"mend","instructions":"Complete each sentence with the correct ending, A–G. There are more endings than sentences.","options":["A draws prey towards the predator's mouth.","B can be seen from furthest away in the sea.","C used lighting that was too powerful.","D confuses attackers while the animal escapes.","E is not visible to most of the animals it hunts.","F brought the animals to the surface too quickly.","G conceals their outline against the light from above."],"questions":[{"q":"Deep-sea animals mostly produce blue or green light because these colours","answer":"B","explanation":"A: blue or green, the colours that travel furthest through seawater."},{"q":"Some squid are hard for predators below them to detect because their glow","answer":"G","explanation":"B: the glow matches the light from above, so a predator cannot make out their shape. D describes the shrimps' glowing liquid, not the squid."},{"q":"The dragonfish's red light is useful because it","answer":"E","explanation":"C: most deep-sea animals are unable to see red light. A describes the anglerfish."},{"q":"Earlier expeditions missed some flashing behaviour because they","answer":"C","explanation":"E: earlier expeditions relied on powerful floodlights. F is a trap: animals die at the surface, but that is not why the flashing was missed."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mend-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"mend","title":"Do algorithms narrow our taste?","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Much of what people now watch, read and listen to is chosen for them, at least in part, by recommendation systems. Streaming services, online shops and news platforms track what each user clicks, finishes or skips, and use these patterns to predict what they will want next. The systems are undeniably convenient. What remains disputed is their long-term effect on cultural taste."},{"label":"B","text":"The most common worry is that recommendations create a narrowing spiral. A listener who plays a few folk songs is offered more folk music; the more of it they play, the more confidently the system concludes that this is what they like, and the less often it suggests anything else. Critics argue that, over years, such feedback could leave people with tastes that are not so much chosen as gradually reinforced."},{"label":"C","text":"Evidence for this effect, however, is mixed. Media researcher Jonas Petrak analysed the listening histories of volunteers on a music service, Tuneway, over three years. Heavy users of the recommendation feature did, on average, listen to a smaller range of genres. But Petrak found that much of this difference existed before they started using the feature: people who already had narrow tastes were simply more inclined to rely on it."},{"label":"D","text":"Other studies point in the opposite direction. For many users, especially those without friends who share their interests, recommendations are the main way they discover unfamiliar material. A reader in a small town with no bookshop, for instance, may encounter far more varied fiction through an online store than they ever could locally. In such cases, the system broadens experience rather than narrowing it."},{"label":"E","text":"The design of the system matters a great deal. Some services deliberately include a proportion of unexpected suggestions, trading a little short-term accuracy for the chance of introducing users to something new. Petrak notes, though, that companies have little commercial reason to do this, since a user who dislikes a surprising recommendation may leave the platform, while one who receives familiar suggestions rarely complains."},{"label":"F","text":"What is lost may be less individual variety than shared experience. When everyone received the same small selection of films or songs through a limited number of broadcasters, those works became common reference points that people could discuss. If each person's cultural diet is now tailored separately, societies may find that they have fewer such shared touchstones, even if each individual's tastes remain as broad as before."}],"groups":[{"type":"mend","instructions":"Complete each sentence with the correct ending, A–H. There are more endings than sentences.","options":["A rarely complain about them.","B largely reflected differences that were already present.","C have few other ways of finding unfamiliar material.","D showed that the feature itself narrows people's taste.","E may abandon the platform altogether.","F already have a limited range of interests.","G are reinforced over time rather than genuinely chosen.","H share fewer cultural reference points with others."],"questions":[{"q":"Critics fear that, over time, recommendation systems may leave people with preferences that","answer":"G","explanation":"B: tastes that are not so much chosen as gradually reinforced."},{"q":"Petrak's research suggested that the link between using recommendations and having narrow tastes","answer":"B","explanation":"C: much of the difference existed before they started using the feature. D is the opposite of his finding."},{"q":"Recommendations are especially likely to widen the experience of people who","answer":"C","explanation":"D: those without friends who share their interests, or with no local bookshop, rely on recommendations to discover unfamiliar material. F describes the heavy users in Petrak's study."},{"q":"Firms may avoid offering unexpected suggestions because users who are disappointed by them","answer":"E","explanation":"E: a user who dislikes a surprising recommendation may leave the platform. A applies to users who receive familiar suggestions."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mfeat-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"mfeat","title":"City birds change their tune","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Anyone who has walked through a busy city at dawn will have heard birds singing above the noise of the traffic. Over the past twenty years, scientists have begun to ask whether city birds sing differently from members of the same species living in the countryside, and if so, why. Three researchers have looked at the question from quite different angles."},{"label":"B","text":"Dr Hannah Leeds recorded robins in a large city and on nearby farmland. She found that the city birds sang at a noticeably higher pitch. Low sounds, she explains, are easily covered by the rumble of engines, so a higher song is more likely to be heard by other birds. In quiet parks within the city, the difference was much smaller."},{"label":"C","text":"Professor Karim Osei took a different approach. Instead of measuring pitch, he looked at timing. His recordings showed that many city birds now begin singing earlier in the morning, before the roads become busy, and some even sing at night near bright street lamps. Birds near the brightest lamps started earliest of all. He believes that artificial light confuses the birds' sense of when the day begins."},{"label":"D","text":"A third researcher, Dr Sofia Marin, was interested in what these changes mean for the birds themselves. In her study, female birds in the city preferred males with louder, higher songs, while females in the countryside showed no such preference. Marin warns that if city and country birds continue to sing differently, they may one day fail to recognise each other's songs, and the two populations could slowly become separate."}],"groups":[{"type":"mfeat","instructions":"Look at the following statements and the list of researchers below. Match each statement with the correct researcher, A, B or C. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Hannah Leeds","B Karim Osei","C Sofia Marin"],"questions":[{"q":"Birds may be affected by the lighting in towns.","answer":"B","explanation":"Osei: artificial light confuses the birds' sense of when the day begins."},{"q":"Noise from vehicles can make certain sounds hard to hear.","answer":"A","explanation":"Leeds: low sounds are easily covered by the rumble of engines."},{"q":"Differences in song might eventually split one group of birds into two.","answer":"C","explanation":"Marin: the two populations could slowly become separate."},{"q":"Some birds avoid competing with traffic by changing the time when they sing.","answer":"B","explanation":"Osei: city birds begin singing earlier, before the roads become busy."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mfeat-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"mfeat","title":"Four days in the classroom","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In a number of rural school districts, the traditional five-day school week has been replaced by four longer days, with students staying at home or attending optional activities on the fifth. The change is usually driven by money: running buses and heating buildings for one day less can save a district a considerable sum. Its effects on learning, however, have been the subject of fierce debate."},{"label":"B","text":"Economist Paul Hendry studied districts in the Ruskin Valley that had switched to the shorter week. He found that the savings were real but smaller than administrators had promised, because many costs, such as teachers' salaries, did not change. More surprisingly, he discovered that these districts found it easier to fill vacant teaching posts, since applicants valued the extra free day highly."},{"label":"C","text":"Education researcher Amira Saleh focused on achievement. Comparing test scores before and after the change, she found that younger children's reading results fell slightly, while those of older students stayed largely the same. Saleh argues that young children struggle to concentrate during long school days, so the extra minutes added to each day do not fully make up for the day that has been lost."},{"label":"D","text":"Sociologist Leon Fischer examined family life. Parents with flexible jobs welcomed the arrangement, using the free day for medical appointments and family activities. For parents in shift work, however, the fifth day created a childcare problem, and some had to pay for supervision they had not needed before. Fischer warns that a policy designed to save public money may simply move those costs onto households."},{"label":"E","text":"All three researchers agree that the four-day week is neither a disaster nor a miracle. Its effects depend heavily on how the fifth day is used, and Saleh in particular believes that districts which offer tutoring on that day can avoid the decline in results that she observed."}],"groups":[{"type":"mfeat","instructions":"Look at the following statements and the list of researchers below. Match each statement with the correct researcher, A, B or C. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Paul Hendry","B Amira Saleh","C Leon Fischer"],"questions":[{"q":"The shorter week may help schools to recruit staff.","answer":"A","explanation":"Hendry: districts found it easier to fill vacant teaching posts."},{"q":"Money saved by schools could become an expense for parents.","answer":"C","explanation":"Fischer: the policy may move costs onto households, e.g. paying for supervision."},{"q":"For younger pupils, longer school days are not an adequate replacement for the day removed.","answer":"B","explanation":"Saleh: the extra minutes do not fully make up for the day that has been lost."},{"q":"Providing extra teaching on the free day can prevent a fall in achievement.","answer":"B","explanation":"Saleh: districts that offer tutoring on that day can avoid the decline in results. All three agree only that effects depend on the fifth day."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mfeat-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"mfeat","title":"The puzzle of the placebo","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"A placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill, given in place of a real medicine. For decades, placebos were used mainly as a tool in drug trials: a new medicine was considered effective only if it worked better than a placebo. The fact that many patients felt better after taking one was regarded as a nuisance, something to be subtracted from the results rather than studied in its own right."},{"label":"B","text":"Neuroscientist Dr Elena Vasquez was among the first to treat the effect as worth investigating. Using brain scans, she showed that when patients with long-term pain took a pill they believed to be a painkiller, areas of the brain involved in the body's own pain-relief system became more active. The improvement they reported, she concluded, was not imaginary; their brains were genuinely producing a response."},{"label":"C","text":"A research group led by Professor Daniel Ahn at the Wellmont Institute focused instead on the setting in which treatment is given. In one experiment, patients received identical placebo pills, but half were seen by a doctor who was warm, attentive and unhurried, while the others saw a doctor who was brisk and distracted. The first group reported noticeably greater relief. Ahn argues that the relationship between patient and doctor is itself a form of treatment, and one that busy health systems tend to neglect."},{"label":"D","text":"Perhaps the most surprising work comes from a team under Dr Miriam Kohl, which tested so-called 'open-label' placebos. Patients were told plainly that their pills contained no medicine at all, yet many still reported less pain than those who received nothing. Kohl suggests that the ritual of taking a pill at regular times may trigger learned expectations of improvement, even when the patient knows the truth."},{"label":"E","text":"None of these researchers claims that placebos can cure disease. Their effects are strongest for symptoms such as pain, tiredness and nausea, which are shaped partly by the brain, and weakest for conditions such as infections. Kohl in particular cautions against offering placebos instead of proven treatments. What the research does suggest is that the experience of being cared for may matter more to recovery than medicine has traditionally recognised."}],"groups":[{"type":"mfeat","instructions":"Look at the following statements and the list of researchers below. Match each statement with the correct researcher or research group, A, B or C. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Elena Vasquez","B Daniel Ahn's group","C Miriam Kohl's team"],"questions":[{"q":"Patients can benefit from a fake treatment even when they have not been misled about it.","answer":"C","explanation":"Kohl: patients were told their pills contained no medicine, yet many still reported less pain."},{"q":"The manner of the person giving treatment affects the degree of relief that patients experience.","answer":"B","explanation":"Ahn: a warm, unhurried doctor produced greater relief with identical pills."},{"q":"Placebos should not be used as a substitute for treatments known to be effective.","answer":"C","explanation":"Kohl cautions against offering placebos instead of proven treatments. The claim that placebos cannot cure disease is shared by all three."},{"q":"The relief patients describe has a measurable basis in the body.","answer":"A","explanation":"Vasquez: brain scans showed the pain-relief system became more active; the improvement was not imaginary."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mfeat-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"mfeat","title":"Before coins","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"The familiar story of how money began goes something like this. In early societies people bartered, swapping grain for cloth or pots for tools. Because it was hard to find someone who both had what you wanted and wanted what you had, people eventually settled on a single commodity, such as silver, that everyone would accept. Coins followed, and later paper money and banks. The story is tidy and intuitive. According to a number of researchers, it may also be largely wrong."},{"label":"B","text":"The anthropologist Grace Mbeki spent several years studying exchange in farming villages in the Oruna highlands, communities that used little cash. She found almost no barter between neighbours. Instead, people gave goods and help freely and kept careful mental track of who owed what to whom. Debts might remain unpaid for years, settled eventually through a gift, a marriage arrangement or labour at harvest time. In Mbeki's view, direct swapping happens mainly with outsiders, who have no reason to trust one another, rather than within a community."},{"label":"C","text":"The historian Victor Lam reached a similar conclusion from a very different source: clay tablets from ancient temple archives. These records show that officials calculated debts in units of silver long before silver commonly changed hands. A farmer might owe the temple a quantity of barley 'worth' a certain weight of silver, but pay in barley. Silver, Lam argues, began as a way of keeping accounts and only later became something people physically exchanged."},{"label":"D","text":"Not everyone accepts this revised account. The economist Ruth Albescu points out that the absence of barter from written records does not prove it never happened, since everyday trades between ordinary people were rarely written down at all. She also notes that Lam's tablets come from large institutions, which had the staff to keep detailed accounts; small traders may have behaved quite differently."},{"label":"E","text":"Albescu does accept that credit, in some form, is far older than coins. Her own work on early coinage, moreover, suggests that the first coins, which appeared much later than the temple tablets, were introduced by rulers to pay soldiers and collect taxes, not by merchants looking for a more convenient way to trade."},{"label":"F","text":"The debate matters beyond the history books. If money grew out of credit and social relationships rather than out of barter, then it is, at heart, a system of promises that depends on trust. That idea has become central to modern discussions about what gives a currency its value, and about what happens when public confidence in it collapses."}],"groups":[{"type":"mfeat","instructions":"Look at the following statements and the list of researchers below. Match each statement with the correct researcher, A, B or C. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A Grace Mbeki","B Victor Lam","C Ruth Albescu"],"questions":[{"q":"Exchanging goods directly is more typical of dealings between people who are unfamiliar with each other.","answer":"A","explanation":"Mbeki: direct swapping happens mainly with outsiders, who have no reason to trust one another."},{"q":"Surviving documents may be unrepresentative because they were produced by a particular type of organisation.","answer":"C","explanation":"Albescu: Lam's tablets come from large institutions; small traders may have behaved differently. Lam uses the tablets but does not question them."},{"q":"A precious metal served as a means of recording obligations before it was used for payment.","answer":"B","explanation":"Lam: silver began as a way of keeping accounts and only later was physically exchanged."},{"q":"The earliest coins were created to meet the needs of those in power rather than those of traders.","answer":"C","explanation":"Albescu's own work: rulers introduced coins to pay soldiers and collect taxes, not merchants. Lam is mentioned nearby but this is not his claim."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mhead-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"mhead","title":"The changing role of the public library","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In the town of Ellsby, as in many places, the public library was once a quiet building full of books, where visitors were expected to whisper. For most of the twentieth century its job was simple: to lend books and to provide a calm place for reading and study. Its shelves were arranged by subject, its rooms were silent, and its staff spent most of their time stamping books and returning them to their places. Few people imagined that it would ever need to be anything else."},{"label":"B","text":"In the early 2000s, however, the number of books borrowed began to fall. More people bought cheap books online or read on screens, and some councils questioned whether libraries were still worth the money they cost. Several small branches in the region closed, and Ellsby's own library had its opening hours reduced. For a time, its future looked uncertain."},{"label":"C","text":"Rather than accept decline, the library's staff decided to change what it offered. They added computers with free internet access, ran classes to help older residents use email and online banking, and opened a small room where job seekers could print documents and practise for interviews. A children's corner hosted story sessions every weekday morning, and local groups were invited to use the meeting rooms at no cost."},{"label":"D","text":"Today the building is busier than it was twenty years ago, even though fewer books are borrowed. Visitors come to meet friends, attend events or simply sit somewhere warm and free of charge. The head librarian describes it as 'the town's living room', a place that belongs to everyone who lives there."}],"groups":[{"type":"mhead","instructions":"The reading passage has four paragraphs, A–D. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. Write the correct number, i–vi.","options":["i A popular meeting place for the whole town","ii The rising price of printed books","iii A building with a single, quiet purpose","iv New services for different groups of people","v Plans for a new library building","vi Falling demand and its consequences"],"questions":[{"q":"Paragraph A","answer":"iii","explanation":"A: the library lent books and provided a calm place for reading; that was its only job."},{"q":"Paragraph B","answer":"vi","explanation":"B: borrowing fell, branches closed and opening hours were reduced. Heading ii is wrong: books online were cheap."},{"q":"Paragraph C","answer":"iv","explanation":"C: computers, classes for older residents, help for job seekers and a children's corner."},{"q":"Paragraph D","answer":"i","explanation":"D: visitors come to meet friends; it is 'the town's living room'. Heading v is never mentioned."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mhead-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"mhead","title":"Fresh water from the sea","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Most of the planet's water lies in the oceans, far too salty to drink or to use on crops. For coastal regions where rivers are shrinking and underground supplies are running low, the idea of removing salt from seawater has an obvious attraction. The process, known as desalination, has been possible for centuries, but only recently has it become a realistic source of water for entire cities."},{"label":"B","text":"Early methods imitated nature. Just as the sun warms the sea and produces clouds of pure water vapour, seawater was heated until it turned to steam, leaving the salt behind, and the steam was then cooled to produce fresh water. This worked well, but boiling large volumes of water requires enormous amounts of fuel, so it was mainly used on ships and in places where energy was cheap."},{"label":"C","text":"The breakthrough came with a technique called reverse osmosis. Instead of being boiled, seawater is pushed at high pressure through extremely fine membranes that allow water molecules to pass but block the salt. Steady improvements in membrane design have reduced the energy needed, making the method far cheaper to run than heating."},{"label":"D","text":"Even so, desalination has an environmental cost. For every litre of fresh water produced, a plant generates a highly salty waste liquid, which is often returned to the sea. If this is released in one place, it can sink to the seabed and harm creatures that cannot tolerate sudden changes in saltiness. Plants that rely on fossil fuels also add to carbon emissions."},{"label":"E","text":"Engineers are now looking for ways to limit this damage. Some plants spread their waste through long pipes with many outlets so that it mixes quickly with seawater. Others are being built alongside solar farms. In the coastal city of Marabel, planners are even testing whether minerals extracted from the waste can be sold to industry, turning a problem into a source of income."}],"groups":[{"type":"mhead","instructions":"The reading passage has five paragraphs, A–E. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. Write the correct number, i–vii.","options":["i Unwanted results of the process","ii Why an old idea has become important","iii The high cost of drinking water in coastal cities","iv A technique that lowered energy demands","v Efforts to reduce the harm","vi How sea creatures adapt to changes in salt","vii Copying a natural process"],"questions":[{"q":"Paragraph A","answer":"ii","explanation":"A: desalination has been possible for centuries, but only recently has it become realistic for cities facing shortages."},{"q":"Paragraph B","answer":"vii","explanation":"B: early methods imitated nature, heating seawater into steam as the sun does."},{"q":"Paragraph C","answer":"iv","explanation":"C: reverse osmosis and better membranes reduced the energy needed."},{"q":"Paragraph D","answer":"i","explanation":"D: salty waste liquid that harms sea creatures, and carbon emissions. Heading vi is wrong: the creatures cannot tolerate the change."},{"q":"Paragraph E","answer":"v","explanation":"E: long pipes, solar farms and selling minerals from the waste to limit the damage."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mhead-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"mhead","title":"Bringing a language back","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"A large share of the world's languages are spoken by only a few thousand people each, and many have no young speakers at all. When the last fluent speaker of a language dies, a whole way of describing the world can disappear with them: words for local plants and weather, ways of counting, and songs and stories that were never written down."},{"label":"B","text":"For a long time, such losses were seen as an unfortunate but natural consequence of modernisation. Families who moved to cities often chose to raise their children in the national language, believing that it offered better jobs and education. In many places, schools actively discouraged children from speaking the language of their parents, and some punished them for doing so."},{"label":"C","text":"The case of Veshtan, a language of the northern hill country, shows that this pattern is not inevitable. Thirty years ago it had fewer than two hundred elderly speakers. A group of teachers began recording these speakers, compiling a dictionary and, crucially, opening nursery schools in which all activities took place in Veshtan. Children who attended those schools now use the language with each other, and some are raising their own children in it."},{"label":"D","text":"Success of this kind depends less on recordings and dictionaries than on creating situations in which the language is genuinely needed. Archives preserve a language, but they do not keep it alive. Unless people use it at home, at work and among friends, a revived language risks becoming a subject studied in classrooms rather than a means of everyday communication."},{"label":"E","text":"Technology offers new opportunities. Speakers of small languages are using social media, messaging apps and online video to reach one another across long distances, and young people have begun to create music and comedy in languages their grandparents feared were dying. Critics warn, however, that the same platforms overwhelmingly favour a handful of global languages, so their overall effect may be to speed up decline rather than reverse it."}],"groups":[{"type":"mhead","instructions":"The reading passage has five paragraphs, A–E. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. Write the correct number, i–vii.","options":["i A mixed blessing for small languages","ii The difficulty of writing down spoken languages","iii What disappears along with a language","iv Why documentation alone is not enough","v Older attitudes that hastened decline","vi Government support for minority-language schools","vii Proof that decline can be reversed"],"questions":[{"q":"Paragraph A","answer":"iii","explanation":"A: when the last speaker dies, words, ways of counting, songs and stories can disappear. Heading ii is tempting, but A only mentions that stories were never written down."},{"q":"Paragraph B","answer":"v","explanation":"B: losses were seen as natural; families and schools discouraged the parents' language."},{"q":"Paragraph C","answer":"vii","explanation":"C: Veshtan went from two hundred elderly speakers to children using it, showing the pattern is not inevitable."},{"q":"Paragraph D","answer":"iv","explanation":"D: archives preserve a language but do not keep it alive; it must be used daily. C mentions recordings, but its main idea is the success story."},{"q":"Paragraph E","answer":"i","explanation":"E: technology offers opportunities, but critics warn it may speed up decline. Heading vi is never mentioned."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-mhead-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"mhead","title":"The power of the default","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Every day people make choices without realising that a choice has already been made for them. A new phone arrives with its settings selected; an online form has certain boxes ticked in advance; an employee joining a firm is placed on a particular pension plan unless they ask to change it. These pre-set options, known as defaults, are so ordinary that they rarely attract attention. Yet a growing body of research suggests that they shape behaviour far more powerfully than most of us would admit."},{"label":"B","text":"A well-known demonstration comes from workplace savings. When the manufacturing firm Dellgate asked staff to sign up for its pension scheme, only a minority did so in their first year. After the company changed the rules so that new employees were enrolled automatically but could leave at any time, the great majority remained in the scheme. Nothing about the plan itself had changed; only the starting point had moved."},{"label":"C","text":"Why should such a small adjustment matter so much? Researchers point to several forces. Switching away from a default requires effort, however slight, and people tend to postpone tasks that offer no immediate reward. Many also interpret the default as a recommendation, assuming that whoever set it knew best. And once an option is treated as their own, people become reluctant to give it up, valuing what they already have more highly than an equivalent alternative."},{"label":"D","text":"This influence raises an obvious concern. If defaults are so effective, those who design them hold considerable power, and that power can be turned against the people it affects. Some subscription services automatically renew unless customers cancel through a deliberately complicated process, and certain websites set data-sharing permissions to the most generous level, knowing that few users will ever change them."},{"label":"E","text":"Defenders of the approach reply that there is no such thing as a neutral design. Someone must decide which option appears first, and whatever is chosen will influence behaviour. The real question, in their view, is not whether to use defaults but how to choose them responsibly: in the interests of the people affected, with the alternatives clearly visible and easy to select."},{"label":"F","text":"Some governments have begun to put this principle into law, requiring, for example, that privacy settings on new devices start at the most protective level. Whether such rules will work is still unclear. Companies may find other ways to steer users, and a default that suits most people may still be wrong for a significant minority whose circumstances are unusual."}],"groups":[{"type":"mhead","instructions":"The reading passage has six paragraphs, A–F. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. Write the correct number, i–viii.","options":["i When design serves the designer","ii No option is truly neutral","iii Same scheme, different starting point","iv Comparing the benefits of rival pension schemes","v Decisions taken on our behalf","vi Turning a principle into rules","vii Growing public resistance to pre-selected settings","viii Explaining a surprisingly strong effect"],"questions":[{"q":"Paragraph A","answer":"v","explanation":"A: phones, forms and pension plans come with choices already made for us. Heading vii is wrong: defaults rarely attract attention."},{"q":"Paragraph B","answer":"iii","explanation":"B: nothing about the plan changed; only the starting point moved. Heading iv is a distractor: only one scheme is described."},{"q":"Paragraph C","answer":"viii","explanation":"C: effort, trust in the designer and reluctance to give things up explain why a small adjustment matters so much."},{"q":"Paragraph D","answer":"i","explanation":"D: designers' power can be turned against users, e.g. hard-to-cancel subscriptions and generous data-sharing settings."},{"q":"Paragraph E","answer":"ii","explanation":"E: defenders say there is no such thing as a neutral design."},{"q":"Paragraph F","answer":"vi","explanation":"F: governments are putting the principle into law, but whether the rules will work is unclear."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-minfo-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"minfo","title":"Shared bicycles in Port Hallow","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"When the coastal city of Port Hallow launched its bicycle-sharing scheme eight years ago, few residents expected it to last. The city had steep hills, frequent rain and narrow streets crowded with cars. Yet the scheme now has more than two hundred docking stations, and the bright green bicycles have become a familiar sight everywhere from the harbour to the university."},{"label":"B","text":"The system is simple to use. Riders download an app, unlock a bicycle by scanning a code on its frame, and return it to any station when they have finished. The first thirty minutes of each journey are free for members, which encourages people to use the bicycles for short trips rather than keeping them for a whole afternoon."},{"label":"C","text":"The early years were difficult. Many bicycles were stolen or thrown into the harbour, and some stations stood empty while others overflowed. The operators responded by fitting each bicycle with a tracking device and hiring staff to move bicycles by van from full stations to empty ones every night."},{"label":"D","text":"The scheme has changed how some people travel. A survey by the city council found that many members now cycle to the railway station instead of driving there, and shops near popular stations report more customers arriving on foot. Electric bicycles, added three years ago, have made the hills far less of a barrier."},{"label":"E","text":"Not everyone is pleased. Some drivers complain that new cycle lanes have made traffic slower, and pedestrians object when bicycles are left on pavements. The council intends to build fifteen more stations next year, but only after a public consultation on where they should go."}],"groups":[{"type":"minfo","instructions":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–E. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"questions":[{"q":"a description of how a bicycle is collected and given back","answer":"B","explanation":"B: riders unlock a bicycle by scanning a code and return it to any station."},{"q":"a reference to a plan that depends on residents' views","answer":"E","explanation":"E: fifteen more stations, but only after a public consultation."},{"q":"an explanation of how the problem of theft was tackled","answer":"C","explanation":"C: bicycles were stolen, so each one was fitted with a tracking device."},{"q":"a mention of a type of vehicle that made the local landscape easier to cope with","answer":"D","explanation":"D: electric bicycles have made the hills far less of a barrier. Paragraph A mentions the hills only as a reason for doubt."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-minfo-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"minfo","title":"The jars of Kessara","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For more than a century, archaeologists working around the eastern Mediterranean have found fragments of a distinctive type of storage jar, tall and narrow with two small handles near the neck. Because so many were discovered near the ancient port of Kessara, they became known as Kessaran jars, and scholars long assumed that every one of them had been made there."},{"label":"B","text":"That assumption began to weaken when researchers started analysing the clay itself. Every deposit of clay contains a slightly different mixture of minerals, so a small sample, ground into powder and examined under a microscope, can reveal where a pot was made. When a team led by Dr Ilse Varner tested jars from twelve sites, they found that fewer than half had been made from local Kessaran clay."},{"label":"C","text":"The rest came from at least four other workshops, some of them several hundred kilometres away. These potters had copied the Kessaran shape so closely that, to the naked eye, their jars were indistinguishable from the originals. Varner suggests that the shape itself had become a kind of guarantee: merchants recognised it and trusted that a jar of that form would hold a standard quantity of oil or wine."},{"label":"D","text":"Marks on the jars support this idea. Many have a small symbol pressed into the handle before firing, and the same symbols appear on jars from different workshops. Rather than identifying an individual potter, the marks may have indicated the volume of the jar, allowing traders to check its contents without opening it."},{"label":"E","text":"The findings have changed how historians picture trade in the region. Instead of a single city exporting its products, they now see a network of producers who followed shared standards, much as modern manufacturers follow agreed sizes for shipping containers. Questions remain, however: nobody yet knows who set these standards, or how they were enforced."}],"groups":[{"type":"minfo","instructions":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–E. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"questions":[{"q":"a comparison between an ancient practice and a present-day one","answer":"E","explanation":"E: producers followed shared standards, much as modern manufacturers follow agreed sizes for shipping containers."},{"q":"a possible function of a feature added while the jars were being made","answer":"D","explanation":"D: symbols pressed into the handle before firing may have indicated the volume of the jar. C explains the shape, not an added feature."},{"q":"a description of a technique used to identify where an object came from","answer":"B","explanation":"B: clay is ground into powder and examined under a microscope to reveal where a pot was made."},{"q":"a statement of a long-held belief about where all the jars were made","answer":"A","explanation":"A: scholars long assumed that every one of them had been made in Kessara. B only refers back to 'that assumption' when describing how it was challenged."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-minfo-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"minfo","title":"Sleep and the teenage brain","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Parents have long complained that teenagers stay up late and are impossible to wake in the morning. For decades this was treated as a matter of discipline. Research over the past thirty years, however, suggests that the pattern is largely biological: during adolescence the body's internal clock shifts, so that the hormone which makes people feel sleepy is released later in the evening than it was in childhood."},{"label":"B","text":"This shift collides with the school timetable. A fifteen-year-old who cannot fall asleep before midnight but must rise at half past six will lose a substantial amount of sleep every weekday. Weekend lie-ins recover some of it, but they also push the internal clock later still, making Monday mornings even harder."},{"label":"C","text":"The cost is not only tiredness. In a series of laboratory studies, psychologist Tomas Brenn taught students lists of unfamiliar word pairs in the evening and tested them the following morning. Those allowed a full night's sleep recalled considerably more pairs than those whose sleep was cut short, even though both groups had spent the same amount of time studying. Brenn argues that sleep is when the brain transfers new information into long-term storage."},{"label":"D","text":"Some schools have tried to respond. In the district of Arlow, secondary schools moved their start time from eight o'clock to nine. Attendance at the first lesson improved, and teachers reported that students seemed more alert, although examination results changed only slightly in the first two years."},{"label":"E","text":"The change was not universally welcomed. Parents who drove their children to school found that the new timetable clashed with their own working hours, and sports clubs complained that later finishing times left less daylight for training. Bus companies, too, had to reorganise routes that were shared with primary schools."},{"label":"F","text":"Researchers caution that a later start is only part of the answer. If students simply use the extra hour to stay up later with their phones, the benefit may disappear. They recommend combining timetable changes with education about evening screen use, since the light from screens can delay sleepiness further."}],"groups":[{"type":"minfo","instructions":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–F. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"questions":[{"q":"evidence that a policy led to only modest improvements in academic performance","answer":"D","explanation":"D: after the later start, examination results changed only slightly in the first two years. C is about memory in a laboratory, not a policy."},{"q":"a warning that the benefits of a reform could be cancelled out by students' own habits","answer":"F","explanation":"F: if students use the extra hour to stay up later with their phones, the benefit may disappear."},{"q":"an account of how an attempt to make up for lost rest can make the problem worse","answer":"B","explanation":"B: weekend lie-ins recover some sleep but push the internal clock later still."},{"q":"practical difficulties that a change created for people outside the school","answer":"E","explanation":"E: parents' working hours, sports clubs and bus companies were all affected."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rb-minfo-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"minfo","title":"The hidden life beneath dry fields","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"A handful of farmland soil may contain billions of microorganisms, from bacteria and fungi to tiny single-celled animals. For most of the history of agricultural science, these organisms were regarded as background: present, certainly, but of little importance compared with the chemistry of the soil and the genetics of the crop. That view has shifted dramatically, and nowhere more so than in research on drought."},{"label":"B","text":"The turning point came from an unexpected observation. At an experimental farm in the Teral Valley, plant scientist Ruth Okonji noticed that wheat grown in one field consistently survived dry summers better than identical wheat in a neighbouring field, even though the two soils had almost the same texture and mineral content. The only clear difference was their history: the first field had never been ploughed deeply, while the second had been turned over every year for decades."},{"label":"C","text":"Okonji's team eventually traced the difference to fungi. In undisturbed soil, certain fungi form vast networks of threads that wrap around plant roots and extend far beyond them, effectively enlarging the area from which a plant can draw water. Deep ploughing tears these networks apart, and they can take several seasons to regrow."},{"label":"D","text":"Bacteria appear to play a role too, though a less visible one. Some species produce sticky substances that bind soil particles into small clumps. These clumps create tiny spaces in which water is held after rain and released slowly to roots as the surrounding soil dries. Other bacteria seem to send chemical signals that cause plants to close the pores on their leaves earlier, reducing water loss."},{"label":"E","text":"Translating these discoveries into farming practice is far from straightforward. Commercial products containing beneficial microbes are already on sale, but their results in the field have been inconsistent. Microbes that thrive in one soil may fail to establish themselves in another, where they must compete with local species that are better adapted to it. Okonji herself is cautious, describing most such products as 'a hopeful guess in a bottle'."},{"label":"F","text":"A more reliable approach, many researchers believe, is to manage soils in ways that allow existing communities to flourish: reducing ploughing, keeping the ground covered with plants between harvests, and limiting the use of certain chemicals. These methods are slower and less dramatic than adding a product, and they may reduce yields in the first few years. Yet for farmers facing longer and more frequent dry spells, they may offer the most dependable protection."}],"groups":[{"type":"minfo","instructions":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–F. NB You may use any letter more than once.","options":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"questions":[{"q":"a researcher's doubts about a ready-made solution","answer":"E","explanation":"E: Okonji describes most microbe products as 'a hopeful guess in a bottle'."},{"q":"a process by which moisture is retained and then gradually supplied to plants","answer":"D","explanation":"D: bacterial clumps create spaces where water is held after rain and released slowly to roots. C is about reaching water over a wider area, not storing it."},{"q":"a contrast between two sites that differed mainly in how they had been worked","answer":"B","explanation":"B: the soils were almost identical; one field had never been ploughed deeply, the other every year."},{"q":"a reason why introduced organisms may not survive in certain conditions","answer":"E","explanation":"E: microbes may fail to establish themselves where they must compete with better-adapted local species. E is used twice; F describes a short-term cost, not a reason for failure."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-sent-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"sent","title":"Bees on the rooftops","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Ten years ago, few residents of the city of Calder would have imagined keeping bees above their offices. Today more than two hundred hives sit on the roofs of hotels, schools and banks. The trend began when a local hotel placed four hives on its flat roof and began serving the honey at breakfast. Guests liked the idea, and other businesses soon followed."},{"label":"B","text":"Rooftops suit bees surprisingly well. They are quiet, rarely visited by people, and often warmer than the ground. City parks, gardens and tree-lined streets provide a wide variety of flowers, so urban honey can have a richer flavour than honey from farmland, where a single crop may cover many fields. Beekeepers in Calder also report that their colonies are less exposed to pesticides than those in the countryside."},{"label":"C","text":"However, the hobby is not without problems. Each hive must be checked every week during the summer, and climbing onto a roof with heavy equipment can be dangerous. Strong wind is a particular concern, so hives must be fixed firmly in place. Neighbours may also worry about being stung, although honeybees are generally calm unless their hive is disturbed."},{"label":"D","text":"Some ecologists have raised a further question. Honeybees compete for food with wild bees, many of which are already declining. If too many hives are placed in one area, there may not be enough nectar for both. For this reason, the Calder Beekeeping Society now advises members to plant flowers whenever they add a new hive, and it keeps a map showing where hives are located across the city."},{"label":"E","text":"Supporters argue that rooftop hives have value beyond honey. Schools use them to teach children about pollination, and several companies offer staff a chance to help with the harvest. For many city workers, it is their first close contact with the insects that help to produce much of what they eat."}],"groups":[{"type":"sent","instructions":"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Rooftop beekeeping in Calder began when a ____ put hives on its roof.","answer":"hotel","alt":["local hotel"],"explanation":"A: the trend began when a local hotel placed four hives on its flat roof."},{"q":"Honey made in the city may taste richer because bees can visit many kinds of ____.","answer":"flowers","explanation":"B: city parks, gardens and streets provide a wide variety of flowers, so urban honey can have a richer flavour."},{"q":"Hives have to be firmly attached because of the danger from ____.","answer":"strong wind","alt":["wind"],"explanation":"C: strong wind is a particular concern, so hives must be fixed firmly in place."},{"q":"The Calder Beekeeping Society uses a ____ to record the location of hives.","answer":"map","explanation":"D: it keeps a map showing where hives are located across the city."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-sent-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"sent","title":"Learning through board games","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"When teacher Ruth Okafor brought a box of board games into her mathematics classroom in the town of Pellbrook, some colleagues assumed she had given up on serious teaching. Five years later, a number of schools in the region have adopted her approach, and a local university is studying its effects."},{"label":"B","text":"Okafor's starting point was a problem that many teachers recognise: pupils who could perform calculations on a worksheet but froze when asked to use the same skills in an unfamiliar situation. Games, she noticed, forced players to make decisions quickly, to estimate probabilities and to keep track of changing scores. Crucially, a mistake in a game carried no lasting consequences, so even anxious pupils were prepared to take risks."},{"label":"C","text":"She does not simply hand out games and hope for the best. Each session begins with a short demonstration, after which pupils play in groups of three or four. In the final ten minutes, the class discusses the strategies that worked and links them to the topic being studied. Okafor describes this closing stage as the part where the real learning happens, because pupils must explain their thinking aloud."},{"label":"D","text":"Researchers at Pellbrook University have compared classes that use games once a week with classes taught in the usual way. Early findings suggest that the game-playing pupils are not better at routine calculation, but they are more willing to attempt difficult problems and more accurate when estimating answers. The researchers stress, however, that the teachers involved volunteered for the project, and their enthusiasm may explain part of the difference."},{"label":"E","text":"Cost has been less of a barrier than expected. Many games are donated by families, and older pupils have begun designing their own versions, a task which itself demands careful thinking about rules and fairness. The main obstacle, according to Okafor, is time: a curriculum crowded with tests leaves little room for anything that looks like play."}],"groups":[{"type":"sent","instructions":"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Some pupils were unable to apply their skills in an ____ situation.","answer":"unfamiliar","explanation":"B: pupils froze when asked to use the same skills in an unfamiliar situation."},{"q":"Nervous pupils took risks because errors in a game had no ____.","answer":"lasting consequences","alt":["consequences"],"explanation":"B: a mistake in a game carried no lasting consequences, so even anxious pupils were prepared to take risks."},{"q":"Okafor believes the most important learning takes place during the ____ of each session.","answer":"closing stage","explanation":"C: Okafor describes this closing stage as the part where the real learning happens. The demonstration comes at the start, so it is not the answer."},{"q":"The researchers say the results may partly be due to the ____ of the teachers who took part.","answer":"enthusiasm","explanation":"D: the teachers volunteered, and their enthusiasm may explain part of the difference."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-sent-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"sent","title":"Sleep and the teenage brain","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Parents have long complained that teenagers stay up late and are impossible to wake in the morning. For many years this was put down to laziness or the attractions of screens. Research over recent decades has offered a different explanation: during adolescence the body's internal clock shifts, so that the hormone which makes people feel sleepy is released later in the evening than it was in childhood."},{"label":"B","text":"The consequences of this shift are significant because school timetables have not changed to match it. A teenager who cannot fall asleep until after midnight but must rise at half past six is likely to be chronically short of sleep. According to the psychologist Anja Brekke of the Halvorsen Institute, the effects go well beyond tiredness. In her laboratory, adolescents who had been allowed only six hours of sleep a night for a week recalled fewer words from a list they had studied, and they rated neutral faces as more hostile than a well-rested group did."},{"label":"C","text":"Brekke's explanation centres on what happens during deep sleep. At this stage, she argues, the brain replays the day's experiences and transfers important information from short-term storage into long-term memory. Cutting sleep short reduces the time available for this process, and the lost learning is not fully recovered by sleeping longer at the weekend, a habit that many teenagers rely on."},{"label":"D","text":"A handful of schools in the district of Norrvik decided to test whether a later start would help. They moved the beginning of the school day from eight o'clock to nine and recorded attendance, grades and the number of pupils arriving late. Lateness fell sharply in the first term, although improvements in grades were smaller and took longer to appear."},{"label":"E","text":"Not all observers are persuaded. Some head teachers point out that a later start pushes after-school activities and part-time jobs further into the evening, and they fear pupils will simply go to bed later still. Brekke accepts that the change is not a cure on its own, but maintains that it removes an obstacle which no amount of willpower can overcome, because the timing of the body clock is not a matter of choice."}],"groups":[{"type":"sent","instructions":"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"In adolescence, a ____ that causes drowsiness starts to be produced later at night.","answer":"hormone","explanation":"A: the hormone which makes people feel sleepy is released later in the evening."},{"q":"Sleep-deprived teenagers in Brekke's study saw ____ as less friendly than they really were.","answer":"neutral faces","explanation":"B: they rated neutral faces as more hostile than a well-rested group did."},{"q":"According to Brekke, extra sleep at the ____ does not completely make up for lost learning.","answer":"weekend","explanation":"C: the lost learning is not fully recovered by sleeping longer at the weekend."},{"q":"The first clear benefit of the later start in Norrvik was a fall in ____.","answer":"lateness","explanation":"D: lateness fell sharply in the first term; grades improved less and more slowly, and no result is given for attendance."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-sent-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"sent","title":"Life without sunlight","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Until the late twentieth century, biologists generally assumed that almost every food chain on Earth ultimately depended on sunlight. Plants and algae captured the sun's energy through photosynthesis, and all other organisms either ate them or ate something that had. The discovery of dense communities of animals clustered around volcanic openings on the deep ocean floor, far beyond the reach of light, forced a substantial revision of that view."},{"label":"B","text":"These openings, known as hydrothermal vents, release water that has been heated by magma beneath the seabed and is rich in dissolved minerals, notably hydrogen sulphide, a compound poisonous to most animals. Certain microbes, however, are able to extract energy from chemical reactions involving this compound, a process termed chemosynthesis. In doing so they produce organic matter without any input from the sun, and it is these microbes, rather than plants, that form the base of the vent food web."},{"label":"C","text":"The relationship between the microbes and larger animals is often remarkably intimate. On the Calloway Ridge, a vent field first surveyed by the research vessel Meridian, the dominant animal is a tube worm that possesses neither mouth nor gut. Instead, it houses vast numbers of bacteria inside a specialised organ, supplying them through its blood with the chemicals they require and, in return, absorbing the nutrients they manufacture."},{"label":"D","text":"Life at the vents is precarious. A vent may remain active for only a few decades before the flow of hot water shifts or ceases, at which point the community that depends on it collapses. How animals colonise new vents, which may lie many kilometres apart, long puzzled researchers. The current hypothesis is that many species release larvae which drift on deep currents, and that only a tiny proportion happen to settle at a suitable site."},{"label":"E","text":"The implications extend beyond the ocean. If complex ecosystems can be sustained by chemical energy alone, then environments once dismissed as lifeless, such as the ice-covered seas thought to exist on certain moons of the outer planets, become plausible places to search for living organisms. Some scientists go further, proposing that life on Earth may have originated not in a sunlit pool, as was traditionally supposed, but in the dark, mineral-rich conditions surrounding such vents."}],"groups":[{"type":"sent","instructions":"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"q":"Around hydrothermal vents, the position normally occupied by plants at the bottom of the food web is filled by ____.","answer":"microbes","alt":["certain microbes"],"explanation":"B: it is these microbes, rather than plants, that form the base of the vent food web. Algae belong to sunlit food chains, so they are a distractor."},{"q":"The Calloway Ridge tube worm obtains its food from bacteria kept in a ____.","answer":"specialised organ","alt":["specialized organ","organ"],"explanation":"C: it houses bacteria inside a specialised organ and absorbs the nutrients they manufacture."},{"q":"A vent community dies out when the supply of ____ moves elsewhere or stops.","answer":"hot water","explanation":"D: a vent may stay active for only a few decades before the flow of hot water shifts or ceases, and the community collapses."},{"q":"Species are thought to reach distant vents in the form of ____ carried by deep-sea currents.","answer":"larvae","explanation":"D: many species release larvae which drift on deep currents."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-short-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"short","title":"The keepers of Gull Rock","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"The lighthouse on Gull Rock stands on a small island about six kilometres off the coast of the county of Wendle. It was built in 1841 after a series of shipwrecks on the rocks below, and for more than a century it was home to a team of three keepers who worked in shifts to keep the light burning through every night."},{"label":"B","text":"Life on the rock was hard. Supplies were brought by boat once a fortnight, but in bad weather the boat could not land, and keepers sometimes waited weeks for fresh food. They grew vegetables in a small walled garden and caught fish from the rocks. Their main duty was to clean the lamp and its large glass lens every morning, so that the light would shine as brightly as possible."},{"label":"C","text":"Fog presented a special challenge. When the light could not be seen, the keepers sounded a horn every thirty seconds to warn ships of the danger. During long periods of fog, this meant that nobody on the island could sleep properly."},{"label":"D","text":"Most keepers served for several years, and many later said they enjoyed the quiet life. To pass the time, they read, painted and made models of ships. One keeper, Thomas Brannigan, kept a diary for twenty-two years, and it is now the most important record of daily life on the island."},{"label":"E","text":"The lighthouse became automatic in 1962, and the last keepers left the island that year. Today the light is controlled by a computer on the mainland. The keepers' cottage has been turned into a small museum, which can be visited by boat during the summer months."}],"groups":[{"type":"short","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Answer with NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.","questions":[{"q":"How often did the supply boat come to the island?","answer":"once a fortnight","alt":["a fortnight"],"explanation":"B: supplies were brought by boat once a fortnight."},{"q":"Apart from the lamp, what did the keepers clean every morning?","answer":"glass lens","alt":["lens","large glass lens"],"explanation":"B: their main duty was to clean the lamp and its large glass lens every morning."},{"q":"How frequently was the horn sounded when there was fog?","answer":"every thirty seconds","alt":["every 30 seconds","thirty seconds","30 seconds"],"explanation":"C: the keepers sounded a horn every thirty seconds."},{"q":"Who wrote the most important record of everyday life on Gull Rock?","answer":"Thomas Brannigan","alt":["Brannigan"],"explanation":"D: Thomas Brannigan kept a diary for twenty-two years, now the most important record of daily life on the island."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-short-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"short","title":"Remembering words for longer","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Anyone who has crammed for an examination knows the experience of forgetting most of the material within days. More than a century ago, researchers began to describe this pattern mathematically, showing that memories fade quickly at first and then more slowly. They also discovered that each time information is reviewed, it is forgotten more slowly than before."},{"label":"B","text":"This insight underlies a technique called spaced repetition. Instead of studying a word ten times in one evening, a learner reviews it after one day, then after three days, then after a week, and so on, with the gaps growing longer each time. The ideal moment for review, according to the theory, is just before the learner would otherwise forget."},{"label":"C","text":"Doing this with paper flashcards requires careful record-keeping, which is why the method became widespread only with the arrival of software. Language-learning apps such as Lingomo track every answer a user gives. If a word is recalled correctly, the app delays the next review; if the user makes a mistake, the word returns the following day."},{"label":"D","text":"Researchers at the Orlen Language Centre compared two groups of adult beginners who were learning Portuguese vocabulary for eight weeks. Both groups spent the same total time studying, but one followed a fixed schedule while the other used spaced repetition. In a test held a month after the course ended, the second group remembered almost twice as many words."},{"label":"E","text":"Critics note, however, that knowing a word in isolation is not the same as being able to use it. Learners who rely entirely on flashcards may recognise thousands of words yet struggle to hold a conversation. Most teachers therefore recommend combining spaced repetition with reading and speaking practice, so that vocabulary is met in context."}],"groups":[{"type":"short","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Answer with NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.","questions":[{"q":"What did learners have to use before software made spaced repetition popular?","answer":"paper flashcards","alt":["flashcards"],"explanation":"C: doing this with paper flashcards requires careful record-keeping, so the method spread only with software."},{"q":"When does Lingomo show a word again if the user gets it wrong?","answer":"the following day","alt":["following day"],"explanation":"C: if the user makes a mistake, the word returns the following day."},{"q":"How long after the end of the course were the Orlen learners tested?","answer":"a month","alt":["one month","1 month"],"explanation":"D: in a test held a month after the course ended. Eight weeks was the length of the course."},{"q":"Besides reading, what type of practice do most teachers suggest adding?","answer":"speaking practice","alt":["speaking"],"explanation":"E: teachers recommend combining spaced repetition with reading and speaking practice."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-short-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"short","title":"The long flight of the amber moth","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Each autumn, millions of amber moths leave the northern forests of the Kestrel Plateau and fly south to the warmer valleys of the Lune Basin, a journey of roughly nine hundred kilometres. Unlike migrating birds, no individual moth completes the round trip. Those that fly south in autumn breed in the valleys over winter, and it is their offspring that make the return journey in spring."},{"label":"B","text":"How insects that have never made the journey find their way has long intrigued biologists. Experiments by the entomologist Priya Dalal suggest that the moths use the position of the moon, correcting for its movement across the sky through the night. On overcast nights, when the moon is hidden, the moths appear to rely instead on the magnetic field of the Earth, although their navigation is noticeably less accurate under those conditions."},{"label":"C","text":"The moths do not fly continuously. They travel mainly in the hours after sunset, resting during the day on the undersides of leaves, where they are less visible to birds. Before departure, they feed heavily on the nectar of late-flowering heather, storing the energy as fat; individuals that fail to put on enough weight rarely survive the crossing of the Varn mountains, the most demanding section of the route."},{"label":"D","text":"In recent years, observers have reported that the autumn departure is occurring later than it did in the past. Dalal links this change to warmer autumns on the plateau, which delay the cue that triggers migration: a fall in night-time temperatures. A later departure is risky, because the heather on which the moths depend may already have finished flowering."},{"label":"E","text":"Conservation groups have responded by planting late-flowering species along the route, and volunteers now count moths at light traps in fourteen villages. The data they collect are incomplete, but they offer the first long-term picture of a migration that, until recently, passed almost unnoticed."}],"groups":[{"type":"short","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Answer with NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.","questions":[{"q":"Which moths make the journey back north in spring?","answer":"their offspring","alt":["offspring"],"explanation":"A: no moth completes the round trip; those that fly south breed, and their offspring make the return journey."},{"q":"What do the moths navigate by when cloud hides the moon?","answer":"magnetic field","alt":["the magnetic field"],"explanation":"B: on overcast nights the moths rely instead on the magnetic field of the Earth."},{"q":"Where do the moths shelter during daylight hours?","answer":"undersides of leaves","explanation":"C: resting during the day on the undersides of leaves, where they are less visible to birds."},{"q":"Which part of the route is the most difficult for the moths?","answer":"the Varn mountains","alt":["Varn mountains","the Varn"],"explanation":"C: the crossing of the Varn mountains is the most demanding section of the route."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-short-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"short","title":"The fair at Velmar","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, the town of Velmar, situated where two navigable rivers met, hosted one of the largest trade fairs in its region. Merchants arrived from as far away as the eastern steppes and the northern coast, and for six weeks each summer a town of perhaps four thousand inhabitants swelled to several times that size."},{"label":"B","text":"The fair's prosperity rested less on geography than on the guarantees offered by the counts who ruled Velmar. Travelling merchants were promised safe passage across the count's lands, and any trader robbed within his territory could claim compensation from the treasury. Disputes arising at the fair were settled by a special court, which sat daily and was required to deliver judgement before the fair closed, so that no merchant was obliged to remain in the town after trading had ended."},{"label":"C","text":"The organisation of the fair followed a strict sequence. The first eight days were reserved for unloading and displaying goods, during which no sales were permitted. Cloth was then traded for ten days, followed by leather and furs, and finally spices and other luxury items. The last week was devoted to settling accounts: merchants rarely carried large sums of coin, which was both heavy and tempting to thieves, and instead recorded debts that were cancelled against one another at the close of the fair."},{"label":"D","text":"This system of credit gave rise to a class of professionals known as money-changers, who assessed the value of the many currencies in circulation and charged a fee for converting them. Over time, some began to accept deposits and to transfer sums between clients by entries in their ledgers, functions that historians have identified as an early form of banking."},{"label":"E","text":"Velmar's decline was gradual. As sea routes improved, goods increasingly bypassed the river towns altogether, and when a later count imposed heavy taxes to pay for his wars, many foreign merchants transferred their business to rival fairs. By the fifteenth century the event had shrunk to a local market, remembered chiefly for the financial practices it had helped to develop."}],"groups":[{"type":"short","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Answer with NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage.","questions":[{"q":"How often did the special court hold its sessions?","answer":"daily","explanation":"B: disputes were settled by a special court, which sat daily."},{"q":"Which goods were the first to be sold at the fair?","answer":"cloth","explanation":"C: no sales were permitted in the first eight days, which were for unloading and display; cloth was then traded for ten days."},{"q":"Apart from its weight, what made coin a problem for merchants to carry?","answer":"tempting to thieves","explanation":"C: coin was both heavy and tempting to thieves, so merchants recorded debts instead."},{"q":"What did a later ruler introduce that drove foreign traders to other fairs?","answer":"heavy taxes","alt":["taxes"],"explanation":"E: when a later count imposed heavy taxes to pay for his wars, many foreign merchants moved to rival fairs."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-summ-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"summ","title":"The night market of Sorrel Bay","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In the coastal town of Sorrel Bay, the busiest shopping hours begin after the sun goes down. Every Friday and Saturday, the car park beside the harbour is cleared at six o'clock, and by seven more than eighty stalls have been set up under strings of lights. The night market started fifteen years ago as a way to attract visitors during the quiet winter months, but it now runs throughout the year."},{"label":"B","text":"Food is the market's main attraction. Fishermen sell grilled seafood caught the same morning, and families from the town's many immigrant communities cook dishes from their home countries. For many of these cooks, the market was the first step towards opening a permanent restaurant, because a stall costs very little to rent and allows them to test recipes on real customers."},{"label":"C","text":"The market also supports craftspeople. Potters, jewellery makers and woodworkers find that customers are more relaxed in the evening and more willing to stop and talk. One basket weaver explains that she sells twice as much at the market as she does online, because shoppers like to meet the person who made the product."},{"label":"D","text":"Not everyone in Sorrel Bay welcomes the crowds. Residents living near the harbour have complained about noise and litter, and some shop owners on the main street argue that the market takes trade away from them. In response, the town council has introduced a rule that music must stop at eleven, and stallholders now pay a small fee that funds extra cleaning."},{"label":"E","text":"Despite these tensions, the market is widely seen as a success. Hotels report that weekend bookings have risen since it began, and the council is considering a second market in the town's northern district. Organisers insist, however, that it must remain small enough to keep its friendly atmosphere."}],"groups":[{"type":"summ","instructions":"Complete the summary using the list of words, A–G, below.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["The Sorrel Bay night market was first set up to bring [1] to the town in winter. For cooks, renting a stall is a [2] way to try out new dishes before opening a restaurant. Craftspeople do well because evening shoppers are more [3] and happy to chat. To answer complaints from local people, the council now controls the hours of [4] and charges stallholders a fee to pay for cleaning."]]},"options":["A visitors","B fishermen","C cheap","D risky","E relaxed","F music","G litter"],"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"A","explanation":"A: the market started as a way to attract visitors during the quiet winter months."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"C","explanation":"B: a stall costs very little to rent and allows them to test recipes."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"E","explanation":"C: customers are more relaxed in the evening and more willing to stop and talk."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"F","explanation":"D: the council has introduced a rule that music must stop at eleven. Litter is dealt with by the fee, not by controlling hours."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-summ-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"summ","title":"Borrowing a ladder","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Campaigners for sharing often point to a simple example: a household drill that spends almost its whole life in a drawer. Tool libraries are one response to this waste. They work like ordinary libraries, but instead of books, members borrow ladders, saws, sewing machines and garden equipment, usually for a week at a time."},{"label":"B","text":"The tool library in the city of Marrow Point opened in a former bus garage and is run almost entirely by volunteers. Members pay an annual subscription, which is reduced for students and pensioners, and they must attend a short safety session before borrowing any power tool. Items are checked and cleaned when they are returned, and anything damaged is sent to a repair workshop held every Thursday evening."},{"label":"C","text":"The founders had expected that saving money would be the main reason people joined. A survey of members, however, found that the most common motive was a lack of storage space, particularly among people living in small flats. Environmental concerns came second, and cost only third."},{"label":"D","text":"The library has also become a social centre. Experienced members run weekend classes in basic woodwork and bicycle maintenance, and borrowers often stay to ask for advice about their projects. Several members say that the library has helped them to get to know their neighbours for the first time."},{"label":"E","text":"Running such a scheme is not easy. Insurance is expensive, and some tools are never returned. The Marrow Point library now asks for a deposit on its most valuable items and sends reminders by text message when a loan is overdue. Its organisers believe the model could work in smaller towns, but only if a local council or business is willing to provide a building free of charge."}],"groups":[{"type":"summ","instructions":"Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"notes","rows":[["Marrow Point tool library"],["• housed in an old [1]"],["• members must complete a [2] before they can borrow power tools"],["• broken items are fixed at a weekly workshop"],["Why people join"],["• most often because they do not have enough [3]"],["Difficulties"],["• high cost of insurance"],["• a [4] is now required for the most valuable tools"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"bus garage","explanation":"B: the library opened in a former bus garage."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"safety session","explanation":"B: members must attend a short safety session before borrowing any power tool."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"storage space","alt":["space"],"explanation":"C: the most common motive was a lack of storage space. Saving money came only third."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"deposit","explanation":"E: the library now asks for a deposit on its most valuable items."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-summ-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"summ","title":"Keeping food through the highland winter","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For the farming communities of the Dalven Highlands, winter once meant five months during which almost nothing could be grown. Survival depended on preserving the summer's harvest, and over the centuries the villagers developed three main techniques, each suited to different foods and each with its own weaknesses."},{"label":"B","text":"The oldest method was air-drying. Strips of meat and slices of fruit were hung on wooden frames on the sheltered side of houses, where the cold mountain wind removed moisture over several weeks. The technique required no fuel or salt, which made it available to even the poorest families. Its great disadvantage was its dependence on the weather: a spell of damp, still air could cause an entire season's supply to grow mould."},{"label":"C","text":"Salting was more reliable but far more costly. Salt had to be carried up from the lowland coast by mule, and in some years its price rose so high that families exchanged a whole sheep for a single sack. Fish and pork were packed in layers of salt inside wooden barrels and could keep for over a year. Historians believe that the salt trade was the main reason the isolated highland villages maintained contact with the outside world."},{"label":"D","text":"The third technique, fermentation, was used chiefly for cabbages and root vegetables. These were sealed in clay jars and buried in pits beneath the floor of the house, where the steady temperature allowed beneficial bacteria to transform them slowly. Fermented vegetables were valued for keeping people healthy through the long winter, but the process was unpredictable, and a jar that had been poorly sealed produced food that was not only inedible but dangerous."},{"label":"E","text":"Today these methods survive mainly as traditions. Refrigeration arrived in the highlands in the middle of the last century, and few younger residents now preserve food at home. However, a cooperative in the village of Teska has begun selling wind-dried meat to restaurants in the capital, where it is marketed as a luxury product."}],"groups":[{"type":"summ","instructions":"Complete the table below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","outline":{"style":"table","rows":[["Method","Foods preserved","Main advantage","Main drawback"],["Air-drying","meat and fruit","affordable even for the [1] families","a whole season's food could be spoiled by [2]"],["Salting","fish and [3]","food kept for more than a year","salt was costly to bring from the coast"],["Fermentation","cabbages and [4]","helped people stay healthy","results could not be predicted"]]},"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"poorest","explanation":"B: the technique required no fuel or salt, which made it available to even the poorest families."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"mould","alt":["mold"],"explanation":"B: damp, still air could cause an entire season's supply to grow mould. Damp air is the cause; mould is what spoils the food."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"pork","explanation":"C: fish and pork were packed in layers of salt. Sheep were traded for salt, not preserved with it."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"root vegetables","explanation":"D: fermentation was used chiefly for cabbages and root vegetables."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"rc-summ-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"summ","title":"The quiet power of defaults","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Economists traditionally assumed that people make choices by weighing costs against benefits, and that the way a choice is presented should make little difference to the outcome. A growing body of research in behavioural science suggests otherwise. One of the most striking findings concerns defaults: the option that takes effect automatically if a person does nothing."},{"label":"B","text":"Consider workplace pension schemes. In a traditional arrangement, employees must actively sign up, completing forms and selecting how much of their salary to contribute. When the logistics firm Brannock reversed this, enrolling all new staff automatically while allowing anyone to leave the scheme at any time, participation rose dramatically. Crucially, nothing about the financial benefits had changed; only the starting position was different."},{"label":"C","text":"Several explanations have been offered. The first is inertia: leaving a scheme requires effort, however slight, and people tend to postpone tasks that bring no immediate reward. A second is that a default may be read as an implicit recommendation, a signal that the employer, who is presumed to know more, considers this the sensible course. A third concerns loss: once enrolled, employees come to regard the contributions as already theirs, and withdrawing feels like giving something up."},{"label":"D","text":"Defaults are not without drawbacks. At Brannock, most automatically enrolled employees remained at the contribution rate the company had chosen, which was deliberately set low to avoid discouraging participation. Some who would previously have chosen to save more therefore ended up saving less. Critics also raise an ethical concern: if organisations can steer behaviour so easily, who decides which direction people should be steered in, and in whose interest?"},{"label":"E","text":"Advocates respond that there is no neutral alternative. Every form must start somewhere, and even the absence of a default influences behaviour, since it favours those with the time and confidence to make an active decision. The question, they argue, is not whether choices should be designed, but how to design them transparently and in a way that leaves people genuinely free to choose differently."}],"groups":[{"type":"summ","instructions":"Complete the summary using the list of words, A–G, below.","outline":{"style":"summary","rows":[["When Brannock made membership of its pension scheme [1] for new employees, the number taking part rose sharply, even though the scheme offered no additional [2]. One explanation is that staff may treat the automatic option as advice from a better-[3] source. However, because the company had fixed the standard contribution at a [4] level, some employees put aside less money than they otherwise would have."]]},"options":["A automatic","B compulsory","C benefits","D effort","E informed","F low","G generous"],"questions":[{"q":"Gap 1","answer":"A","explanation":"B: Brannock enrolled all new staff automatically. It was not compulsory, because anyone could leave the scheme at any time."},{"q":"Gap 2","answer":"C","explanation":"B: nothing about the financial benefits had changed; only the starting position was different."},{"q":"Gap 3","answer":"E","explanation":"C: the employer is presumed to know more, so the default is read as a recommendation from a better-informed source."},{"q":"Gap 4","answer":"F","explanation":"D: the contribution rate was deliberately set low, so some who would have saved more ended up saving less."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-tfng-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"tfng","title":"How honeybees give directions","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"When a honeybee finds a good patch of flowers, it flies back to the hive and tells the other workers where to go. It does this with a movement known as the waggle dance. The dancer runs forward in a straight line while shaking its body from side to side, then circles back to the start and repeats the run, sometimes dozens of times."},{"label":"B","text":"The direction of the straight run tells other bees which way to fly. Inside the dark hive, the dance is performed on a vertical sheet of honeycomb, and the bee uses 'straight up' to stand for the position of the sun. If the run points directly upwards, the flowers lie towards the sun. If it points slightly to the right of upwards, the food lies at the same angle to the right of the sun."},{"label":"C","text":"Distance is shown by time. The longer each waggle run lasts, the further away the food is. At the Brennick Field Station, the insect scientist Clara Voss trained bees to visit feeders placed at different distances and filmed their dances. She found that a run of about one second usually meant a feeder several hundred metres away, although bees from different colonies did not always 'agree' exactly on the scale."},{"label":"D","text":"The dance is not the whole story. Bees that watch it also pick up the smell of the flowers from the dancer's body, which helps them recognise the right plants when they arrive. Voss also noticed that young workers often had to follow several dances before they flew out and found the food, which suggests that reading the dance is a skill that improves with practice."},{"label":"E","text":"Beekeepers have long been fascinated by the dance, and some now place small cameras inside their hives so that computer software can 'read' the dances automatically. This shows them which areas around the hive provide the most food at different times of the year."}],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the reading passage? Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"q":"A dancing bee repeats the straight part of its dance more than once.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph A: the dancer 'repeats the run, sometimes dozens of times'."},{"q":"Bees dance faster when the flowers they have found contain a lot of nectar.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"The passage describes direction and distance but never links the speed of the dance to the amount of nectar."},{"q":"A longer waggle run shows that the food is closer to the hive.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph C: 'The longer each waggle run lasts, the further away the food is.'"},{"q":"Voss found that bees from all colonies showed distance in exactly the same way.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph C: bees from different colonies 'did not always agree exactly on the scale'."},{"q":"Inexperienced bees may need to watch more than one dance before they find food.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph D: young workers 'often had to follow several dances before they flew out and found the food'."},{"q":"Using cameras in hives has helped beekeepers to produce more honey.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"Paragraph E: cameras show which areas provide the most food; nothing is said about honey production increasing."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-tfng-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"tfng","title":"The salt road of Varrow","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"For nearly four centuries, the mountain town of Varrow owed its wealth to a single substance: salt. The springs beneath the town produced brine so concentrated that, when it was boiled in shallow iron pans, it left behind a grey-white crust that could be cut into blocks. Records kept by the town council show that by the late fifteenth century more than sixty boiling houses were operating along the river."},{"label":"B","text":"Salt was valuable because it preserved food. Before refrigeration, meat and fish could only be kept through the winter if they were salted, and communities far from the sea depended on inland sources such as Varrow. Merchants carried the blocks by mule along a narrow route through the Kessel Pass to the lowland markets, a journey of about nine days. Tolls collected at the bridges along this road paid for the construction of Varrow's town hall, which still stands today."},{"label":"C","text":"The trade was tightly controlled. Only families holding a licence from the council were allowed to boil brine, and the number of licences was fixed. Historians have often described this arrangement as an early form of monopoly. Recent work by the archivist Tomas Rehn, however, suggests that licences were in fact bought and sold quite freely, so that new families regularly entered the business."},{"label":"D","text":"The industry's decline was gradual rather than sudden. Boiling brine required enormous quantities of wood, and by the seventeenth century the forests around Varrow had been cut so heavily that fuel had to be brought from further away, which pushed up costs. At the same time, cheaper sea salt began to arrive by canal. The last boiling house closed in 1803."},{"label":"E","text":"Today the old salt road is a popular walking trail, and a small museum in one of the former boiling houses demonstrates the traditional method to visitors."}],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the reading passage? Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"q":"Salt in Varrow was produced by heating water from underground sources.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph A: brine from 'the springs beneath the town' was 'boiled in shallow iron pans'."},{"q":"Merchants could reach the lowland markets from Varrow in under a week.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph B: the journey took 'about nine days'."},{"q":"Income from the salt trade funded a building that has survived to the present day.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph B: tolls 'paid for the construction of Varrow's town hall, which still stands today'."},{"q":"Rehn's research supports the traditional view that the salt trade was a closed monopoly.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph C: Rehn suggests licences were 'bought and sold quite freely' and new families 'regularly entered the business', which challenges the monopoly view."},{"q":"A shortage of local wood made salt production more expensive.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph D: forests were cut so heavily that fuel came from further away, 'which pushed up costs'."},{"q":"The canal that brought sea salt to the region was built by Varrow's council.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"D only says sea salt 'began to arrive by canal'; who built the canal is not mentioned."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-tfng-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"tfng","title":"Seeding the reef","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Coral reefs damaged by rising sea temperatures can recover naturally, but only if enough coral larvae drift in from healthy reefs nearby. Where whole regions have been affected, that supply may be too small. For two decades the most common response has been 'coral gardening': fragments are broken from surviving colonies, grown on underwater frames and later fixed back onto the reef by divers. The technique works, but it is slow and labour-intensive, and because every fragment is a clone of its parent, a reef rebuilt in this way may lack genetic variety."},{"label":"B","text":"An alternative, known as larval seeding, takes advantage of the annual mass spawning, when many corals release eggs and sperm into the water on the same few nights. Teams led by the marine biologist Anika Dorsey at the Pellam Bay laboratory collect the spawn in floating nets, fertilise it in large tanks and rear the resulting larvae for about a week. The larvae are then released over damaged areas beneath fine mesh tents, which hold them close to the seabed until they settle."},{"label":"C","text":"Because each larva comes from a different combination of parents, the new corals are genetically diverse, which may make the population better able to cope with future change. The method can also treat far larger areas than gardening. Survival, however, is low: in Dorsey's early trials only a small proportion of settled larvae were still alive after a year, chiefly because young corals are eaten by fish or smothered by seaweed."},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsey's group has since tested small ceramic 'seeding units', on which larvae settle while still in the tanks before the units are wedged into the reef. Early results suggest this improves survival, although the units add to the cost. Critics, including the ecologist Rafael Moen, warn that restoration of any kind treats the symptoms rather than the cause. Unless ocean warming is reduced, he argues, newly seeded reefs could be lost in the next marine heatwave."}],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the reading passage? Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"q":"Reefs damaged by warm water cannot recover without human intervention.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph A: damaged reefs 'can recover naturally' if enough larvae drift in from healthy reefs."},{"q":"Corals grown by gardening are genetically identical to the colony they were taken from.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph A: 'every fragment is a clone of its parent'."},{"q":"Larval seeding was first developed at the Pellam Bay laboratory.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"B says Dorsey's teams at Pellam Bay use the method, but not where or by whom it was first developed."},{"q":"Larval seeding can be used over a wider area than coral gardening.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph C: 'The method can also treat far larger areas than gardening.'"},{"q":"Most of the larvae in Dorsey's early trials survived for at least a year.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph C: 'only a small proportion of settled larvae were still alive after a year'."},{"q":"Moen believes that larval seeding is less effective than coral gardening.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"Paragraph D: Moen criticises 'restoration of any kind'; he does not compare the two methods."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-tfng-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"tfng","title":"Sleep and the making of memories","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"That sleep benefits memory has been suspected since at least the early twentieth century, when experiments showed that people who slept after learning lists of nonsense syllables forgot fewer of them than those who remained awake. For decades, however, the effect was attributed to a passive process: sleep, it was assumed, merely shielded fresh memories from the interference of new experiences. Only relatively recently has a more active account gained ground, according to which the sleeping brain replays and reorganises what it encountered during the day."},{"label":"B","text":"The most persuasive evidence comes from recordings of neurons in the hippocampus, a structure essential for forming memories of events. When rats explore a maze, particular cells fire in a sequence corresponding to the route taken; during subsequent deep sleep, the same sequences recur, compressed into a fraction of the original time. A team at the Halvorsen Institute reported that disrupting these replay events impaired the animals' later navigation, whereas disrupting an equal number of randomly selected brain signals did not."},{"label":"C","text":"Human studies are necessarily less direct. In one experiment directed by the neuroscientist Ines Marchetti, participants learned the locations of objects on a screen while a distinctive sound accompanied each object. During their subsequent deep sleep, the sounds belonging to half of the objects were replayed quietly. On waking, participants recalled the cued locations more accurately than the uncued ones, although none reported having heard anything during the night."},{"label":"D","text":"Such findings have prompted speculation that memory might be deliberately enhanced during sleep, for instance to assist students or patients recovering from strokes. Marchetti herself is cautious. The improvements observed so far, she notes, are modest, and it remains unclear whether strengthening some memories comes at the expense of others. Moreover, most studies have examined simple, recently acquired material; whether the same mechanisms govern complex knowledge built up over months is an open question."},{"label":"E","text":"Nor is deep sleep the whole story. The stage associated with vivid dreaming appears to contribute more to emotional memories and to skills such as playing an instrument, although the evidence for this is considerably more contested."}],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the reading passage? Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"q":"Early twentieth-century researchers believed that sleep actively reorganised memories.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph A: for decades the effect 'was attributed to a passive process'; the active account gained ground only recently."},{"q":"In rats, the replay of a route during sleep lasts less time than the original journey.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph B: the sequences recur 'compressed into a fraction of the original time'."},{"q":"Interfering with random brain signals affected the rats' navigation as much as interfering with replay.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph B: disrupting replay impaired navigation, 'whereas disrupting an equal number of randomly selected brain signals did not'."},{"q":"Marchetti's study was the first to use sounds to influence memory during sleep.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"C describes 'one experiment' by Marchetti but says nothing about whether it was the first of its kind."},{"q":"Marchetti has tested sleep-based memory techniques on patients recovering from strokes.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"Paragraph D: stroke patients are mentioned only as a possible future use in others' speculation; no testing by Marchetti is reported."},{"q":"Marchetti considers it possible that improving certain memories could weaken others.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"TRUE","explanation":"Paragraph D: 'it remains unclear whether strengthening some memories comes at the expense of others'."},{"q":"Scientists agree that dream sleep plays the main role in learning practical skills.","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"FALSE","explanation":"Paragraph E: the evidence for the role of dreaming sleep 'is considerably more contested', so there is no agreement."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-ynng-1","kind":"reading","qtype":"ynng","title":"Is a four-day school week a good idea?","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In several districts, schools have recently switched to a four-day week, with longer lessons from Monday to Thursday and no classes on Friday. Supporters say the change saves money on buses and heating, and that teachers, who are often hard to recruit, find the job more attractive. I understand why districts with tight budgets find the idea appealing, but I believe it is a mistake."},{"label":"B","text":"My main concern is younger children. A six- or seven-year-old cannot concentrate well for an extra hour at the end of a long day, so much of the additional time is wasted. Older students may cope better, and I accept that a longer day could suit secondary schools, where students often study one subject in depth."},{"label":"C","text":"The free Friday also creates problems for families. Parents who work full time must find childcare for an extra day every week, and this is hardest for those on low incomes. Some districts offer optional Friday clubs, but these usually charge a fee, which seems to me unfair."},{"label":"D","text":"The savings, too, are smaller than they first appear. Buildings still need to be maintained, and many costs simply move from schools to families. It is true that teachers in four-day districts say they are happier, and this matters. But there are better ways to keep good teachers, such as higher salaries and smaller classes."},{"label":"E","text":"Before more districts make the switch, we need careful, long-term studies of how the change affects learning. Until then, I would keep the five-day week."}],"groups":[{"type":"ynng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in the reading passage? Choose YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","questions":[{"q":"The writer can see why some districts are attracted to the four-day week.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph A: 'I understand why districts with tight budgets find the idea appealing'."},{"q":"Young children benefit from having longer school days.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph B: young children 'cannot concentrate well for an extra hour', so the time is wasted."},{"q":"A longer school day may be suitable for some older students.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph B: 'I accept that a longer day could suit secondary schools'."},{"q":"Friday clubs in four-day districts are usually badly organised.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"Paragraph C: the writer objects to the fees, but says nothing about how well the clubs are organised."},{"q":"Whether teachers are happy is not an important consideration.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph D: teachers say they are happier, 'and this matters'."},{"q":"More research is needed before other districts adopt a four-day week.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph E: 'we need careful, long-term studies' before more districts switch."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-ynng-2","kind":"reading","qtype":"ynng","title":"Taking back the streets","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"When the city of Marlow Haven closed its central district to private cars five years ago, local shopkeepers predicted disaster. Without drivers, they argued, customers would simply go elsewhere. Their fears were understandable, yet they rested on a mistaken assumption: that people who arrive by car spend more than those who walk or cycle. Surveys carried out by the council since then suggest the reverse. Pedestrians may spend less on each visit, but they come far more often, and over a month they spend more in total."},{"label":"B","text":"The benefits extend beyond the till. Streets once dominated by traffic have been filled with benches, trees and market stalls, and the air is noticeably cleaner. Parents report feeling safer letting their children walk to school. It would be naive, however, to claim that everyone has gained. Elderly and disabled residents who relied on being dropped at the door have found the centre harder to reach, and the council's shuttle bus, though welcome, runs too infrequently to meet their needs."},{"label":"C","text":"Some critics go further and argue that car-free zones merely push traffic into the surrounding neighbourhoods. This is a genuine risk, but it is not inevitable. Where cities have also improved public transport and built safe cycle routes, the total number of car journeys tends to fall rather than simply move elsewhere. The lesson is that closing streets is not enough on its own; it must form part of a wider transport plan."},{"label":"D","text":"I am convinced that the car-free centre is, on balance, one of the best decisions Marlow Haven has made. Other cities, however, should not copy it blindly. A small, compact historic centre is far easier to close than a sprawling business district, and planners must be honest about who will bear the costs."}],"groups":[{"type":"ynng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in the reading passage? Choose YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","questions":[{"q":"The shopkeepers' worries were based on an incorrect belief about how customers spend money.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph A: their fears 'rested on a mistaken assumption' that drivers spend more."},{"q":"The shuttle bus for older and disabled residents should be free of charge.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"Paragraph B: the writer criticises how infrequently the bus runs, not its price."},{"q":"Closing a city centre to cars will always increase traffic in nearby areas.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph C: this is 'a genuine risk, but it is not inevitable'."},{"q":"Car-free zones work best when they are combined with other transport improvements.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph C: closing streets 'must form part of a wider transport plan' with better public transport and cycle routes."},{"q":"Overall, the scheme in Marlow Haven has been a success.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph D: 'I am convinced that the car-free centre is, on balance, one of the best decisions'."},{"q":"Other cities should introduce exactly the same scheme as Marlow Haven.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph D: 'Other cities, however, should not copy it blindly.'"}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-ynng-3","kind":"reading","qtype":"ynng","title":"Can a copy settle the question?","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Few debates in the museum world are as heated as that over the return of objects acquired during periods of conquest or colonial rule. Many institutions now accept that some items in their collections were taken unjustly, yet they continue to resist returning them, often on the grounds that the objects are safer, or seen by more people, where they are. Recently a new argument has entered the discussion: advances in 3D scanning and printing mean that a museum could return the original and keep a near-perfect copy, or the other way round."},{"label":"B","text":"At first sight this looks like an elegant compromise, and I confess I was initially drawn to it. A modern replica can reproduce the surface texture and colour of a carved figure so accurately that most visitors could not tell it from the original. Scans also allow researchers anywhere in the world to examine an object in detail without handling it, which is a genuine benefit for conservation."},{"label":"C","text":"Yet the more closely one examines the proposal, the less satisfactory it appears. The suggestion that a museum might keep the original and send the copy home misunderstands what is being requested. Communities seeking the return of a sacred mask or a royal throne are not asking for an object that looks the same; they are asking for the thing itself, with its history and meaning intact. To offer them a copy is, in effect, to repeat the original act of taking."},{"label":"D","text":"The opposite arrangement, in which the original goes back and the museum displays a replica, is more defensible, and some institutions have adopted it with apparent success. Even here, though, museums should be wary of presenting the copy as an equivalent. Visitors deserve to be told plainly what they are looking at and why the original is no longer there; that explanation may prove more educational than the object ever was."},{"label":"E","text":"Technology can support a fair settlement, but it cannot substitute for one. The decision to return an object is ultimately a moral question, not a technical one."}],"groups":[{"type":"ynng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in the reading passage? Choose YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","questions":[{"q":"Few museums now accept that any of the objects in their collections were obtained unfairly.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph A: 'Many institutions now accept that some items in their collections were taken unjustly', which contradicts 'few'."},{"q":"The writer was attracted to the idea of using replicas when it was first proposed.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph B: 'I confess I was initially drawn to it.'"},{"q":"Digital scanning can help to protect fragile objects.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph B: scans let researchers examine objects 'without handling it, which is a genuine benefit for conservation'."},{"q":"Producing high-quality replicas is too expensive for most museums.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"The writer discusses the accuracy of replicas but never their cost."},{"q":"Returning a replica to the community of origin is a reasonable compromise.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph C: to offer a copy 'is, in effect, to repeat the original act of taking'."},{"q":"Telling visitors why an original object has gone could have educational value.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph D: 'that explanation may prove more educational than the object ever was'."}]}],"type":"Exercise"},{"id":"ra-ynng-4","kind":"reading","qtype":"ynng","title":"Hiring by algorithm","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Companies that receive thousands of applications for a handful of posts have turned increasingly to software that screens candidates automatically, ranking CVs, analysing recorded video interviews or scoring online games designed to measure traits such as persistence. The vendors of these systems make two claims: that they are more efficient than human recruiters, and that they are fairer, because a machine does not share the prejudices of a tired manager. The first claim is largely uncontroversial. The second deserves considerably more scrutiny than it usually receives."},{"label":"B","text":"An algorithm trained on the records of past successful employees will, unless carefully corrected, learn to favour candidates who resemble them. If a firm has historically hired from a narrow range of universities or backgrounds, the software may reproduce that pattern while lending it a misleading appearance of objectivity. Developers are well aware of this problem, and the more responsible among them test their models for disparities between groups. It would be unjust to suggest that the industry has ignored the issue; the difficulty is that such testing is voluntary, its methods vary widely, and its results are seldom made public."},{"label":"C","text":"Defenders of automated hiring sometimes reply that human judgement is no better, and on this point the evidence is on their side: interviewers are notoriously swayed by first impressions, accent and appearance. But the comparison is less reassuring than it seems. A biased manager affects only the candidates he or she happens to interview; a biased algorithm, deployed across hundreds of employers, can exclude the same kind of applicant thousands of times, and do so invisibly."},{"label":"D","text":"None of this implies that the technology should be abandoned. Used for routine tasks, such as checking whether applicants hold a required licence, it can free recruiters to spend more time with candidates. What is needed is not prohibition but accountability: independent audits, a right for rejected applicants to know that software was involved, and a requirement that final decisions remain with a person."}],"groups":[{"type":"ynng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in the reading passage? Choose YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","questions":[{"q":"It is widely accepted that automated screening saves employers time.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph A: the claim that the systems are 'more efficient than human recruiters' is 'largely uncontroversial'."},{"q":"Analysing video interviews is the most unreliable form of automated screening.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NOT GIVEN","explanation":"A lists video analysis as one technique, but the writer never ranks the techniques by reliability."},{"q":"Software developers have generally failed to recognise that their systems can copy past hiring patterns.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph B: 'Developers are well aware of this problem' and it would be 'unjust to suggest that the industry has ignored the issue'."},{"q":"Human interviewers are less prone to bias than automated systems.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph C: the writer agrees with defenders that 'human judgement is no better'; interviewers are 'notoriously swayed' by impressions."},{"q":"A biased algorithm can affect far more people than a biased individual.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph C: a manager affects only those he or she interviews, while an algorithm can exclude the same kind of applicant 'thousands of times'."},{"q":"Automated tools can be useful for straightforward checks on applicants.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"YES","explanation":"Paragraph D: for routine tasks such as checking a licence, the technology 'can free recruiters to spend more time with candidates'."},{"q":"Automated hiring tools should be banned until better safeguards exist.","options":["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],"answer":"NO","explanation":"Paragraph D: 'None of this implies that the technology should be abandoned'; what is needed is 'not prohibition but accountability'."}]}],"type":"Exercise"}];
window.IELTS_T1X = [{"kind":"line","q":"The graph below shows the average daily energy consumption per household from three sources in Country A between 1995 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"line","unit":"kWh per household per day","cols":["Source","1995","2000","2005","2010","2015","2020"],"rows":[["Gas","32","34","31","27","22","18"],["Electricity","14","16","19","21","22","24"],["Solar","0","0.5","1.5","4","8","13"]]}]},{"kind":"line","q":"The graph below shows the percentage of adults who smoked in four countries, W, X, Y and Z, from 2000 to 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"line","unit":"% of adults","cols":["Country","2000","2005","2010","2015","2020"],"rows":[["Country W","34","30","26","21","17"],["Country X","22","23","24","22","20"],["Country Y","41","38","32","25","19"],["Country Z","15","14","12","11","9"]]}]},{"kind":"line","q":"The graph below shows sales of printed books, e-books and audiobooks in Country B between 2010 and 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"line","unit":"copies sold (millions)","cols":["Format","2010","2013","2016","2019","2022","2025"],"rows":[["Printed books","48","44","41","40","38","37"],["E-books","6","15","21","20","18","17"],["Audiobooks","1","2","4","8","14","21"]]}]},{"kind":"bar","q":"The chart below shows the number of overseas visitors to five coastal towns in Country C in 2015 and 2023. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"bar","unit":"visitors (thousands)","cols":["Town","2015","2023"],"rows":[["Brackenhaven","120","185"],["Saltmere","95","80"],["Port Ellis","60","140"],["Dunmore Bay","210","230"],["Kestwick","40","38"]]}]},{"kind":"bar","q":"The chart below shows what male and female school leavers in Country D did after finishing school in 2022. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"bar","unit":"% of school leavers","cols":["Destination","Male","Female"],"rows":[["University","38","47"],["College","22","20"],["Apprenticeship","18","9"],["Employment","15","16"],["Not in work or education","7","8"]]}]},{"kind":"bar","q":"The chart below shows the average weekly amount spent per person on five types of food by three age groups in Country E in 2022. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"bar","unit":"dollars per week","cols":["Food type","Aged 18-34","Aged 35-54","Aged 55+"],"rows":[["Fresh fruit and vegetables","9","13","16"],["Meat and fish","14","19","17"],["Ready meals","12","8","4"],["Snacks and sweets","10","7","5"],["Eating out","25","21","12"]]}]},{"kind":"pie","q":"The pie charts below show how water was used in Country F in 1990 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"pie","unit":"% of water used","cols":["Use","1990","2020"],"rows":[["Agriculture","68","55"],["Industry","20","24"],["Households","9","16"],["Other","3","5"]]}]},{"kind":"pie","q":"The pie charts below show the composition of household waste in the city of Norbridge in 2005 and 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"pie","unit":"% of household waste","cols":["Material","2005","2025"],"rows":[["Food","34","28"],["Paper and card","26","18"],["Plastic","14","24"],["Glass","9","6"],["Metal","5","4"],["Other","12","20"]]}]},{"kind":"pie","q":"The pie charts below show the main ways workers travelled to work in the town of Hartwell in 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"pie","unit":"% of workers","cols":["Mode","2000","2020"],"rows":[["Car","62","48"],["Bus","15","12"],["Train","8","14"],["Bicycle","5","16"],["Walking","10","10"]]}]},{"kind":"process","q":"The diagram below shows the life cycle of the Atlantic salmon. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features.","charts":[{"kind":"process","steps":["Adult salmon lay eggs in gravel on a river bed","Eggs hatch into small fish called alevins after about three months","Alevins live on their yolk sacs and stay hidden in the gravel","Young fish, called fry, swim up and begin to feed","Fry grow into parr and live in the river for one to three years","Parr change into smolts and swim downstream to the sea","Salmon feed and grow in the ocean for one to four years","Adult salmon return to the river where they were born to breed"]}]},{"kind":"process","q":"The diagram below shows how olive oil is produced. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features.","charts":[{"kind":"process","steps":["Olives are picked from the trees in autumn","Leaves and twigs are removed and the olives are washed","Olives are crushed into a paste, including the stones","The paste is mixed slowly for 30 to 40 minutes","The paste is spun in a machine to separate the oil from water and solids","The oil is left to settle and is filtered","The oil is stored in steel tanks away from light","The oil is bottled, labelled and sent to shops"]}]},{"kind":"process","q":"The diagram below shows how waste paper is recycled to make new paper. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features.","charts":[{"kind":"process","steps":["Waste paper is collected from homes and offices","Paper is sorted by type and grade at a recycling centre","Paper is mixed with water in a pulper to make pulp","The pulp is cleaned to remove plastic, staples and glue","Ink is removed using air bubbles and soap","The pulp is pressed flat between rollers to remove water","The sheets are dried on heated rollers","Large rolls of new paper are cut and sent to manufacturers"]}]},{"kind":"map","q":"The maps below show the coastal village of Port Selby in 1990 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"map","maps":[{"title":"1990","grid":[["Sea","Sea","Sea"],["Fishing harbour","Beach","Sand dunes"],["Fish market","Village houses","Farmland"]]},{"title":"2020","grid":[["Sea","Pier","Sea"],["Marina","Beach","Hotel"],["Restaurants","Village houses","Holiday apartments"]]}]}]},{"kind":"map","q":"The maps below show the riverside area of the town of Westbridge in 1980 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"map","maps":[{"title":"1980","grid":[["Warehouses","River","Railway yard"],["Factory","River","Factory"],["Workers' houses","Bridge","Wasteland"]]},{"title":"2020","grid":[["Apartments","River","Railway station"],["Arts centre","River","Office park"],["Workers' houses","Bridge","Riverside park"]]}]}]},{"kind":"map","q":"The maps below show the campus of Greystone University in 2005 and 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"map","maps":[{"title":"2005","grid":[["Car park","Sports field","Woodland"],["Library","Lecture halls","Student halls"],["Main entrance","Cafeteria","Car park"]]},{"title":"2025","grid":[["Science building","Sports centre","Woodland"],["Learning centre","Lecture halls","Student halls"],["Main entrance","Food court","Bus stop and bike racks"]]}]}]},{"kind":"mixed","q":"The graph below shows the average house price in the town of Millbrook compared with the national average from 2000 to 2020, and the pie chart shows how homes in Millbrook were occupied in 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"line","unit":"average price (thousand dollars)","cols":["Area","2000","2005","2010","2015","2020"],"rows":[["Millbrook","120","165","150","210","280"],["National average","140","170","165","190","230"]]},{"kind":"pie","unit":"% of homes","cols":["Type","2020"],"rows":[["Owned outright","28"],["Owned with a mortgage","34"],["Private rent","26"],["Social housing","12"]]}]},{"kind":"mixed","q":"The chart below shows membership of sports clubs by activity in Country G in 2010 and 2020, and the table shows the average hours of exercise per week by age group in the same years. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"bar","unit":"members (thousands)","cols":["Activity","2010","2020"],"rows":[["Football","85","80"],["Swimming","40","52"],["Tennis","22","18"],["Cycling","15","41"],["Gym","60","110"]]},{"kind":"table","unit":"hours per week","cols":["Age group","2010","2020"],"rows":[["16-24","5.2","4.8"],["25-44","3.1","3.9"],["45-64","2.0","2.8"],["65+","1.2","2.1"]]}]},{"kind":"mixed","q":"The graph below shows the number of small and large farms in Country H between 1980 and 2020, and the pie chart shows how farmland was used in 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","charts":[{"kind":"line","unit":"farms (thousands)","cols":["Farm size","1980","1990","2000","2010","2020"],"rows":[["Small farms","210","180","150","115","90"],["Large farms","12","15","19","24","27"]]},{"kind":"pie","unit":"% of farmland","cols":["Use","2020"],"rows":[["Crops","46"],["Grazing","38"],["Woodland","9"],["Other","7"]]}]}];
